There has been a rapid increase in the cumulative incidence of novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19) in the EU/EEA in the United Kingdom between January 1 and March 15, 2020.
In various EU/EEA countries as well as the United Kingdom, the cumulative incidence of novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19) shows a similar trend, confirming that although different countries are at different stages, the COVID-19 pandemic is developing rapidly.
According to the experience of Italy, the country, hospitals and intensive care units need to be better prepared to welcome the number of COVID-19 patients, and the intensive care task is particularly important.
On December 31, 2019, a number of cases of unexplained pneumonia were reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, China’s Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported that the pathogen is a new type of coronavirus, now known as Severe Acute Respiratory Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
Evidence to date shows that about 80% of COVID-19 cases are mild, i.e. respiratory infections with or without pneumonia, and that the majority can recover.
In about 14% of cases, COVID-19 progresses into severe illness and requires hospitalization, while the remaining 6% progresses into critical condition and requires intensive care.
Mortality among hospitalized patients due to COVID-19 was about 4%.
In this study, we evaluated trends in COVID-19 cumulative incidence in various countries of the European Union (EU/European Economic Area (EEA), as well as those in the United Kingdom (UK), and compared it to Hubei Province, China.
We have also compared the number of existing COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom between January 31 and March 15, 2020, to Italy.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
The geographical distribution of COVID-19 has further expanded after China, and the current dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic in other parts of the world are similar to those in China.
On March 11, 2020, the Director-General of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In the March 5, 2020 issue of the journal Monitoring Europe, Spiteri and others reported the first confirmed COVID-19 cases in Europe according to WHO case definitions.
In the EU/EEA, France reported the first three confirmed cases on January 24, 2020, and the patients were returnees from the city of Wuhan in Hubei Province, China.
COVID-19 cases had been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries, as well as the United Kingdom, and from 31 December to 15 March 2019, 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths had been reported, including 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths in Italy alone.
Cumulative number of COVID-19 cases and cumulative incidence of COVID-19
The European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC) updates the number of COVID-19 cases reported in every country around the world at 8:00 a.
These data have been used to assess COVID-19 trends in the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom for comparison with Italy.
We Calculated the 14-day Cumulative Incidence of COVID-19 cases in each EU/EEA country, as well as in the United Kingdom, between January 1 and March 15, 2020, as an indicator of the prevalence of active COVID-19 in order to take into account the natural course of COVID-19.
We have also provided the total number of cumulative cases that have been reported per country as at 8:00 a.m. on March 15, 2020, with data from Italy for the period from January 31 to March 15, 2020.
COVID-19 trends for EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
The trend of 14-day truncated cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom is generally in line with that in Hubei (China) (Figure 1).
The cumulative incidence of COVID-19 for the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom as a whole has increased since around February 21, 2020, followed by a sharp increase (supplementary material).
This is mainly due to the rapid increase in the number of cases reported in Italy, but the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in all other EU/EEA countries, as well as the United Kingdom, is showing a similar increasing trend (supplementary material).
Figure 2 shows the cumulative COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom from 31 January to March 15, 2020, compared to Italy.
It should be pointed out that as of 8:00 a.m. on March 15, the other 15 EU/EEA countries, as well as the United Kingdom, had already reported a total number of cases comparable to that of Italy in less than three weeks.
“Our results show that the number of COVID-19 cases notified by the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom is increasing rapidly.
The observed trends in COVID-19’s cumulative incidence suggest that outbreaks are developing at a similar rate in all countries.
Although each country is at different stages, the national public health response is different, the definition of cases in each country may be different, and there are differences in the options used to select patients who must undergo COVID-19 diagnostic testing, including additional tests, but the situation is the same.
In early March 2020, about 10 per cent of COVID-19 patients required intensive care, according to doctors in the affected regions of Italy, while media reports indicated that hospitals and intensive care units in those areas had reached maximum capacity.
At the EU/EEA level, only 6% and 1% of COVID-19 cases are currently available (the data is not shown).
However, this information should be collected in a systematic manner in order to supplement current surveillance data that focuses on the number of reported cases and deaths.
According to a study conducted in 2010-11, the number of intensive care and medium-term care beds available in European countries varies widely, ranging from 29.2 beds per 100,000 people in Germany to 4.2 in Portugal.
This means that countries are likely to have more or less resources than in Italy (with 12.5 intensive care and medium-term care beds per 100,000 people in 2010-2011).
ECDC’s sixth update of the COVID-19 rapid risk assessment modeled scenarios associated with saturation of health care capabilities, and estimated the relationship between the prevalence of hospitalized COVID-19 patients in each EU/EEA country, as well as in the United Kingdom, with a risk of more than 90 per cent of intensive care beds.
Because cases have so far gathered in EU/EEA countries as well as in certain areas of the UK, and hospitals and intensive care wards typically serve the population covered in identified areas, it is advisable to provide information on cases and intensive care beds at the “Standard Statistical Geographic Unit 2” (NUTS-2) level.
Italy’s experience, as well as current trends in other countries, suggest that the EU/EEA and UK’s COVID-19 pandemic is developing rapidly.
Therefore, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should be prepared to deal with the continued community transmission of SARS-CoV-2 and the increasing number of COVID-19 sufferers in need of health care, especially intensive care, such as those in affected areas in Italy.
As the recent ECDC rapid risk assessment points out, a rapid, proactive, and comprehensive approach is critical to delaying the spread of SARS-COV-2 and requires a transition from containment to a remission approach, as the rapid increase in the number of expected cases may not give decision-makers and hospitals sufficient time to understand, accept, and adjust their response if not implemented in advance.
The rapid risk assessment also lists public health measures to mitigate the effects of the pandemic.
There is now a short window of opportunity for countries to further increase their control efforts to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce pressure on health-care systems.
Otherwise, the healthcare systems of other EU/EEA countries are likely to face a surge in patients requiring intensive care in the next few days or weeks.
So far, the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19), caused by Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2), has so far infected more than 80,000 people in China and other parts of the world, resulting in more than 3,000 deaths and a huge human catastrophe.
Similar to SARS-CoV, which infected tens of thousands of people in SARS in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 can also be transmitted by bats and can cause similar symptoms through similar mechanisms.
Although COVID-19 has a lower severity and death rate than SARS, it is more transmissible, affecting more older people than younger people and more men than women.
In view of the rapid increase in publications related to the new crown pneumonia, this article wants to provide a timely and comprehensive review of this rapidly developing research topic.
We will introduce the basic knowledge about the epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and prevention of the disease.
While a number of questions remain to be answered, we hope that this review will contribute to the understanding and eradication of this horrendous disease.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020, has become an unprecedented and unforgettable memory for the Chinese people, and the outbreak of a new viral disease has forced everyone to leave the house throughout the holidays and the weeks that followed.
The virus is highly homogenous with the coronavirus (CoV), which caused a severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) outbreak in 2003; therefore, the World Health Organization (WHO) named it SARS-CoV-2 on February 11, 2020, and the associated disease is known as coronavirus disease-19 (COVID-19).
The epidemic began in Wuhan, China, and quickly spread throughout the country, and then spread to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of March 2, 2020, the virus has resulted in more than 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, of which more than 40,000 patients have been discharged and more than 3,000 have died.
COVID-19 has become the “number one public enemy” and its threat is likely to exceed that of terrorism.
More than 200 papers on COVID-19 in less than a two-month period have been published since PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), more than 200 COVID-19 papers covering virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis and treatment were first reported on January 7, 2020.
The purpose of this article is to summarize the progress of research in this emerging disciplinary area.
COVID-19 is used to comparing COVID-19 with SARS and another disease caused by CoV, the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS outbreak).
We will also discuss what we have learned so far about the prevention and prognosis of the disease, as well as a number of pressing issues that have yet to be addressed.
In the past, it has been believed that CoV is a non-lethal pathogen of humans, mainly causing about 15% of the common cold.
However, this century has encountered two highly pathogenic human CoVs, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which broke out in China in 2003 and in Saudi Arabia in 2012, and soon spread to many other countries with terrible morbidity and mortality rates.
The COVID-19 outbreak is the third CoV outbreak recorded in human history.
As shown in Figure 1-1, the city of Wuhan first reported a number of unexplained cases of pneumonia to the Chinese National Health Commission on December 31, 2019.
The CoV sequence was released seven days later.
On January 15, 2020, Wuhan reported its first death.
At the same time, the epidemic quickly spread to neighboring cities, provinces and countries.
On January 20, it was reported that medical staff were infected, suggesting that there may be human-to-human phenomenon.
On January 23, the city of Wuhan closed the city and closed all public transportation.
On January 24, the first clinical study of the new crown pneumonia reported that of the 41 confirmed cases, only 21 had had direct contact with the Wuhan South China Seafood Market, which is believed to be the starting point of infection from unknown animal sources.
On January 30, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared the outbreak a “global health emergency.”
At the time of publication of this report, the disease had spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Figure 2 (Fig.2)).
Due to the rapid development of the epidemic, the final scope and severity of this outbreak have yet to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multicenter study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 diagnosed COVID-19 patients, provided an update of the epidemic, as shown below (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 is capable of infecting people of all ages, but the majority age group is 30-65 years.
Almost half (47.7 per cent) of those infected were over the age of 50, a very small number were under the age of 20, and only 14 were under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 infection in more men than in women (0.27 per 100,000).
COVID-19 is mainly agglomeration and propagation in Hubei and surrounding areas.
COVID-19 takes an average of 5 (2-9) days from onset to diagnosis.
The average incubation period is 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days.
The average time from onset to death was 9.5 (4.8-13) days.
The basic regeneration number (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51-4.05), and the adjusted R0 was 2.23-4.82.
The number of infected people increased exponentially by January 23, 2020, coinciding with large-scale population movements before the Chinese New Year.
Mortality in confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%), and the adjusted mortality rate for all patients was 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 are gender (male), age (≥60 years), and severe pneumonia.
CoV is a large envelope virus subfamily that contains a single righteous strand of RNA.
They can be grouped into four genies, namely α, β, γ, and δ, where α-CoV and β-CoV are known to infect humans.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV enveloped spike glycoglycoprotein (S) binds to its cellular receptor angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) and dipeptide peptidease 4 (DPP4) and then undergo membrane fusion.
Viral RNA genomes are released into the cytoplasm; after the viral genome replicates, genomic RNA, together with enveloped glycoproteins and nuclear capsid proteins to form vesicles containing vesicles, then fuses with the plasma membrane and releases the virus.
The first genomic sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was reported on January 10, 2020.
It turned out that SARS-CoV-2 is a new type of β-CoV, with more than 99.98% genetic consistency from the 10 sequencing samples collected from the Wuhan South China Seafood Market, the site of the initial outbreak.
Compared with MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 is more genetically similar to SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 granules have been found in ultra-thin slices of airway epithelium through transmission electron microscopy.
Studies have found that human ACE2 is a receptor for SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV.
However, compared with SARS-CoV, the S-protein of SARS-CoV-2 has a weaker binding force to human ACE2, which is consistent with the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes less severe infections in patients than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form novel short proteins encoded by orf3b, as well as secreted proteins encoded by orf8.
Orf3b of SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in viral pathogenicity and inhibit the expression of IFNβ; however, orf8 does not contain any known functional domains or basals.
On Feb. 18, 2020, Zhou et al. reported a cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM) structure with an amino acid transporter B0AT1 that forms a complex in a full-lived human ACE2 with a resolution of 2.9 Å.
They found that the complex had an open and closed conformation that was assembled into dimers and that the ACE2-B0AT1 complex could bind two S proteins to provide evidence of CoV identification and infection.
B0AT1 could be a therapeutic target for drug screening to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Origins and intermediate hosts
Both SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originated in bats and were transmitted to humans through civets and camels, respectively.
By comparing SARS-CoV-2 with the phylogenetic development of other CoVs, bats are thought to be a natural host of SARS-CoV-2 because this new virus is 96% consistent with two SARS-like coVs from bats, called bat-SL-CoVZX45 and bat-SL-CoVZX21 respectively.
However, it’s not clear what intermediate hosts have helped the virus infect humans across the species barrier, and its route of transmission has yet to be elucidated.
Ji and others suggested that snakes are virus carriers from bats to humans, which involve homologous recombination within the S protein.
According to one study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, believe that since a coV found in pangolins, a long-kissed anteater that is often used in traditional Chinese medicine, is 99 percent gene homology with SARS-CoV-2, it is thought that pangolins are a potential intermediate host for SARS-CoV-2.
However, the 1% difference between the two genomes is still a huge difference; therefore, concrete evidence has yet to provide a decisive result (Fig.
To a large extent, the physioficial nature of SARS-CoV-2 remains largely unclear.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive for up to 48 hours in a dry environment and up to 5 days at 20 °C and 40%-50% humidity.
SARS-CoV-2 may be of a similar nature.
It is reported that SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to ultraviolet rays and heat - 56 °C for 30 minutes; ether, 75% ethanol, chlorinated disinfectants, peracetic acid, chloroform and other lipid solvents can effectively inactivate the virus, but chloro chloride can not effectively inactivate the virus.
Humans generally lack immunity to SARS-CoV-2 and are therefore susceptible to this new virus.
There have been no detailed studies of immune responses to SARS-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies on other CoVs, in particular SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Fig.4).
In general, after intrusion into the host, the virus is first recognized by the host’s inherent immune system through pattern-recognition receptors (PRRs), including type C lectin-like receptors, Toll-like receptors (TLR), NOD-like receptors (NLRs), and RIG-I-like receptors (RLRs).
Induced inflammatory factor expression, dendritic cell maturation through different pathways, as well as type I interferon (IFN) synthesis, they limit the spread of the virus and accelerate the engulfment of macrophages of viral antigens.
However, SARS-CoV’s N protein can help get the virus out of its immune response.
Adaptive immune responses will join the fight against the virus in the near future.
T lymphocytes, including CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, play an important role in antiviral defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B-cells to produce virus-specific antibodies, while CD8+ T cells directly kill virus-infected cells.
Assistive T cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to help the above-mentioned defensive cells.
However, CoV can inhibit T-cell function by inducing apoptosis in T cells.
Solimatic immunity, which includes complements such as C3a and C5a, and antibodies, is also essential for combating viral infections.
For example, antibodies isolated from rehabilitated patients were able to neutralize MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, overreaction by the immune system produces a large number of free radicals locally, which can cause serious damage to the lungs and other organs and, in the worst case, lead to multiple organ failure or even death.
SARS-CoV-2 infection characterized by agglomeration is more likely to affect older adults with multiple comorbidities, as well as pregnant women.
Typically, individuals who are exposed to a large amount of the virus or impaired immune function have a higher chance of developing an infection than others.
According to a study of the earliest 425 cases in Wuhan, the average incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 is estimated to be 1-14 days, mostly 3-7 days.
However, a study of 1,099 cases showed an average incubation period of 3 days, ranging from 0 to 24 days.
As mentioned earlier, a most recent study showed an incubation period of 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days, based on the demographic of 8,866 cases.
It’s very important for the health department to adjust the effective isolation time according to the most accurate incubation period, which can prevent asymptomatic infected people from spreading the virus to others.
By convention, a viral contact or infected person usually needs to be quarantined for 14 days.
Should the isolation period be extended up to 24 days?
Fever is usually the main and first symptom of COVID-19, and can be accompanied by symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, myalgia, dizziness, headache, sore throat, runny throat, chest pain, diarrhoea, nausea, and vomiting.
Some patients experience breathing difficulties and/or hypoxemia one week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, it progresses rapidly into acute respiratory distress syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidosis, and clotting dysfunction.
Even in the absence of pulmonary imaging abnormalities, patients with febrile and/or respiratory symptoms and acute febrile fever should be screened for early diagnosis.
According to a statistical study conducted in late December 2019, the percentages of various symptoms were: fever, 76% dry cough, 55% difficulty breathing, and 3% diarrhoea; 8% of patients who needed ventilation support.
Similar results have been reported in two recent studies on the onset of agglomeration in families and asymptomatic individual propagation.
In comparison, a demographic study conducted in 2012 showed that patients with MERS-CoV also had fever (98%), dry cough (47%) and difficulty breathing (55%) as the main symptoms.
However, 80 per cent of these patients required ventilated support, far more than in patients with COVID-19, with MERS having a higher fatality rate than COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also observed in MERS patients.
In patients with SARS, fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), breathing difficulties (40% to 42%), diarrhoea (20-25%) and sore throat (13-25%) have been shown to be the main symptoms, and about 14% to 20% of patients require ventilation support.
As of February 14, the number of confirmed COVID-19 cases worldwide has reached 66,576, with a mortality rate of 2%.
In comparison, as of November 2002, SARS had a mortality rate of 10 per cent of 8,096 of the 8,096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, according to a June 2012 demographic study, the mortality rate of 2,494 confirmed cases was 37%.
An earlier study reported that SARS-CoV-2 has an R0 of 6.47, a 95% confidence interval (CI) of 5.71-7.23, while SARS-CoV has an R0 of only 2 to 4.
SARS-CoV-2 and MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV are shown in Table 1.1 for symptom, Mortality, and R0.
The above data show that the diffusion capacity of SARS-CoV-2 is higher than that of MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but the lethality is lower.
Therefore, controlling SARS-CoV-2 outbreaks is more challenging than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Aggregation usually occurs within the same family, or from the same parties or means of transportation, such as cruise ships.
Patients usually have a history of travel or residence in Wuhan or other affected areas within the last two weeks before the onset of the disease, or have a history of close contact with an infected person or patient.
However, it is reported that people can carry the virus with no symptoms for more than two weeks, and cured patients who have been discharged from the hospital can carry the virus again, thus issuing an alert for an extended isolation time.
There is a normal or decrease in the number of peripheral white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) at the beginning of the disease.
For example, leukoplocyte denigration of leukocyte counts < 4 × 109/L, including the lymphocyte count < 1 × 109/L, was found in 1,099 COVID-19 patients, elevated aspartate aminotransferase levels, and viremia.
In some patients, elevated levels of hepatic enzymes and myoproteins in the blood can be seen, as well as increased levels of hematopoietic protein and hemorrhoids in most patients.
Heavy-heavy patients showed elevated blood fibroblast decomponin decomponent levels as well as an aggressive reduction in lymphocyte counts.
Most COVID-19 patients can see chest anomalies, characterized by bilateral flacal shadows or pulmonary abrasive glass shadows.
Patients often experience atypical pneumonia, acute pulmonary injury, and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).
When ARDS occurs, uncontrolled inflammatory, fluid build-up, and advanced fibrosis can seriously impair gas exchange.
Alveolar epithelial cell lesusions of type I and type II can reduce surfactant levels and increase surface tension, thereby reducing the ability of the lungs to expand and increase the risk of lung collapse.
As a result, the worst thoracic imaging findings usually occur at the same time as the most severe of the disease.
On February 18, 2020, COVID-19’s first pathological analysis found alveolar epithelial cell shedding, transparent membrane formation, interstitial lymphocyte infiltration, and polynucleustic cells in the lungs of a patient who died from the disease, consistent with viral infections and the pathological manifestations of ARDS, and similar to patients with SARS and MERS.
The detection of SARS-CoV-2 RNA through retrospective polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) has been used as the primary criterion for diagnosing COVID-19.
However, due to the higher false negative rate may accelerate the outbreak, China began to use clinical manifestations for diagnosis on February 13, 2020, instead of relying solely on RT-PCR.
A similar situation occurred when SARS was Diagnosed.
Therefore, the combination of medical history, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests and imaging results is essential for an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, Zhang Feng’s team reported a solution to detect SARS-CoV-2 using the CRISPR-based SHERLOCK technology, which requires no sophisticated instruments to detect synthetic SARS-CoV-2 RNA snippets in less than an hour in less than an hour with concentrations ranging from 20 × 10-18 mol/L to 200 × 10-18 mol/L (10-100 copies of the virus per microliter).
If validated in a clinical sample, this new technique has the potential to greatly improve the sensitivity and convenience of diagnosis.
Due to the lack of experience in the treatment of novel CoV, doctors mainly provide support treatment for patients with COVID-19, while trying various therapies that have been used or proposed for other CoVs, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, as well as other viral diseases (table 2).
These include current and potential treatments, such as antivirals, immunosuppressants, steroids, blood plasma from rehabilitated patients, traditional Chinese medicine, and psychological support.
It is even recommended that the plasma of recovered patients be used for treatment.
Pharmaceutical companies are racing to develop antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 primarily attacks the lungs at the beginning and may also attack other organs expressing ACE2 to a lesser degree, such as the gastrointestinal system and the kidneys.
Despite this, respiratory dysfunction and failure are the primary threats and leading causes of death for patients.
Respiratory support is therefore essential to relieve symptoms and save lives, including conventional, high-traffic inhalation, non-invasive ventilation, and invasive mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms must be supported by extra vitro membrane oxygenation (ECMO), a modified cardiopulmonary bypass technique for the treatment of life-threatening heart or respiratory failure.
In addition, maintaining electrolyte balance, preventing and treating secondary infections and septic shock, as well as protecting critical organ function, are also essential for patients with SARS-CoV-2.
It is well known that overreacting the immune systems of SARS and MERS patients can lead to cytokine storms.
Cytokine storms are a form of systemic inflammatory response characterized by the release of a range of cytokines including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ, and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to release a large number of free radicals, which is the main cause of ARDS and multiple organ failure.
Immunosuppression is essential in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially in severely ill patients.
Corticosteroids (corticosteroids) and tocilizumab – an anti-IL6 monoclonal antibody – have already been used to treat cytokine storms.
Other immunosuppressive therapies for cytokine storms include: Regulating T-cell directed immune responses; blocking IFN-gamma, IL-1, and TNF; inhibition of JAKs; Bonacumab; cytokine signaling inhibitors4; and HDAC inhibitors.
Steroids as immunosuppressants are widely used to treat SARS in order to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, large doses of steroids did not benefit from severe lung damage in patients with SARS and COVID-19.
On the contrary, they can cause serious side effects, especially ischemic bone necrosis, which can seriously affect the prognosis.
Despite this, it is still recommended to treat patients with critically ill COVID-19 with a low and medium dose of corticosteroids for short-range treatment.
At the time of writing, effective antiviral therapies have not yet been confirmed.
However, intravenous administration of redicivir, a nucleotide analog, has been found to be effective for a COVID-19 patient in the United States.
Ridsivir is a novel antiviral drug developed by Gilead that was originally used to treat diseases caused by the Ebola and Marburg viruses.
It was also later shown that radcivir may inhibit other single-stranded RNA viruses, including the MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on these reasons, Gilead has provided the drug to China for two clinical trials involving people with SARS-CoV-2, the results of which are expected.
In addition, balactinib, alpha-interferon, lopinavir/ritonavir, and ribavirin have been recommended as potential therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage, and other adverse reactions may occur after lopinavir/ritonavir combined treatment.
The interaction between these treatments and other drugs used by the patient should be carefully monitored.
Plasma and antibody production in recovered patients
The collection of blood from patients recovering from infectious diseases has a long history of treating other patients with the same disease or protecting healthy individuals from contracting the disease.
In fact, rehabilitating patients often have relatively high levels of antibodies in their blood that target pathogens.
Antibodies are an immunoglobulin (Ig) produced by B-lymphocytes to fight pathogens and other foreign bodies, and they can identify unique molecules in pathogens and directly neutralize them.
Based on this principle, plasma was collected in the blood of a group of COVID-19 patients who had recovered and then injected into 10 critically ill patients.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, along with reduced inflammation and viral load and increased blood oxygen saturation.
However, prior to the development of specific therapies, it is necessary to verify and clarify to propose methods that can be used on a large scale.
In addition, considering the effectiveness of the treatment, you should carefully consider some of the shortcomings associated with plasma.
Antibodies, for example, can overstimulate the immune response and cause cytokine-releasing syndrome, a toxic effect that can be life-threatening.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is generally low, and the demand for plasma for the treatment of critically ill patients is high.
It’s hard to develop and produce specific antibodies fast enough to fight a global epidemic.
Therefore, a more critical and practical approach is to isolate B-cells from rehabilitated patients and identify the genetic code that encodes effective antibodies, or to screen effective antibodies against proteins necessary for the virus.
In this way, we can easily increase the scale of antibody production.
In China, TCM has been used to treat a variety of diseases for thousands of years.
However, its therapeutic effect depends largely on the combination of multiple components in the prescription, which varies according to the TCM-theoretic disease diagnosis.
The majority of active ingredients remain unknown or unclear, as it is difficult to extract and verify such ingredients or their optimal combination.
Due to the lack of effective and specific therapies for COVID-19, TCM has become one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or those who have recovered from severe stages.
For example, it has already been found to be effective for COVID-19 treatment.
In several provinces in China, 87% of patients were treated with TCM, of which the highest cure rate for COVID-19 patients was observed, including Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%) and Hunan (50%), while only about 30% of COVID-19 patients in Hubei Province used TCM treatment with the lowest cure rate (13%).
However, this is just a cursory comparison, because the assessment should also include a number of other influencing factors, such as the number and severity of patients.
On February 18, 2020, Zhang and his colleagues published a study comparing Western (WM) treatment alone with WM and TCM treatment.
They found that body temperature recovery in the WM+TCM group was significantly reduced in the time it took for the WM+TCM group to get rid of its symptoms and hospitalization.
Impressively, the symptom exacerbation rate in the WM+TCM group (from light to heavy) was significantly lower than in the WM group alone (7.4% versus 46.2%), and the mortality rate in the WM+TCM group was also lower than in the WM group alone (8.8% versus 39%).
Despite this, TCM’s effectiveness and safety still needs to be confirmed by higher-quality controlled trials, a wider range, and more centers.
Determining the mechanism by which TCM treatments or their combinations work and, where possible, elucidates their active components will also be of concern.
Suspected or confirmed COVID-19 patients are most likely to have great fear because of this highly contagious or even fatal disease, and those who are isolated will also feel depressed, lonely, and angry.
In addition, the symptoms of the infection, such as fever, lack of oxygen and cough, as well as adverse effects of treatment, such as corticosteroid-induced insomnia, may increase anxiety and psychological distress.
A range of psychological reactions have been reported in the early stages of the SARS outbreak, including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor arousal, psychotic symptoms, delirium, and even suicide.
As part of a public health response to the COVID-19 outbreak, mandatory close contact tracing and isolation can make people more anxious and guilty about the effects of infection, isolation, and stigma on their family and friends.
Mental health care should therefore be provided to COVID-19 patients, suspected cases, close contacts, and members of the public in need.
Psychological support should include the establishment of a multidisciplinary mental health team, clear communication through regular and accurate updating of SARS-CoV-2 outbreak information and treatment plans, and the avoidance of close contact using professional electronic devices and applications.
“Effective vaccines are critical to disrupting the chain of transmission from animal hosts and infected patients to vulnerable hosts, and are often used as a complement to antiviral treatment in controlling outbreaks caused by emerging viruses.”
The industry has made efforts to develop S-protein-based vaccines to produce long-term and effective neutralizing antibodies and/or protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
Live attenuated vaccines have already been evaluated in animal models of SARS.
Prior to initiating clinical studies, however, it had not yet been possible to determine the in vivo efficacy of these vaccine candidates in older and lethal attack models, as well as their protective effect against zoonotic viral infections.
This is most likely because SARS disappeared as early as 17 years ago, and no new cases have been reported since.
In contrast, due to the persistence of zoonotic sources in the affected areas, sporadic cases of MERS and agglomerations in the Middle East continue to spread to other regions.
Vaccination strategies for MERS have been developed using inactivated viruses, DNA plasmids, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-like particles, and recommunicative protein subunits, some of which have already been evaluated in animal models.
Developing safe and effective vaccines against SARS-CoV-2 for non-immune individuals is an urgent and critical task to control current outbreaks.
However, it is challenging to overcome this difficulty due to the long time it takes to develop vaccines (an average of 18 months on average) as well as the dynamics of CoV.
COVID-19 is just beginning to demonstrate its complete clinical process in thousands of patients.
In most cases, the patient can gradually recover without sequelae.
However, similar to SARS and MERS, patients with severe COVID-19 also have high morbidity and mortality rates.
Building prognostic models of the disease is therefore critical for health-care providers to prioritize services, especially in areas with limited resources.
According to clinical studies reported to date, the following factors may affect the prognosis of COVID-19 patients or be associated with prognosis (Table 33):
Age: Age is the most important factor affecting the prognosis of SARS, as does COVID-19.
In a study that included 8,866 cases, COVID-19 occurred primarily in the 30- to 65-year-old age group, with 47.7 percent of patients over the age of 50.
Patients in need of intensive care are more likely to have underlying comorbidities and complications, and are significantly older than those who don’t need intensive care (medium ages 66 versus 51), suggesting a prognosis factor in patient outcomes for COVID-19.
Gender: As mentioned earlier, there are more men than women infected with SARS-CoV-2 (0.31 per 100,000 versus 0.27 per 100,000).
Combinations and Complications: COVID-19 patients requiring intensive care are more likely to develop acute heart damage and arrhythmias.
Heart events are also the leading cause of death among SARS patients.
SARS-CoV-2 is also reported to bind to ACE2-positive biliary cells, which can lead to hepatic abnormalities in COVID-19 patients.
It is important to note that age is closely related to underlying diseases and may interact with each other.
Abnormal laboratory test results: C-response (CRP) levels in the blood reflect the severity of inflammatory or tissue damage and have been seen as potential prognostic factors for disease, treatment response, and eventual recovery.
It has also been suggested that CRP levels are associated with the severity and prognosis of COVID-19.
In addition, elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT), and creatinated kinase (CK) may also help predict outcomes.
These enzymes are widely expressed in a number of organs, especially in the heart and the liver, and released during tissue damage.
Therefore, they are traditional markers of heart or liver dysfunction.
Key clinical symptoms: Chest imaging and the timing of clinical symptoms should be considered in conjunction with other questions to predict outcomes and complications of COVID-19.
steroid use: As mentioned earlier, steroids are immunosuppressants commonly used as adjuvant treatments for infectious diseases in order to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
Since large-dose corticosteroids are widely used in heavy-duty SARS patients, many survivors suffer from ischemic bone necrosis, resulting in lifelong disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, steroids should be used in small doses and for a short period of time in COVID-19 patients if needed.
Psychological stress: As mentioned earlier, during the COVID-19 outburst, many patients are under extreme stress because they often suffer from prolonged isolation and extreme uncertainty, and have witnessed the deaths of relatives and patients.
Psychological counseling and long-term support must be provided to help these patients get out of stress and return to a normal life.
The epidemiological characteristics of COVID-19 appear to differ from those of SARS, according to demographic studies to date.
In addition to replicating within the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can also replicate effectively within the upper respiratory tract and cause mild or asymptomatic symptoms in the early stages of infection, which is similar to other CoVs that cause the common cold.
Therefore, infected people will produce a large number of viruses in the course of their daily activities in the early stages or incubation periods, which brings great difficulties to the control of the epidemic.
However, the transmission of SARS-CoV is thought to occur when the patient is in serious condition, and most transmission does not occur at an early stage.
The current COVID-19 outburst is therefore more severe and difficult to control compared to SARS.
China is currently making great efforts, including a blockade of Wuhan and neighboring cities, as well as the ongoing isolation of almost all its population, with a view to halting the spread of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these measures have caused serious damage to China's economy and other aspects, the number of new cases is dwindling, indicating that the development of the epidemic is slowing down.
The most optimistic estimate is that the outbreak will end in March, and the decline phase will last 3-4 months.
However, some other experts are not so optimistic.
Paul Hunter and colleagues estimate that COVID-19 is significantly more contagious than SARS and won’t end in 2020.
Ira Longini and others have built models to predict the consequences of the epidemic, arguing that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world’s population.
A team in Canada reported that SARS-CoV-2 was detected in patients who recovered and were discharged two weeks ago in both nasal and pharynopharyngeal swabs, suggesting that the newly-discovered virus may have become a cyclical epidemic similar to influenza.
However, as the number of new cases has dwindled, there have been encouraging signs that the current strategy may already be working.
The Ebola virus has been predicted to cause 1 million cases and cause 500,000 deaths.
However, through strict isolation, the disease was eventually brought under control.
Similar to SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 may become less infectious, eventually disappearing or becoming a less pathogenic virus to co-exist with the human body.
A comparison of COVID-19 outbreaks with SARS and MERS is provided below (Fig.55).
SARS-CoV-2 is highly contagious and can be transmitted by coughing or sneezing, and may even be transmitted through direct contact with items contaminated by the virus.
The virus has also been found in feces, which increases the possibility of fecal oral transmission.
According to a recent study of 138 cases, 41% may have been caused by in-hospital infections, including 17 patients previously suffering from other diseases and 40 health care workers.
Therefore, more stringent precautions should be taken to protect the population, in particular medical personnel, social workers, family members, colleagues, and even bystanders who are in contact with patients or infected persons.
The first line of defense that can be used to reduce the risk of infection is the wearing of masks; the use of surgical masks and N95 dust masks (1860s series) can help to control the spread of the virus.
Surgical masks can prevent droplets from a potentially infected person from spreading in the air or sticking to the surface of items that may be transmitted to others.
However, only N95 (1860s series) masks can prevent inhalation of virons as small as 10 to 80 nm, and only 5% of viruses can penetrate completely; SARS-CoV-2 is similar in size to SARS-CoV, which is about 85 nm.
Since the virus particles can even penetrate five surgical masks stacked together, healthcare workers who come into direct contact with patients must wear N95 (1860s series) masks, rather than surgical masks.
In addition to masks, medical staff should also wear isolation suits to further reduce contact with the virus.
The virus can also be transmitted through the eyes.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor was infected with SARS-CoV-2 while wearing an N95 mask; the virus may have entered its body through the inflamed eyes.
Therefore, medical staff should also wear transparent masks or goggles in the face of patients.
For the members of the public in areas affected or potentially affected by the epidemic, it is strongly recommended that everyone wash their hands more frequently than usual with sterilized soap, try to stay indoors to self-quarantine, and limit contact with potentially infected people.
Three feet is believed to be an appropriate distance for people to stay away from their patients.
These measures are an effective way to reduce the risk of infection and prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 broke into the human world as a new type of virus, based on deep memories of the 2003 SARS outbreak, its high homology with SARS-CoV reported on January 7, 2020 should have aroused high alarm in China.
However, until January 19, 2020, the director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Prevention and Control was still appeasing the public, saying that this new virus is not contagious, the possibility of human-to-human transmission is limited, and the epidemic can be prevented and controlled.
This news has obviously relaxed the public's vigilance, and the Spring Festival is approaching, and the key node of containing the epidemic in Wuhan has been missed.
China’s disease control agencies are likely to learn this painful lesson and make significant improvements in the future.
For example, these institutions should (1) be more cautious in issuing announcements, because each word is critical to citizens and can change their attitudes and decision-making; (2) be more sensitive and responsive to abnormal information from hospitals, rather than waiting for formal reports from doctors or officials; (3) more stringent control of potential outbreaks at an early stage, rather than trying to appease the public; and (4) conducting targeted and effective exercises more frequently to raise public awareness of epidemics and to regularly test and improve social response systems.
The COVID-19 outbreak, caused by the novel virus SARS-CoV-2, began at the end of December 2019.
In less than two months, it has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world.
Because the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV, and the symptoms between COVID-19 and SARS are similar, the COVID-19 outbreak looks like a recurrence of SARS.
However, there are a number of significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS, which are critical to controlling outbreaks and treating patients.
COVID-19 affects more older people than younger men, more men than women, and older patients have a higher severity and mortality rate than younger patients.
Mortality from SARS was higher than COVID-19 (10.91% versus 1.44%).
Patients with COVID-19 transmit the virus even without symptoms, while patients with SARS usually spread the virus when they are in serious condition, making it much more difficult to control COVID-19 transmission.
This partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 spreads much more quickly and extensively than SARS-CoV.
In some COVID-19 patients, conventional RNA tests for SARS-CoV-2 may be negative.
On the other hand, patients who have been curated may turn yang again.
These findings significantly increase the risk of viral transmission.
In view of the fact that COVID-19 has been studied so rapidly, the following key issues remain to be addressed:
Where does SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although 96% homology was found between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-like CoV, we still cannot conclude that SARS-CoV-2 came from bats.
What kind of animal transmits a virus from its original host—assuming a bat—to an intermediate host to a human?
If we don’t know the answers to questions 1 and 2, we won’t be able to effectively cut off the route of propagation, and the outbursts can recur at any time.
Although molecular models and biochemical analysis have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to ACE2, how exactly does the virus enter airway cells and cause subsequent pathological changes?
Does the virus also bind to cells expressing ACE2 in other organs?
Without a clear answer to these questions, we will not be able to achieve rapid, accurate diagnosis and effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic last?
How does the virus genetically evolve during human-to-human transmission?
Is it going to become a global pandemic, disappearing like SARS, or is it going to recur on a regular basis like the flu?
It’s important to find the answers to these and many other questions, but it can take a long time.
No matter what the cost, we have no choice but to stop the epidemic as soon as possible and bring our lives back to normal.
Origins of zoonotic diseases of human coronavirus
For thousands of years, mutantity and adaptability have driven the co-evolution of coronaviruses (CoVs) and their hosts, including humans.
Prior to 2003, it was known that there were two human coronaviruses (HCoVs) that can cause minor illnesses, such as the common cold.
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) Flirted the situation and revealed the destructiveness and lethality of HCoV infection.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in central China at the end of 2019 has once again put CoV in the spotlight, surprisingly, it is highly transmissible but less pathogenic compared to its sister virus, SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is a zoonotic disease, so it would be helpful to understand the zoonotic origins of HCoV.
The majority HCoV originates from bats and is non-pathogenic.
A number of HCoV intermediate storage hosts are also known.
Identifying animal hosts has direct implications for the prevention of human diseases.
Studying CoV host interactions in animals may also give us a deeper understanding of the pathogenesis of CoV in humans.
In this review, we provide an overview of the existing knowledge of the seven HCoVs, focusing on their discovery history, zoonotic origins, and interspecies propagation.
It is important that we compare and compare different HCoVs from the perspective of viral evolution and genomic recombination.
In this context, we explored the current 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19) outbreak.
In addition, this article focuses on the requirements for the successful completion of host transitions and the impact of viral evolution on disease severity.
CoV belongs to the coronavirus family and is made up of a righteous single-stranded RNA virus with envelopes.
These viruses have the largest genome in the RNA virus, contains between 2.6 and 32,000 bases, and are called "coronaviruses" (CoVs) due to their coronal morphology under an electron microscope.
Structurally, CoV has a non-segmented genome that shares a similar tissue.
About two-thirds of the genome contains two large overlapping open reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b), which have been translated into pp1a and pp1ab replication enzyme polyproteins.
These polymeric proteins are further processed to produce 16 non-structural proteins, called nsp1 to 16.
The rest of the genome contains ORF of structural proteins, including spikes (S), envelopes (E), membranes (M), and nucleoproteins (N).
Many genealogy-specific adjuvant proteins are also encoded by CoV of different genealogies.
According to the differences in protein sequences, CoV is divided into four genus (alpha-CoV, β-CoV, gamma-CoV and δ-CoV), of which β-CoV contains the majority HCoV and is subdivided into four genealogies (A, B, C, and D).
The phylogenetic evidence suggests that bats and rodents are the genetic source of most α-CoV and β-CoV, while birds are the primary hosts of γ-CoV and δ-CoV.
For thousands of years, CoV has crossed the species barrier, some of which have become important human pathogens.
So far, there have been seven known human-infected coronaviruses (HCoV).
The HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 are alpha-CoV.
The other five beta-CoVs include HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, Severe Acute Respiratory Coronavirus (SARS-CoV), Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV), and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1, and HCoV-NL63 usually cause mild symptoms, such as the common cold and/or diarrhoea.
In contrast, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and the newly discovered SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogenic, leading to severe lower respiratory tract infections in a relatively large number of patients, and are more likely to develop Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) and extrapulmonary manifestations.
In the mid-1960s, the first HCoV-229E strain B814 was isolated from the nasal secretions of patients with the common cold.
Since then, humans have accumulated more relevant knowledge through the extensive study of the HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 viruses that can cause self-limiting symptoms.
In fact, prior to the SARS outbreak, the concept that HCoV was usually harmless was widely accepted.
An outbreak of SARS in 2003 was one of the most destructive events in current history, infecting more than 8,000 people with a coarse case fatality rate of about 10 percent.
Ten years later, after the outbreak of the Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), it continued to spread in the Arabian Peninsula and spread sporadically to the rest of the world.
2019 The new HCoV virus (2019-nCoV, later renamed SARS-CoV-2) is a pathogen that continues to be circulating the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19), which has captured more than 3,120 lives and infected more than 91,000 as of March 3, 2020.
Alerts are ringing, and the world must be ready for the upcoming SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven HCoVs have zoonotic origins from bats, mice, or domestic animals.
There is a variety of evidence that the evolution of all HCoV originated in bats, and the viruses in bats are well-adapted and pathogenic, but show great genetic diversity.
The COVID-19 epidemic has brought great medical, scientific, social and moral challenges to China and the world.
Tracking HCoV’s zoonotic origins provides a framework for understanding the natural history, driving forces, and constraints of virus species jumping.
In addition, this will also guide or promote the search for SARS-CoV-2 storage hosts, intermediate hosts, and expanded animal hosts, which are important for preventing future viral spillovers.
In this review, we provide an overview of HCoV’s zoonotic origins, interspecies propagation, and pathogenesis.
In particular, we highlight and explore a common topic, i.e. HCoV’s parental viruses are not usually pathogenic in their natural storage hosts, but are pathogenic after trans-species spread to new hosts.
In addition, we reviewed trends in the evolution of HCoV, in which an increase in transmissibility is often accompanied by a decrease in pathogenicity.
In this context, we have also discussed the results of the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
Since the end of the 1930’s, animal CoV has become widely known.
Prior to the first separation of the HCoV-229E strain B814 from the nasal secretions of patients with the common cold, doctors had isolated different CoVs in a variety of infected animals, including turkeys, mice, cattle, pigs, cats, and dogs.
In the past few decades, seven HCoVs have been discovered.
A brief chronological summary of HCoV’s discovery (table 1) provides us with a wealth of information and guidance.
In 1966, the first HCoV-229E virus strain was isolated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infections and subsequently adapted for growth in the WI-38 pulmonary cell strain.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E have common cold symptoms such as headache, sneezing, discomfort, and sore throat, and 10-20% of patients have symptoms such as fever and cough.
In the second half of 1967, HCoV-OC43 was isolated from organ cultures in the breast-rat brain and its subsequent succession.
HCoV-OC43 infection has clinical characteristics similar to HCoV-229E infection and does not vary significantly in symptoms compared to infections with other respiratory pathogens, such as Influenza A virus and rhinovirus.
HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are distributed around the world, mainly in temperate winter transmission.
Usually, the incubation period of these two viruses is less than one week, after which it will occur for about 2 weeks.
A human volunteer study showed that healthy individuals infected with HCoV-229E developed a mild common cold.
Only a small number of immunocompromised patients exhibit severe lower respiratory tract infections.
SARS, also known as “atypical pneumonia,” was the first well-documented Pandemic caused by HCoV in human history, and its pathogen is SARS-CoV, the third HCoV to be discovered.
The first cases of SARS can be traced back to Guangdong Province, China, in late 2002.
The SARS outbreak caused 8,096 reported cases and 774 deaths, spreading to many countries and continents.
With the exception of super-spreaders, it is estimated that each case may result in about 2 secondary cases with an incubation period of 4 to 7 days and a spike in viral load on the 10th day of onset.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially show signs of myocardial pain, headache, fever, discomfort and cold, followed by advanced symptoms such as breathing difficulties, coughing and respiratory distress.
Lymphocyte reduction, abnormal hepatic function and elevated creatinate kinase are common laboratory abnormalities in SARS.
In addition, diffuse alveolar damage, epithelial cell proliferation, and increased macrophages were observed in SARS patients.
About 20-30% of patients then require intensive care and mechanical ventilation.
In these critically ill patients, in addition to the lower respiratory tract, multiple organs, including the gastrointestinal tract, the liver and the kidneys, may also be infected, often accompanied by cytokine storms, which can be fatal in immunocompromised patients.
The virus was first isolated from an open pulmonary biopsy by a relative of a patient who traveled from Guangzhou to Hong Kong.
HCoV research has been dedicated to HCoV research ever since.
HCoV-NL63 was isolated from a seven-month-old baby in the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
It was initially found that the virus was circulating in young children, the elderly, and in immunocompromised patients with respiratory diseases.
Among the diseases caused by HCoV-NL63, rhinitis, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchitis are common.
Another independent study showed that the same virus was isolated from the nasal specimens of an eight-month-old boy with pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although the virus was found in the Netherlands, it is actually distributed all over the world.
It is estimated that HCoV-NL63 accounts for about 4.7% of common respiratory diseases, and its onset peaks in early summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 has been associated with obstructive laryngitis (also known as glubu laryngitis).
In the same year, a 71-year-old HCoV-HKU1 was separated from a 71-year-old man, who was hospitalized in Hong Kong for pneumonia and bronchitis.
In addition to community acquired pneumonia and bronchitis, HCoV-HKU1 was reported to be associated with acute asthma attacks.
Similar to HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 is found around the world and can cause mild respiratory diseases.
All four of these community-ucgated HCoVs have been well adapted to humans and are generally less likely to mutate to cause highly pathogenic diseases. There have also been unexpected and unexplained rare subtypes such as HCoV-NL63, which has recently been reported to cause severe lower respiratory tract infections in China.
Usually, when these HCoVs gain the ability to effectively spread and survive in the human body, their toxicity or pathogenicity is also reduced.
MERS-CoV was first isolated from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient with acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia in 2012.
Although the majority of laboratory-confirmed cases come from the Middle East, imported cases have been reported in European countries and in Tunisia, with occasional secondary transmission to close contacts.
In 2015, secondary sexual transmission broke out again in South Korea with 186 confirmed cases.
MERS’ clinical manifestations are similar to those of SARS, characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
In contrast to SARS, many MERS patients also experience acute renal failure, a feature that has so far been unique to MERS in diseases caused by HCoV.
More than 30% of patients experience gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhoea and vomiting.
As of February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases have been reported, with a case fatality rate of up to 34.4%, making MERS-CoV one of the most destructive viruses known in humans.
In mid-to-late December 2019, patients with concentrated pneumonia were found in the city of Wuhan, Hubei Province of Hubei Province of China, which were confirmed by retrospective analysis to be associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection.
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), an ongoing outbreak of lower-respiratory infections caused by SARS-CoV-2 is a public health emergency of international concern and has named the disease COVID-19.
As of March 3, 2020, there have been 90,053 confirmed cases worldwide, with a coarse case fatality rate of 3.4%.
It is worth noting that the case fatality rate is 4.2% in China's Hubei, compared with 1.2% outside Hubei.
As with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 can lead to severe respiratory infections characterized by febrile, coughing and breathing difficulties.
Diarrhea also occurs in some patients.
Pneumonia is one of the most serious symptoms and can rapidly develop into acute respiratory distress syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are very similar due to high nucleotide sequence homology of 82%, they cluster into different branches in phylogenetic trees.
Compared with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 is noticeably less pathogenic but more contagious.
SARS-CoV-2 asymptomatic infections have been reported, which may accelerate the rapid spread of the virus around the world.
By comparing SARS-CoV-2 with six other HCoVs, we can find very interesting similarities and differences.
First of all, the incubation period and course duration of HCoV disease are very similar.
SARS-CoV-2 is in line with the overall trend of the other six HCoV species.
The severity of COVID-19 symptoms ranged between SARS-CoV and the four community-accessible HCoV (HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the one hand, SARS-CoV-2 infections are characterized more common in community-acquired HCoV infections, including non-specific, mild, or even asymptomatic manifestations.
On the other hand, a small percentage of severe COVID-19 cases are also seen in cases of SARS-CoV infection, albeit at a slightly lower rate.
Third, the spread of SARS-CoV-2 also shows interesting pattern characteristics of community-accurable HCoV and SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2, on the one hand, is at least as transmissible as community-acquired HCoV.
On the other hand, whether the transmissibility of SARS-CoV-2, like SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, will decline after human transmission, remains to be verified.
Finally, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in fecal samples, as with other HCoV.
At least in some cases, the fecal-oral propagation of SARS-CoV-2 plays the same important role as in SARS-CoV remains to be clarified by future studies.
It’s also very important to see if SARS-CoV-2 is as seasonal as community-promoted HCoV.
However, SARS-CoV-2 transmissibility, pathogenicity and continuous human transmission will have an important impact on the ultimate fate of the current COVID-19 epidemic.
All four community-acquired HCoVs that cause mild symptoms are well-adapted to humans.
From another point of view, humans have also adapted well to these four HCoVs.
In other words, both sides may be survivors of the ancient HCoV pandemic.
HCoV, which causes serious human diseases, and people with severe HCoV disease, are dead.
To do this, HCoV must replicate enough in humans in order to accumulate adaptive mutations against host limiting factors.
In this sense, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic lasts, the more people are infected, and the more likely it is to fully adapt to humans.
If it adapts well, its spread in humans will be difficult to stop by isolating or other infection control measures.
Over the past few years, these four kinds of community-sanitized coronaviruses have spread in the population, causing people with normal immune function to develop the common cold.
These viruses don’t require animal hosts.
In contrast, highly pathogenic SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV are not well-adapted to humans and cannot be continuously transmitted in humans.
They need to survive and reproduce in zoonotic hosts, and may use one or more intermediate hosts and expand their hosts, looking for opportunities to spread to vulnerable human targets.
SARS-CoV-2 has characteristics similar to SARS-CoV/MERS-CoV and four community-ucicited HCoV.
It’s as highly contagious as community-acquired HCoV, at least for the time being.
However, it’s more pathogenic than the community’s acquired HCoV, and it’s less pathogenic than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It remains to be seen whether it will be fully acclimatized to humans and circulate within humans in the absence of hosts or intermediate animal hosts.
Before discussing the animal origin of HCoV, discussing the definition and characteristics of HCoV's evolutionary hosts, natural hosts, storage hosts, intermediate hosts, and expanding hosts has some guiding significance for us.
If an animal has a closely related ancestor with a highly homologous nucleotide sequence level, the animal will become an evolutionary host of HCoV.
Ancestor viruses are usually adaptable and non-pathogenic in this host.
In the same way, the storage host carries HCoV continuously for a long period of time.
In both cases, the hosts are naturally infected and are natural hosts of HCoV or its parent viruses.
In contrast, if the HCoV is immediately or immediately parasitic into the human body, it will not be well-adapted to the new host, and it is usually pathogenic.
Such an intermediate host can act as a source of zoonotic disease for human infection and play the role of amplifying the host by allowing the virus to replicate briefly, and then spreading it to humans to expand the scale of human infection.
If HCoV does not maintain its propagation within an intermediate host, a terminal infection may occur.
In contrast, HCoV can also adapt to intermediate hosts and even establish long-term locality.
In this case, an intermediate host becomes a natural storage host.
A retrospective analysis of epidemiologic data found that SARS index cases had a history of contact with hunting animals.
Subsequent seroprevalence surveys showed higher levels of immunoglobulin (IgG) against SARS-CoV in animal traders compared to the general population.
For the first time on the live animal market, civet (flower beaver) and a civeted cat were first found to carry almost exactly the same SARS-CoV-like virus as the SARS-CoV virus.
The fact that there have been no further SARS reports after the removal of all civets in the market indirectly supports this view.
However, the report states that farm civets in wild or uncontacted live animal markets were largely negative for SARS-CoV, suggesting that civets may only be intermediate amplified hosts, rather than SARS-CoV natural hosts.
It is worth noting that because 80% of the different animals in the Guangzhou market have SARS-CoV antibodies, it cannot be ruled out that a large number of small mammals may also act as an intermediate expanded host of SARS-CoV.
All of these animals appear to be terminal hosts of SARS-CoV.
A subsequent study of SARS-CoV natural animal hosts found a closely related bat coronavirus known as SARS-associated chrysanthemum bat coronavirus HKU3 (SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3), which survives in Chinese chrysanthemum bats.
These bats were positive for SARS-CoV antibodies and the genome sequences of SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3.
The virus and other bat coronaviruses with SARS-CoV have a nucleotic acid sequence homology of 88-92%.
These studies lay the foundation for the new concept that bats are hosts of newly emerging human pathogens.
In addition, the researchers also identified a variety of SARS-like coronaviruses (SL-CoV) in bats, but with the exception of a specified WIV1, no virus can be isolated as a living virus.
Human angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) is a well-known receptor for SARS-CoV.
Experiments have shown that WIV1 extracted from bat fecal samples uses bats, civets, and human ACE2 as receptors to enter cells.
Interestingly, Serum in SARS recovery patients was able to neutralize WIV1.
So far, WIV1 is the closest ancestor to bat SARS-CoV, with a 95 percent nucleotide sequence homology of nucleotides.
Although these two viruses are highly homologous, WIV1 is generally not considered to be a direct parental virus for SARS-CoV, and bats are not a direct reservoir of SARS-CoV.
The phylogenetic analysis classifies MERS-CoV in the same group as bat CoV-HKU4 and bat CoV-HKU5.
Bacteria CoV-HKU4 and MERS-CoV intruded against the virus using the same host receptor dipeptidease4 (DPP4).
MERS-CoV’s RNA-dependent RNA polymerase sequences are phylogenically closer to their corresponding RNA in beta-CoV in bats from Europe and Africa.
So far, live MERS-CoV has not been found in wild bats.
The MERS-CoV is only 87% nucleotide sequence homology with its close-native bat CoV-HKU25.
As a result, bats may not be a direct reservoir of MERS-CoV.
Studies in the Middle East, on the other hand, have shown seroprevalence positive for MERS-CoV-specific neutralizing antibodies, similar to camels originating in the Middle East in multiple African countries.
The live MERS-CoV isolated from the nasal swabs of dromedics is identical to the virus found in the human body, which further suggests that camels are MERS-CoV’s true reservoir host.
It is also important to point out that in camels infected with MERS-CoV, the general symptoms are mild, but a large amount of the virus has been observed.
It is worth noting that infected camels not only spread the virus through the respiratory tract, but also through the fecal route, which is also the main way for bats to spread the virus.
However, the problem persists, as many MERS-confirmed cases do not have a history of contact with camels prior to the onset of symptoms, which may be attributed to human-to-human or unknown routes of transmission, including unidentified animal species carrying MERS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 is 96.2% nucleotide homology from the chrysanthemum-separatism isolated.
As was the case with SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, the sequence difference between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 was too large to determine parental relationships.
In other words, bats may not be a direct reservoir of SARS-CoV-2 unless nearly identical bat coronaviruses are discovered in the future.
Presumably, SARS-CoV-2’s intermediate animal hosts should be sold and slaughtered in wild animal species sold and slaughtered at the South China Seafood Wholesale Market, and many initial COVID-19 cases are associated, suggesting that animal-to-human propagation events may occur.
Several recent meta-genome-squencing studies have shown that an endangered small mammal known as pangolins (Malay pangolins) may also carry an ancestor beta-CoV associated with SARS-CoV-2.
These new pangolin coronavirus genomes with SARS-CoV-2 have a nucleotide sequence homology of 85-92% nucleotide.
However, they are just as closely associated with RaTG13, with about 90% homology at the nucleotide sequence level.
In the phylogenetic trees, they are classified as two SARS-CoV-2-like virus subsystems, one of which has a more similar receptor binding domain (RBD) to SARS-CoV-2, and has 97.4% amino acid sequence homology.
In stark contrast, SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 have RBD differences that are even greater, even though genome-wide sequence homology is higher.
An earlier study of diseased pangolins also reported the detection of viral overlaps from lung samples, with similar associations with SARS-CoV-2.
Using a variety of assembly methods and manual processing, the study obtained a partial genome sequence, which accounts for about 86.3 percent of the virus’s entire genome.
We can’t rule that pangolins are one of SARS-CoV-2 intermediate animal’s hosts.
However, due to differences in the β-CoV sequences associated with pangolin SARS-CoV-2, there is currently no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 comes directly from pangolins.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is even shorter than the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and pangolin SARS-CoV-2-related β-CoV.
The evolutionary path of SARS-CoV-2 in bats, pangolins and other mammals remains to be determined.
Although the highest sequence homology was found in RBD between SARS-CoV-2 and pangolins, SARS-CoV-2-associated beta-CoV, SARS-CoV-2, and RaTG13 had the highest whole-genome sequence homology.
It can be highly insputably that the high similarity between pangolin SARS-CoV-2-related β-CoV and RBD of SARS-CoV-2 is caused by selective-mediated converging evolution.
The opposite view is that pangolin SARS-CoV-2-associated beta-CoV recommunicated with RaTG13 in a third wildlife species.
As a driving force for evolution, recombination is widespread in beta-CoV.
Inconclusive origin of SARS-CoV-2’s direct zoonotic disease is inconclusive.
In addition to highly pathogenic HCoV , the researchers also looked at zoonotic origins of HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-HKU1.
The phylogenetic evidence suggests that both HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-229E may have originated in bat coronaviruses, while protoviruses of HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1 have also been found in rodents.
According to the report, a bat-coronavirus called ARcoV.2 (Appalachian Ridge CoV), which is found in three-color bats in North America, is strongly associated with HCoV-NL63.
HCoV-229E, on the other hand, is genetically associated with another bat coronavirus found in Ghana (known as Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008), and camelids are also suspected to be their intermediate hosts.
For the sake of clarity, Figures 1 and Table 2 summarize the available knowledge about the animal origins of known HCoV.
phylogenhetic analysis provides evidence for historical interspecies propagation of HCoV events.
Around 1890, when HCoV-OC43 infects humans from livestock across species, there was a pandemic of respiratory infectious diseases.
HCoV-229E interspecies propagation history is unclear.
Bactat alpha-CoV, which is closely related to HCoV-229E, has been discovered.
There is an alpaca α-CoV between the two.
There are several pieces of evidence to support the spread of the virus directly from bats to humans.
First of all, humans, rather than alpacas, may come into contact with bats in a common niche.
On the contrary, humans are in close contact with alpacas.
Second, bat alpha-CoV associated with HCoV-229E is diverse and non-pathogenic in bats, while alpaca α-CoV causes outbreaks of respiratory disease in infected animals.
In the end, alpaca alpha-CoV has not been found in wild animals.
Therefore, it cannot be ruled out that alpacas receive HCoV-229E-related α-CoV from humans cannot be ruled out.
In fact, bats are a direct source of human pathogenic viruses that include rabies, Ebola, Nipah and Hendra viruses.
Therefore, it is not surprising that bats spread the HCoV-229E directly to humans.
Or, although bat alpha-CoV acts as a gene base for HCoV-229E, alpacas and dromedary camels may be intermediate hosts of the virus to humans, in exactly the same situation with MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is a good example of interspecies transmission from bats to dromedary camels as well as from dromedics to humans.
MERS-CoV evolutionary origins of bats are known in their initial identification, and subsequent findings reinforce this argument.
It is clear that bats provide a rich pool of virus species for interspecies exchange and interspecies propagation of gene snippets.
Long-lived and dense groups, close social interaction, and strong flying abilities are all favorable conditions for bats to become the ideal "viral propagator".
MERS-CoV, on the other hand, has been in dromedary camels for decades.
It is well-adapted to these camels, and the camel has changed from an intermediate host to a stable natural storage host.
MERS-CoV causes very mild disease in these animals and maintains a relatively low mutation rate.
Its sporadic propagation of humans is an accident, and due to the inability to continuously propagate, humans are still terminal hosts of MERS-CoV.
Pangolins play a different role in SARS-CoV-2 propagation, if any, compared to the camel’s role in MERS-CoV propagation.
In particular, pangolin beta-CoV is highly pathogenic in pangolins.
They may be SARS-CoV-2-associated β-CoV terminal hosts, similar to civets in SARS-CoV.
In future studies, it will be necessary to identify or rule out several possibilities of SARS-CoV-2 transmission from animals to humans.
First of all, bats may be reservoir hosts of SARS-CoV-2-related viruses that are almost identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans are likely to share niches with bats through slaughter or coal mining.
Secondly, pangolins may be one of the intermediate-expansion hosts of SARS-CoV-2-related virus intrusion.
Humans are infected with the virus by culling and edifying wild food.
Many mammals, including livestock, may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2.
It is necessary to conduct antibody investigations of livestock and wild animals.
Third, as noted above, SARS-CoV-2 may reorganize and acclimatize in a third species that has come into contact with bats and pangolins.
Research on animal origins of SARS-CoV-2 continues.
In addition to different types of animal hosts, the three main factors related to the virus also play an important role in promoting the coronavirus’s interspecies barrier.
First, they have a higher mutation rate in RNA replication.
Compared to other single-stranded RNA viruses, the mutation rate of coronaviruses is estimated to be "moderate" to "high", with an average substitution rate of about 10-4 times per year at each locus 2, according to the coronavirus's adaptive stage for the new host.
Coronaviruses have proof-of-corrigine exonuclease, and its removal can lead to a very high degree of variability and attenuation, or even failure to survive.
Interestingly, the nucleotide-like rendezivir is known to inhibit coronavirus replication by inhibiting this ribonucleic acid exonuclease and the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.
Redcivir is one of the most promising anti-SARS-CoV-2 drugs to be tested in clinical trials.
However, the mutation rate of the coronavirus is about 1 million times higher than that of its host.
In addition, if the coronavirus is not well-adapted to the host, its mutation rate is usually very high.
Compared with SARS-CoV, which has a high mutation rate, the mutation rate of SARS-CoV-2 is significantly lower, indicating that it is more adaptable to humans.
It’s conceivable that it’s adapted to another host that’s close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2 , this also applies to MERS-CoV, which is well-adapted to dromedary camels.
In theory, gene bleaching is unlikely to rapidly disable vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2.
Secondly, the larger RNA genomes in coronaviruses have additional plasticity in genetic mutations and recommunicative modifications, thus increasing the possibility of synergistic co-evolution, which is conducive to the emergence of the new coronavirus when the conditions are suitable.
A large number of unique open reading frames and protein functions encoded towards the end of the genome provide evidence for this.
Third, coronaviruses randomly and frequently switch templates through a unique “replicating selection” mechanism during RNA replication.
In a host that acts as a mixed container, chains are frequently exchanged during CoV RNA transcription.
Highly homologous full-nature and sub-genome RNA recommunicates to produce new coronaviruses.
The phylogenetic evidence for natural recombination has been found in animal coronaviruses such as HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-OC43, as well as in animal coronaviruses such as bat SL-CoV and bat CoV-HKU9.
Virus-host interactions associated with transmission
In addition to the above three virus factors, the interaction between the virus and the host receptor is another key factor affecting interspecies propagation.
A typical example of the recombination of SARS-CoV in this paper shows evidence of positive selection in interspecies propagation events.
Based on a comparative analysis of the isolated strains of human and civet SARS-CoV, the researchers believe that SARS-CoV can be rapidly adapted to different hosts, in particular through mutations in the S protein RBD to enable this process.
In general, the RBD in coronavirus S proteins interacts with cell receptors and is rigorously screened by host antibody responses.
In SARS-CoV, the RBD is located in the 318th to 510 amino acids of the S1 fragment, which binds to human ACE2 and its co-receptors to allow the virus to enter.
The RBD of SARS-CoV is able to recognize ACE2 receptors in a variety of animals, including bats, civets, mice, and cats, allowing the virus to spread between species.
In fact, in RBD, only six amino acid residues of different amino acid isolates were observed, with 4 residues located in the receptor binding motivator that interacts with the ACE2 receptor.
There are K479N and S487T mutations in the RBD of civet SARS-CoV, which may increase the affinity of the fibronite protein for interacting with human ACE2 receptors.
In other words, the replacement of these two amino acids may be crucial for viruses to adapt to humans.
It is worth noting that SARS-CoV-2 has the same cellular receptor as SARS-CoV.
The difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in the S protein S1 unit is 30%, which means that the binding affinity of the S protein to human ACE2 may change.
Indeed, a cryomicroscopic study showed that this binding affinity was 10 to 20 times higher than that between human ACE2 and SARS-CoV S proteins.
It would also be interesting to determine whether any other co-receptors are needed in SARS-CoV-2 propagation.
Interestingly, HCoV-NL63 is also associated with ACE2, but with different parts of S.
In addition, there are a number of other HCoV receptors, such as HCoV-229E's aminopeptide enzyme N and HCoV-OC43 of 9-O-acetylated salic acids.
They may also explain why the above-mentioned coronavirus has successfully adapted to humans after spreading from animal hosts across species.
In addition to cellular receptors, the results of HCoV interspecies propagation are also influenced by other host-dependent and restrictive factors.
The differences between these host proteins in humans and natural HCoV hosts such as bats, dromedaries and rodents may pose barriers to interspecies propagation.
HCoV must deprive the host dependency factor and break the host constraint in order to successfully achieve interspecies propagation.
In this regard, molecular determinants in this important area of viral-host interactions remain to be identified and characterized.
Unbiased genome-wide sifting of SARS-CoV-2’s host-dependent and restrictive factors using the latest CRISPR technology may yield results.
Emergence of the new HCoV: back to the original
The diversity of bat coronaviruses provides ample opportunities for the emergence of novel HCoV.
In this sense, bat coronaviruses act as a gene pool for HCoV.
In addition, rapid mutation and gene recombination are also drivers of HCoV’s evolution and two important steps in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of new protein-coding genes could revolutionize the phenotype of the virus.
In SARS-CoV adjuvant proteins, because SARS-CoV-associated bat viruses were isolated, but they were found to encode different ORF8 proteins, ORF8 was thought to be important for adapting to humans.
29 nucleotide missing signatures of SARS-CoV were found in isolated virus strains.
This deletion classifies ORF8a and ORF8b and is thought to be an adaptive mutation that promotes host conversion.
In addition, SARS-CoV may be recombined with the α- and γ-CoV genealogies, in which a large number of smaller recombinant regions are found in RNA-dependent RNA polymerases.
Reconfiguration sites have also been found in nsp9, most nsp10, and partial nsp14.
Similarly, there have been studies showing that the popularity of MERS-CoV has experienced recombinant events between different pedigres, which occur in dromedary camels in Saudi Arabia.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV , recombinant events were also observed in other HCoV, in which HCoV recombined with other animal coronaviruses in their non-structural genes.
It should also be noted that artificial selection may lead to unexpected changes in the viral genome, which is likely due to the reduction of the selection pressure exerted by the host immune system of the virus.
An example of these effects is in the HCoV-229E protovirus strain, in which the absence of double-nucleotides results in the absence of full-long ORF4.
Although complete ORF4 can be observed in bats and camel viruses associated with HCoV-229E, alpaca α-CoV shows a single nucleotide insertion, causing a transcoding mutation.
Last but not least, the evolution of the new HCoV is also driven by the pressure of storage host selection.
After bats are infected with the coronavirus, asymptomatic or only mild symptoms are detected, indicating mutual adaptation between the coronavirus and bats.
From an anatomical and physiological point of view, bats appear to be well adapted to coronavirus.
For example, defects activated by the pro-inflammatory response in bats have effectively reduced the pathological changes triggered by the coronavirus.
In addition, due to the positive regulation of the inhibitory natural killer cell receptor NKG2/CD94 and the low level of molecular expression of the major histocompatibility complex I, the bat's natural killer cell activity is inhibited.
Moreover, the high level of active oxygen (ROS) produced by the high metabolic activity of bats inhibits coronavirus replication and can be detected by ribonucleic acid exonucible enzymes, thus providing a selective pressure to produce high pathogenicity when the virus enters the new host.
More pathogenic coronavirus strains may also evolve by recommunicating, resulting in the acquisition of new proteins or protein signatures to accommodate the host.
It’s therefore no accident that three new HCoVs have emerged over the past two decades.
Coronaviruses do not cause illness or cause mild symptoms in reservoirs such as bats and camels.
They do a lot of replication and do not cause a strong host immune response.
That’s why asymptomatic carriers, asymptomatic carriers, as well as cases that lead to serious infections in humans.
Severe symptoms are mainly due to overactivation of the immune response and cytokine storms, so the stronger the immune response, the more severe the lung damage.
In contrast, in asymptomatic carriers, the immune response has been decoupled from coronavirus replication.
The same strategy for destranding the immune response may play a positive role in anti-SARS-CoV-2 treatment.
Interferon reactions in bats are particularly strong.
Therefore, it should be beneficial at least in the early stages of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans using type I interferon.
In addition, NLRP3 inflammatory body activation in bats is also defective.
On the basis of this reasoning, the use of MCC950 to suppress NLRP3 inflammatory bodies may be useful for the treatment of COVID-19.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows the general themes on which SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV were produced.
Although β-CoV has been found to have a 95% nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV, there is also a 96% nucleotide homology of nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV-2.
Although civets and other animals in the market have been found to carry the same virus as SARS-CoV, a direct intermediate host for SARS-CoV-2 has not yet been determined.
Prominent homologous pangolin beta-CoV with SARS-CoV-2 has been found, suggesting that pangolins may be one of the intermediate hosts, or pangolin β-CoV may have contributed gene fragments to the final version of SARS-CoV-2.
Although there are still doubts, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 was intentionally or unintentionally man-made.
Due to the recent outbreak of SARS-CoV-2, the coronavirus has once again become the focus of people's attention.
The study of coronaviruses in bats and other animals has dramatically changed our understanding of HCoV zoonotic origins and the importance of animal hosts in human transmission.
Substantial evidence suggests that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and SARS-CoV-2 originated in bats and transmitted to humans through intermediate hosts.
Given that SARS-CoV infection originates in contact between humans and civets on the market, closing the wet goods market and killing foxes could effectively put an end to the SARS epidemic.
In the same way, in view of the multiple lineages of pangolin beta-CoV, which are closely related to SARS-CoV-2, pangolins should be removed from the wet goods market to prevent the spread of zoonotic diseases.
But whether SARS-CoV-2 will be transmitted to humans through pangolins and other mammals remains to be studied.
MERS-CoV, on the other hand, has been around for a long period of time in dromedary camels.
These camels are an important means of transportation and a major source of meat, milk, leather and yarn products for the locals.
They are widely distributed in the Middle East and Africa.
Therefore, it is not possible to follow the example of the Chinese wildlife market to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 by sacrificing all camels to control MERS.
In order to prevent the recurrence of MERS, a comprehensive initiative should be taken in conjunction with other infection control measures to develop an effective anti-MERS-CoV vaccine for camels.
Since we cannot eliminate these viruses, new genotypes may emerge, leading to outbreaks.
A variety of zoonotic coronaviruses circulate in the wild.
In particular, bat-to-coronavirus diversity with zoonotic potential is high.
These zoonotic coronaviruses have many opportunities to evolve and reorganize, leading to new coronaviruses that are more likely to spread and/or be deadly in humans in the future.
In some parts of China, the culture of eating wild animals should be abandoned in order to reduce unnecessary contact between humans and animals.
After SARS, MERS and COVID-19, we should develop better prevention and response plans.
In fact, many viruses have been on Earth for a long time.
They stay with their natural hosts until there is an opportunity to overflow.
Although bats have many characteristics conducive to the spread of the virus, human exposure to bats and other wild species is greatly reduced if people are educated away from bats and other wild species.
In order to better understand the ecological status of coronaviruses and their natural hosts, there is a need for continuous monitoring of mammals, which will prove to be very useful in preventing animal-to-human transmission as well as future outbursts.
In short, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonotic diseases is for humans to stay away from the ecological position of the natural host of zoonotic viruses.
There are still a number of gaps in SARS-CoV-2’s zoonotic origin mystery.
First of all, if bats spread an ancient SARS-CoV-2 virus into pangolins, it makes sense to look at when bats and pangolins can share the same niche.
Second, if bats play a more direct role in the spread of humans, how humans come into contact with bats remains to be determined.
Third, if a third mammal acts as a true intermediate host, it must become clear how it interacts with different species including humans, bats and pangolins.
Finally, since many mammals, including domestic animals, may be susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, surveillance and experimental infection studies should be conducted.
For bats, pangolins, or other mammals, SARS-CoV-2 is expected to be found in their natural hosts, or almost the same parental virus.
Continued research in this area will shed light on the evolutionary pathways of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, which is important for the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
It is necessary to update the diagnostic criteria for COVID-19 "suspected cases" and "confirmed cases"
On February 6, 2020, our team released the Guidelines for Rapid Recommendations for Diagnosis and Diagnosis and Diagnosis of 2019 Novel Coronavirus (2019-nCoV) Infections, through which we provide experience and lessons for the global response to the epidemic.
However, the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is a novel disease based on ongoing research results and clinical practical experience, and our understanding of the disease is gradually improving; therefore, diagnosis and treatment strategies are constantly being updated.
In this letter, we respond to a comment on the above guidelines and provide the latest diagnostic criteria for “suspected cases” and “confirmed cases” according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Program (7th Edition) issued by the National Health Commission of the People’s Republic of China.
In December 2019, the 2019 novel coronavirus (2019-nCoV) outbreak has been officially named Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19), and the virus has been designated as Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
On March 11, 2020, the World Health Organization listed COVID-19 as a global pandemic.
To combat SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team developed rapid advice guidelines, which were published online February 06, 2020 in the journal Military Medical Research.
Since its release, it has attracted a lot of attention.
However, please note that COVID-19 is a new type of the disease, based on ongoing research results and clinical practical experience, our understanding of the disease is gradually improving; therefore, diagnosis and treatment strategies are constantly being updated.
For example, the People’s Republic of China’s National Health Commission (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/) released seven versions of the COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Plan between January 16, 2020 and March 3, 2020, some of which have undergone substantial changes.
Now, our guide has received input from Zhou and others who have come up with a simple scoring regimen based on their clinical experience.
Their work sheds new light on our guidelines and a valuable reference for this global pandemic.
We recognize and express our gratitude for the important work they have done.
However, their proposal also needs to be updated according to the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis Program (Trial Seventh Edition), as well as recent studies.
According to the 7th edition (March 3, 2020), the diagnosis of suspected cases requires a combination of an epidemiologic historical feature and two clinical manifestations for a comprehensive analysis, and if there is no clear epidemiological history, three clinical manifestations need to be met:
Epidemiologic history: (1) travel or habitation in the city and surrounding areas or other communities that have reported COVID-19 cases within 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms; (2) have a history of contact with SARS-CoV-2 infection cases (nucleic acid test-positive); (3) have had a history of contact with patients with fever or respiratory symptomatic symptoms from a community with COVID-19 in the 14 days prior to the onset of the symptoms; (4) a history of contact with patients with fever or respiratory symptoms in a limited area within 14 days prior to the onset of symptoms.
Clinical manifestations: (1) febrile and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) imaging characteristics of COVID-19 infection; and (3) normal or decreasing of the total number of leukocytes in the early stages of the disease and a decrease in the lymphocyte count.
Diagnosis of confirmed cases should be based on a suspected case, with any of the following pathological or serological evidence: (1) real-time PCR test-positive for SARS-CoV-2; (ii) Viral whole-genome Sequencing showed a high homonymity with known novel coronaviruses; and (3) specific IgM and IgG antibodies against SARS-CoV-2 in the seroviral trials; or SARS-CoV-2-specific IgG antibodies.
As we can see, real-time PCR nucleic acid testing in respiratory tract or blood specimens has been added in the second (January 18, 2020) and the third edition (January 22, 2020).
The 4th (January 27, 2020) and 5th edition (February 8, 2020) added hematological assays; serological evidence was later added in the 7th edition.
These modifications are based on the ongoing work of the researchers to study optimal nucleic acid test kits to achieve rapid diagnosis, as well as respiratory tract specimens including blood collection, which increases the availability of different specimens and provides evidence for the incorporation of positive results of specific antibodies into the diagnostic criteria.
In addition, there is a growing body of evidence to remind us of atypical and asymptomatic and asymptomatic patients.
Therefore, the flowchart of Zhou et al. should be updated, because they classify people with no clinical symptoms as “low-risk”.
In addition, the scoring system needs to be validated in further clinical practice and research.
In conclusion, we would like to see more direct evidence and call on readers to comment.
For the diagnosis of "suspected cases" and "confirmed cases", we recommend referring to and following the latest guidelines of the country where the cases are located.
Our team will also update our guidelines in a timely manner to help.
Bangladesh reports 5 new COVID-19 deaths, a single-day high
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new COVID-19 deaths on the same day.
This is the highest single-day death rate caused by the virus.
As of yesterday, there have been 114 confirmed cases of infection recorded by the Bangladesh Institute of Epidemiology, Disease Control and Prevention (IEDCR), and 33 cases have been curated after rest at home.
A total of 17 deaths occurred.
Speaking at an online news conference, Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora, head of the IEDCR, said the deaths included four men and one woman.
According to Dr. Meerjady, there were two cases between the ages of 60 and older, two between the ages of 51-60 and one between the ages of 41 and 50.
She also said that two of the patients who died came from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a global pandemic on March 11.
A hospital official revealed to local news outlet Anadolu Agency that one of the deceased patients was Jalal Saifur Rahman, director of the Bangladesh Anti-Corruption Commission, who had been treated at Kuwait Maitree Hospital.
On Saturday, Obaidul Quader, Bangladesh’s minister of road transport and bridges, announced in an online video that public transport outages would be extended beyond its original plan to this Saturday.
The public transportation shutdown began on March 26 and was originally scheduled to end on Saturday, April 4.
Basic items such as medical care, fuel and food can still be transported normally.
Of the first confirmed cases of COVID-19 recorded in Bangladesh on March 8, two returned from Italy, with the other being the wife of a confirmed patient.
As of March 19, the three patients had recovered.
SARS-CoV-2 affects more than a million people worldwide
According to data from the Johns Hopkins University, the total number of SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus infections in the world exceeded 1 million on Thursday.
At least 52,000 deaths have been linked to COVID-19, a disease triggered by the coronavirus.
On the same day that the milestone occurred, Malawi confirmed the country’s first case of coronavirus infection and the first coronavirus-related death in Zambia.
As of Thursday, North Korea said it was one of the few countries without coronavirus infection.
As of yesterday, the World Health Organization (WHO) had reported 1051,635 confirmed cases, including 79,332 in the 24-hour period prior to 10 a.m.
There have been more than 244,000 confirmed cases of coronavirus in the United States, resulting in at least 5,900 deaths.
CBS News, citing data from the Johns Hopkins University, reported that the coronavirus infection killed more than 1,000 people in the U.S. on Wednesday.
Throughout the world, more stringent measures have been announced in various countries to curb the spread of the disease.
Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin on Thursday extended it to May 1.
President Vladimir Putin has announced that Russians across the country will continue to be remunerated until April 30.
The Portuguese parliament decided to extend the state of emergency for 15 days; the vote was adopted with 215 votes in favor, 10 abstentions and one vote against.
Saudi Arabia extended a curfew in the holy cities of Mecca and Medina to an all-day curfew, which had previously been in effect only between 3 p.m. and 6 a.m.
Thailand plans to impose a curfew between 10 p.m. and 4 a.m.
Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine announced that the state has extended a stay-at-home order until May 1.
Shops in Australia have lowered the limit on the amount of toilet paper per transaction.
On Sunday and Saturday evenings, Woolworth’s and Coles, Australia’s chain stores, respectively, lowered the limit for every transaction of toilet paper purchases in all of their stores to two and one pack.
ALDI also introduced a one-package restriction on Monday.
These restrictions are posted in the form of messages on the cash register or posted to the chain’s Facebook page.
Buyers hoard goods for fear of COVID-19 in case they need self-isolation.
On Wednesday, Woolworths also limited the number of door-to-door toilet paper purchases to a single package.
Prior to these changes, Woolworths and Coles introduced a limit of 4 packs per transaction on March 4th and 5th, respectively.
Coles, in its March 8 press release, reported that “many stores were still selling out within an hour of their arrival” following the imposition of a four-package restriction, calling the demand “unprecedented,” while ALDI called it “unanticipated” in a Facebook post on Tuesday.
According to a Woolworths spokesman, there was a “dramatic increase” in sales last week.
Last week, Costco stores in Canberra were also limited to two packs.
To further assuage the shortage, Coles ordered larger packages from its suppliers and increased the frequency of deliveries, with Woolworths ordering additional inventory, while ALDI prepared the inventory ahead of schedule for the planned Wednesday special.
Russell Zimmerman, executive director of the Australian Retailers’ Association, said retailers were trying to increase inventory, but local council restrictions on truck delivery times made this difficult.
He anticipates that production costs will rise as suppliers do their best to meet the demand, while preferential activities will decrease.
On Tuesday, the ALDI announced that some stores will not be able to hold Wednesday special offers after the early release of inventory.
In a report by News.com.au, Dr Gary Mortimer, a retail specialist at Queensland University of Technology, said stores replenished their inventory every night.
He said that toilet paper is a large item, so there is a small amount of inventory, and once sold out, it will leave a huge shelf space, exacerbating the feeling of material shortages.
“The Coles and Woolworths’ view is that if there are a lot of things on the shelves, goods such as reels and hand sanitizer that can be [available] and there’s plenty, you probably won’t panic,” Russell Zimmerman told ABC News.
Recycled toilet paper manufacturer Who Gives a Crap said last Wednesday that they no longer have stock.
Kimberly-Clark, who manufactures toilet paper, and Solaris Paper, which makes Sorbent toilet paper, have stressed that they are working around the clock, on weekends to guarantee supplies.
Domain.com, a real estate website, reported that due to the small number of auctions held by buyers during the Labor Day long weekend break, some real estate dealers will provide free toilet paper to the first bidder at the auction held in Melbourne.
NT News, a daily newspaper printed in the city of Darwin, included an eight-page insert in its Thursday edition, designed to be cropped and used as toilet paper.
According to ABC Australia on March 3, the store was initially reluctant to implement the purchase restrictions, and they said that they had no plans to implement the purchase restrictions.
Russell Zimmerman added that there is also a high demand for other goods, including masks, hand sanitizer, dry goods, hand sanitizer and flour.
Similarly, outside of Australia, Ocado, a UK-based online supermarket, was capping the purchase of Andres toilet paper to 2 packs of 12 rolls per package on Sunday night.
The World Health Organization has declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
On Wednesday, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared an ongoing outbreak of COVID-19 (disease caused by coronavirus SARS-CoV-2) a pandemic.
Although the term “pandemic” refers only to the extent to which the disease has spread rather than the level of risk in a particular case, the World Health Organization (WHO) pointed out the need to urge governments to take measures to:
“All countries/regions can still change the course of this pandemic.
If every country or region tests, tests, treats, isolates, tracks and mobilises its people as a response,” WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus said.
“We are deeply concerned about the alarming level of proliferation and severity, as well as the alarming level of inaction.”
Dr Tom Frieden, a former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, said the pandemic was “unprecedented.”
Commenting on CNN in February, he said: “In addition to the flu, it has not yet been found that any respiratory virus has continued to spread around the world since its inception.”
Ghebreyesus expressed a similar view, saying "we've never seen a coronavirus-induced pandemic before."
He continued, “Similarly, we’ve never seen a controlled viral pandemic before.”
Ahead of entering a new state of the pandemic, the WHO identified the outbreak in January as a public health emergency of international concern.
Dr. Anthony Fauci, director of the U.S. Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, said of the outbreak, “the most important thing is that it’s still getting worse.”
As of Thursday, the Associated Press reported at least 126,000 COVID-19 cases around the world, resulting in more than 4,600 deaths.
The 2019-2020 Coronavirus Outbreak is the ongoing 2019 novel coronavirus disease (COVID-19), caused by Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
This outbreak was confirmed in Wuhan, China in December 2019, and was listed as a public health emergency of international concern on January 30, 2020, and was recognized as an epidemic on March 11, 2020.
As of April 10, 2020, about 1.61 million COVID-19 cases have been reported in 210 countries and territories around the world, with about 97,000 deaths.
It cures about 36.4 million people.
The case fatality rate in China is about 4%, compared to between 0.08% (New Zealand) and 13.04% (Algeria).
Common symptoms include febrile fever, cough and shortness of breath.
It may also be accompanied by complications such as pneumonia and acute respiratory distress syndrome.
The incubation period ranging from exposure to the onset of the disease ranges from 2 to 14 days and is usually about 5 days.
At present, there is no vaccine or specific antiviral treatment program.
Clinical treatment is based on symptomatic support treatment. Recommended preventive measures include frequent hand-washing, occulting the mouth when coughing, maintaining a safe distance from others, monitoring and self-isolating of suspected infected people.
Official agencies have taken measures such as travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, the prevention and control of workplace hazards and the closure of facilities.
The global socio-economic impact has taken a heavy toll, sports, religious, political and cultural activities have been postponed or cancelled, and panic shopping has exacerbated a large-scale supply shortage.
193 countries and regions around the world have implemented national or local suspension measures, and about 99.4% of primary and secondary school and university students are affected.
Due to the spread of false viral information on the Internet, there have been incidents of xenophobia and discrimination against Chinese people, other East and South-East Asian and South-East Asian ethnic groups, as well as groups in regions with severe epidemics.
However, the reduction of travel and the closure of heavy industry have reduced the air pollution index and reduced carbon emissions.
On December 31, 2019, the health department in Wuhan (Hubei Provincial capital) reported a number of unexplained pneumonia cases, and an investigation was launched in early January 2020.
Most of these cases are related to the wholesale seafood market in South China, so the new coronavirus is believed to be zoonotic.
Known as SARS-CoV-2, the virus that triggered the outbreak is a newly-discovered virus that is closely related to bat coronavirus, pangonolin coronavirus, and SARS-CoV. The earliest identified patients were later confirmed to become ill on December 1, 2019, and there was no clear link to a number of cases in the South China Seafood Market.
Two-thirds of these early cases notified in December 2019 were confirmed to be related to the South China Seafood Market.
On March 13, 2020, the South China Morning Post published an unsubstantiated report saying that the first case may date back to November 17, 2019, a 55-year-old male from Hubei Province. On February 26, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) reported a decline in newly reported cases in China, but the number of newly reported cases in Italy, Iran, and South Korea suddenly increased significantly, and the number of new cases outside of China exceeded the country for the first time.
There may be a large number of unreported cases, especially mild cases.
As of February 26, a relatively small number of juvenile cases have been reported, accounting for only 2.4% of the world's 19-year-olds and under. Patrick Vallance, Britain's chief scientific adviser, estimates that about 60% of the UK's population needs to be infected with the new coronavirus in order to achieve effective herd immunity.
The number of cases refers to the number of people who have received COVID-19 testing and have confirmed the positive test result according to the official program.
As of March 23, the proportion of people around the world tested did not more than 3% of the total population, and many other countries have also issued official policies that do not detect people with mild diseases, including Italy, the Netherlands, Spain and Switzerland.
According to a study published on March 16, as of January 23, an estimated 86% of people living with COVID-19 in China have not been tested, and these unrecorded infected people are the source of infection of 79% of recorded cases.
According to a statistical analysis released on March 30, the actual number of infections in Italy far exceeds the number of reported cases.
The basic infection number (R0) of COVID-19 was initially estimated to be between 1.4 and 2.4.
A study published by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention suggests that R0 may be 5.7.
Most COVID-19 patients recover.
Patients who are unable to resuscitate are between 6 and 41 days from onset to death, which is the most common for 14 days.
As of April 10, 2020, about 97,000 people had died from COVID-19.
As of Feb. 5, about 80 percent of the deaths in China were people over the age of 60, and 75 per cent were accompanied by underlying diseases such as cardiovascular disease and diabetes. The official statistics of COVID-19 deaths usually refer to patients who have tested positive for the new coronavirus according to an official program.
Actual COVID-19 deaths may far outpace statistics, as this data may not include patients who have not been tested, such as those who have died at home, sanatorium and other places.
According to some data from Italy, the actual number of deaths during the epidemic is 4-5 times the number of deaths from new coronavirus pneumonia in official statistics.
A spokeswoman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) said: "We know that [the reported death toll] is lower than the actual value", and rumors that the U.S. data are undervalued also confirms this statement. Underestimation occurs frequently in epidemic areas, such as the 2009 H1N1 swine flu epidemic. On January 9, 2020, there was the first confirmed death in China in Wuhan.
On February 1, the first case of death outside China appeared in the Philippines, and on February 14, the first death outside of Asia appeared in France.
As of February 28, there have been more than a dozen deaths in Iran, South Korea and Italy outside China.
As of March 13, more than 40 countries and territories on all continents other than Antarctica have reported deaths. There are several methods commonly used to quantify mortality.
These numbers vary from region to region and change over time and are influenced by population characteristics such as age, gender, quality of the health system, treatment options, the timing of the first outbreak, and population characteristics such as age, sex, and overall health. The case fatality rate is the number of deaths within a given period of time divided by the number of people diagnosed.
As of April 10, 2020, the global case fatality rate was 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204).
Mortality rates are also different in different regions.
In China, the estimated case fatality rate dropped from 17.3 per cent (prevalence between January 1 and 10, 2020) to 0.7 per cent (mortality after February 1, 2020). Some other measures include case mortality (CFRs) and infection death rates (IFRs), which reflect disease deaths as a percentage of the number of people diagnosed, and the latter as a percentage of the number of infections (including the number of confirmed and undiagnosed).
There is no time limit to these statistics, and it tracks the entire process from infection to case resolution in a specific population.
Many scholars try to calculate the above data for specific groups of people.
According to the University of Oxford’s Evidence-Based Medical Center, the overall infection death rate in the outbreak ranges between 0.1% and 0.39 per cent.
The upper estimate limit for this range is in line with Germany’s first randomized detection of COVID-19, as well as a statistical analytical study to detect the effects of CFR estimates.
The World Health Organization says the outbreak is under control.
Outbreak peaks and final durations cannot be determined and vary from region to region.
Maciej Boni, from Penn State University, points out: “If left unchecked, infectious disease outbreaks usually enter a period of stagnating after existing hosts are exhausted, and then begin to moderate.
But at the moment, it is almost impossible to reasonably predict when an inflection point will occur.”
Zhong Nanshan, a senior medical adviser to the Chinese government, believes that if all countries are mobilized to adopt the World Health Organization's proposal to take measures to stop the spread of the virus, then "the end of the epidemic in June can be expected."
On March 17, Adam Kucharski, from the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, said that SARS-CoV-2 “is likely to erupt repeatedly for one to two years.”
Research led by Neil Ferguson at the Imperial Institute of Technology shows that it is important to maintain physical distancing and other measures “before a vaccine can be put into use (which may take 18 months or more).
William Schaffner, from Vanderbilt University, said: "This coronavirus is very transmissible, and I don't think it's likely to go away completely" and "it could morph into a seasonal disease with annual outbreaks."
The pathogenicity of repeated outbreaks depends on herd's immunity and the degree of mutation.
COVID-19 symptoms are relatively non-specific, and some infected people may be asymptomatic.
Two of the most common symptoms are fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, respiratory sputum (sputum), impaired olfactory perception, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throats, headaches, chills, vomiting, bleeding, diarrhoea, or collation. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), about one in six patients can become severe and have difficulty breathing.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) lists breathing difficulties, persistent chest pain or stress, sudden blurred consciousness, difficulty waking, and bulging on the face or lips as an urgent symptom; if these symptoms occur, it is recommended to seek immediate medical attention. The further development of the disease can lead to severe pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, sepsis, sluggishness and death.
Some infected people may be asymptomatic and do not have any clinical performance, but the test results are positive, so the researchers recommend close observation and examination of the confirmed patient's close contacts to rule out the possibility of infection.
In China, the proportion of asymptomatic infected people is estimated to range from very low to 44%.
The incubation period (interval of time from infection to onset) is typically 1 to 14 days; 5 days is the most common. In terms of uncertainty, it can be illustrated by the fact that the proportion of patients with COVID-19 who have lost their sense of olfactory was initially estimated at 30% and later reduced to 15%.
A number of details about the route of transmission of the disease have not yet been determined.
At present, it is believed that the disease is mainly transmitted through close contact as well as droplets generated during coughing, sneezing, or talking; close contact refers to the interval of 1-2 meters (3-6 feet).
The study found that an unobstructed cough can cause droplets to travel up to 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (15-27 feet).
It has also been suggested that people's droplets remaining in the air for a longer period of time can also lead to the spread of the virus. Although the virus usually does not spread through the air, it can also produce droplets when people exhale or speak.
These droplets may fall into the mouths and noses of the surrounding people, and may even be inhaled into the lungs.
Medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) can negate respiratory secretions, resulting in airborne transmission.
In addition, if a person touches the virus-contaminated surface (including the skin), and then touches the mouth of their own eyes and nose, it can also cause the virus to spread.
There are also concerns that the virus may spread through feces, but this risk is thought to be lower.
The Chinese government has denied the possibility of SARS-CoV-2 fecal oral propagation. The virus is most contagious in the first three days of the disease, but it is also contagious before the onset of the disease and in the later stages of the disease.
It is possible to test a positive result as early as three days before the onset of the disease, which indicates that the new coronavirus is already contagious before obvious symptoms appear.
Although laboratory-diagnosed asymptomatic cases are rarely reported, asymptomatic infections have been detected in some countries in the process of conducting contact tracing surveys.
According to the European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), although it is not entirely clear about the spread of the new coronavirus, 2-3 people are usually infected by one person. The survival time of the virus on the surface of the object ranges from a few hours to a few days.
Specifically, it is known that the new coronavirus can survive for up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel for up to three days, one day on cardboard, and up to four hours on copper.
However, its survival time is also affected by humidity and temperature. There have already been positive cases of COVID-19 in pets and other animals.
There is currently no evidence of re-transmunportation of the virus to humans, but UK authorities advise people to wash their hands after coming into contact with an animal, like other surfaces that may have been touched by an infected person.
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Corona (SARS-CoV-2) is a new type of virus that was first isolated from three pneumonia patients in a batch of acute respiratory disease cases in Wuhan.
All the features of the new SARS-CoV-2 virus are present in nature-related coronaviruses. Before the virus enters the human body, it can be annihilated by household soap, which dissolves the virus's protective membrane. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
It is now considered a zoonotic disease.
According to genetic analysis, the coronavirus is genetically associated with the genus Sarbecovirus subgenus of the β genus Sarbecovirus and a cluster of two bat-derived virus strains.
Coronavirus and other bat coronavirus samples (BatCov RaTG13) were 96% consistent at the genome-wide level.
In February 2020, Chinese researchers discovered that viruses in pangolgolins and viruses in the human body are only one amino acid in certain parts of the genome sequence.
So far, the genome-wide comparison has found that up to 92% of the genetic material between pangolin coronavirus and SARS-CoV-2 is the same, which is not enough to prove that pangolin is an intermediate host.
The infection of the virus can be initially diagnosed according to the symptoms, but it can eventually be diagnosed by the reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) or CT imaging of infected secretions.
A PCR and CT comparative study conducted in Wuhan shows that CT is significantly more sensitive than PCR, but the specificity is low, and many imaging features overlap with other pneumonia and disease development processes.
As of March 2020, the American Radiological Society recommended that “CT should not be used to screen for COVID-19 or as the first test to diagnose the disease.”
The World Health Organization has announced a number of nucleic acid detection programs for SARS-CoV-2, the first of which was released on January 17.
The nucleic acid test uses real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR).
Respiratory samples or blood samples can be used for testing.
The results can usually be obtained within a few hours to a few days.
In general, this test is carried out by nasopharyngeal swabs and can also be done through pharyngeal swabs. Many laboratories and companies are developing serological tests to detect antibodies.
As of April 6, 2020, none of these assays had been proven to be accurate enough to warrant widespread use.
In the United States, serological tests developed by Cellex have been approved for use only by accredited laboratories in emergency situations.
Typical imaging characteristics of X tablets and computed tomography (CTs) in pathogenic patients include periphery glass shadow asymmetry and no thoracic cavity fluid.
The Italian Radiological Society is compiling an international online database on the image representation of confirmed cases.
Because it overlaps with other viral infections (such as adenoviruses), images that are not confirmed by PCR have limited specificity in identifying COVID-19.
A large-scale study in China compared chest CT results with PCR results and found that although the images are less specific to identifying infections, the detection is faster and more sensitive, so it can be considered as a screening tool in endemic areas.
Artificial intelligence-based convolutional neural networks have been developed, which can be combined with X-chip and CT to detect the imaging characteristics of viruses.
Measures to prevent the spread of the disease include good personal hygiene practices, frequent hand-washing, avoiding unwashing directly touching the eyes and nose, coughing or sneezing with paper towels to cover the mouth and nose, and using paper towels thrown directly into the trash.
Suspected infected people should wear medical surgical masks when entering public places.
It is also recommended to maintain physical distancing measures to prevent the spread of the virus. Many governments have restricted or recommended avoiding non-necessary travel to countries and regions affected by the outbreak.
However, the virus has now reached the stage of community transmission in most parts of the world.
This means that the virus is spreading within the community, and there is no way for some community residents to know where they are or how they are infected. Medical staff who come into contact with suspected infected people should take standard preventive measures, contact prevention measures, and eye protection. Tracking contacts is an important means for health authorities to determine the source of infection and prevent further transmission.
The government’s use of mobile phone-location data for tracking has raised privacy concerns, with more than 100 organizations including Amnesty International issuing a statement calling for restrictions on such oversight.
There are currently a variety of mobile applications that have been put into use or have entered proposals for voluntary use, and as of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups are working on privacy-preserving solutions, such as using Bluetooth to record the distance between users and other mobile phones.
Once the user has been in close contact with patients who have tested positive for COVID-19, they will be alerted by the information. Misperegations to prevent the infection continue to spread; rinsing the nose and rinsing the mouth with a mouthwash is invalid.
The COVID-19 vaccine does not yet exist, but many organizations are actively developing it.
It is recommended to stop the spread of the disease through frequent hand washing.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention advises people to regularly wash their hands with soap and tap water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or having obvious dirt on their hands, before meals, and after engping their nose, coughing, or sneezing.
This is because before the virus enters the human body, it is only necessary to use household soap to destroy its protective bladder film, which plays a role in destroying the virus.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) also recommends that if you don’t have soap and running water, you can wash your hands with at least a 60 percent alcohol-containing hand sanitizer.
The World Health Organization (WHO) advises people to avoid touching their eyes, nose or mouth without washing their hands.
A variety of solutions can be sterilized on the surface of the object (the disinfectant can take effect less than one minute on the stainless steel surface), including 62-71% ethanol, 50-100% isopropanol, 0.1% sodium hypochlorite, 0.5% hydrogen peroxide and 0.2-7.5% polyvirone iodine.
Other solutions, such as benzachloroammonium chloride and chlorhex gulfonate, are less effective.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that if there are suspected or confirmed cases of novel coronavirus pneumonia in an office or daycare facility, all areas should be sterilized, including offices, bathrooms, public areas, shared electronic devices, such as tablets, touchscreens, keyboards, remote controls, and ATMs used by patients.
WHO advises people to cover their mouths and noses with crooked elbows or paper towels when coughing or sneeze, and to dispose of all contaminated paper towels immediately.
Suspected infected people are advised to wear medical surgical masks, because wearing masks can reduce the amount of droplets exhaled when talking, sneezing and coughing, and shortening the transmission distance.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has issued a statement on the use of masks and methods.
Stephen Griffin, a virologist at the University of Leeds, believes that "wearing a mask can reduce people's propensity to touch the face, and in the absence of hand hygiene, touching the face is also a major source of infection."
The World Health Organization recommends that healthy people wear masks only in high-risk areas, such as those responsible for caring for COVID-19 patients, but the organization also recognizes that wearing masks can help avoid touching faces.
Some countries and regions have begun to encourage the general public to wear masks.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends wearing non-medical masks made of cloth. China explicitly advises healthy citizens to also wear disposable medical masks, especially when in close contact with others within 1 meter (3 feet).
It is recommended to wear a mask on public transportation or when entering crowded places.
Thai health officials advised people to make their own masks and wash them every day.
In the Czech Republic and Slovakia, it is strictly forbidden not to wear a mask or to cover their mouths and noses to enter public places.
On March 16, Vietnam requires everyone to wear a mask to enter public places to protect themselves and others.
The Austrian government stipulates that everyone must wear a mask to enter the grocery store.
Israel requires all residents to wear masks in public.
The daily production of masks in Taiwan since the middle of March has reached 10 million, and on April 1, it began requiring them to wear masks on trains and intercity buses.
Panama stipulates that masks must be worn when going out, and it is recommended that residents who can not buy masks make their own masks.
Masks are also widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Infection prevention and control measures related to maintaining social distance (also known as maintaining physical distance) are aimed at minimizing close human-to-human contact, thereby slowing the spread of the disease.
Measures include quarantine; travel restrictions; school stoppages, shutdowns and closures of stadiums, theatres or shopping malls.
In terms of maintaining social distance, individuals can take measures such as staying at home, restricting travel, avoiding entering crowded areas, switching to non-contact greetings, and maintaining a physical distance from others.
Now, many governments have made it mandatory or advised to maintain social distancing in areas affected by the outbreak.
With regard to the size of the largest population, the initial recommendation of U.S. government agencies and WHO was 250 people (in areas where there is no COVID-19 transmission), which was quickly reduced to 50, and later to 10.
On March 22, 2020, public gatherings of more than two people were banned in Germany. Older people and groups living with underlying diseases such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory disease, high blood pressure, and compromised immune system had a higher risk of severe illness and complications. The German Centers for Disease Control (CDC) recommended that such people living in the affected areas do not go out as far as possible. In late March 2020, the World Health Organization and other health agencies began to replace the term "social distancing" with "maintaining physical distance" in order to clarifies that its purpose is to reduce physical contact while still maintaining social connections, including virtual connections.
The use of the term "social distancing" implies a requirement that people be completely socially segregated and does not encourage people to use other ways to maintain human-to-human connection. Some authorities have also issued sexual health guidelines applicable during the pandemic.
Suggestions include having sex only with a partner who is not infected with the virus or asymptomatic.
CoVID-19-confirmed patients and suspected infected people are advised to self-isolate at home.
Health agencies have issued detailed instructions on correct self-isolation. Many governments have mandated or recommended self-isolation of all residents in affected areas.
Strictest self-segregation instructions have also been issued to high-risk groups.
People who may have been exposed to COVID-19, or have recently visited a wide-ranging country or region, will be advised to self-isolate for 14 days from the last possible exposure to the virus.
Epidemic prevention and control strategies include containment/suppression and mitigation.
Containment is applicable in the early stages of an outbreak and is designed to track and isolate infected people, along with other infection prevention and control and vaccination measures to stop the spread of the disease to other populations.
If the spread of the disease can no longer be stopped, efforts must be made to transition to the mitigation stage: measures are taken to slow the spread of the disease and reduce its impact on the health system and society.
Mitigation and mitigation measures can be implemented at the same time.
Inhibition requires more extreme prevention and control measures to reverse the pandemic by reducing the number of basic regenerations to less than 1. Part of the effort to control infectious disease outbreaks is to try to reduce the peak of the epidemic, which is what we are known as the smoothing of the epidemiological curve.
This can reduce the risk of a collapse of the health service system and allow more time for the development of vaccines and treatment programs.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that can contain the outbreak include personal preventive measures, such as maintaining hand hygiene, wearing masks, and self-isolation; community-based measures aimed at maintaining physical distancing, such as stopping school and canceling large-scale gatherings; community-based actions aimed at encouraging the acceptance and participation of such interventions; and environmental measures such as surface cleaning.
Other countries and regions have also taken various measures to limit the spread of the virus.
South Korea has introduced large-scale screening and local isolation measures and issued an alert on the activity trajectories of infected people.
Singapore provides financial support for self-isolating infected persons, imposing hefty fines on those who refuse to self-quarantine.
Taiwan has increased the production of face masks, imposing penalties on suppliers of medical supplies that are sold. Simulations in the United Kingdom and the United States show major challenges in mitigating (mitiating but not preventing the spread of the epidemic) and restraining (reversing the spread of the epidemic).
Optimal mitigation policies are likely to reduce peak healthcare needs by a two-thirds and halving the number of deaths, but it will still lead to hundreds of thousands of deaths, causing health systems to collapse.
Repression may be the preferred measure, but it needs to be maintained until the spread of the virus stops in the population (or until the vaccine is rolled out, whichever comes first), otherwise the propagation will rebound rapidly once the measures are relaxed.
Long-term interventions aimed at curbing pandemics come with social and economic costs.
There are currently no specific antivirals approved for COVID-19, but development is ongoing, including trials of existing drugs.
Taking over-the-counter cold medicines, rehydration fluids, and breaks may help relieve the symptoms.
Depending on the severity of the condition, oxygen therapy, intravenous fluids and respiratory support may be required.
Using steroids can make the situation worse.
A number of compounds previously approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are currently being studied to determine whether they are suitable for the treatment of COVID-19.
The World Health Organization has also noted that some “traditional and family therapies” can relieve symptoms caused by SARS-CoV-19.
According to the World Health Organization, improving processing capacity and adjusting medical systems to meet the needs of COVID-19 patients is a basic outbreak response.
The ECDC and World Health Organization Regional Office for Europe have published guidelines for hospital and primary health care services to transfer resources at multiple levels, including focusing laboratory services on COVID-19 testing, removing selective procedures as much as possible, segregating and isolating COVID-19-positive patients, and improving intensive care capabilities by training personnel and increasing the number of ventilators and beds available.
Controversies about the origin of the first case (the so-called patient zero) vary widely.
At present, the first known case of novel coronavirus pneumonia may date back to December 1, 2019, located in Wuhan, Hubei, China.
Within a month, the number of new coronavirus cases in Hubei Province gradually increased.
Most of these patients are related to the South China Seafood Wholesale Market, which also sells live animals, a theory that the new coronavirus originated from one of the animals; or in other words, it originated from zoonotic diseases. On December 26, Zhang, a doctor at Hubei Provincial Chinese and Western Medical Hospital, was the first to find and receive a number of pneumonia patients of unexplained pneumonia, and was reported to the Wuhan Jianghan District Disease Prevention and Control Center on December 27.
On December 30, a group of doctors at Wuhan Central Hospital alerted their colleagues against an “SARS-like coronavirus.”
Eight doctors, including Li Wenliang, were reprimanded by the police for spreading "rumours", and Ivan, another doctor, was admonished by his superior for sounding the alarm.
The Wuhan Municipal Health and Health Commission subsequently issued an announcement on December 31 and reported it to the World Health Organization.
An investigation was launched in early January following numerous reports of unexplained pneumococcal pneumonia cases received by the Wuhan health department. In the early stages of the outbreak, the number of cases roughly doubled every seven-and-a-half days.
From the beginning of January to mid-January 2020, the virus spread to other provinces in China, affected by the large population movement of the Chinese New Year and Wuhan as a transportation hub and a major railway hub.
China reported nearly 140 new confirmed cases in a single day, including two in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
As of January 20, 2020, 6,174 people had developed symptoms. As of March 26, the U.S. surpassed China and Italy to become the country with the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, there were more than 1.61 million confirmed cases worldwide; more than 97,000 deaths and more than 364,000 cures.
Confirmed cases have been reported in about 200 countries and territories around the world.
Affected by the epidemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen area have adopted prevention and control measures to restrict the free movement of people and establish border controls.
Measures at the national level include containment, such as quarantines (also known as home orders, in-place asylum orders, or lockdowns) and curfews. As of April 2, nearly 300 million people in the United States, or about 90 per cent of the total population, were in some form of lockdown, with more than 50 million people in the Philippines, about 59 million in lockdowns in South Africa and 1.3 billion in India.
On March 26, 1.7 billion of the world’s population were under some form of lockdown, and two days later that number rose to 2.6 billion, accounting for about one-third of the world’s population.
The first COVID-19-confirmed case dates back to December 1, 2019, in Wuhan; an unconfirmed report speculates that the earliest case dates back to November 17.
On December 26, Dr. Zhang found a number of unexplained cases of Pneumonia, which was reported to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention in Jianghan District of Wuhan on December 27.
On December 27, 2019, a set of SARS-like coronaviruses was detected in preliminary genetic testing of patient samples.
The Wuhan Municipal Health and Health Commission issued an announcement on December 31.
It is reported to the World Health Organization on the same day.
After these notices were issued, several doctors in Wuhan were reprimanded by the "rumors" of walking the epidemic.
China’s National Health Commission initially claimed that there was no “unspecified evidence” of human-to-human transmission.
In late January, the Chinese government launched a radical movement, which was later described as a “people’s war” to curb the spread of the virus.
On January 23, the government announced that it was lifting an anti-epidemic line in Wuhan to prohibit people from entering and exiting Wuhan, which was later extended to 15 other cities in Hubei, affecting a total of about 57 million people, which was called "the largest isolation event in human history."
It is forbidden to use private cars in the city.
In many places, the Chinese Lunar New Year (January 25) celebrations have also been cancelled.
The government department also announced the construction of a makeshift hospital, Vulcan Mountain Hospital, which will be completed and put into service within 10 days.
Subsequently, another hospital was built, Raytheon Hospital, to be used to receive more patients.
In addition to building new hospitals, China has transformed 14 other places in Wuhan, such as the Convention Center and the Stadium, into a cabin hospital. On January 26, the government took further steps to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, including issuing health declaration forms to travelers and extending the Spring Festival holiday.
National universities and primary and secondary schools have also been completely suspended.
A number of measures have also been taken in Hong Kong and Macau, particularly for primary and secondary schools and universities.
Many places in China have implemented remote home-to-work measures.
In addition to Hubei Province, travel restrictions have also been imposed on other identities.
Public transportation has been restructured, and museums from all parts of China are temporarily closed.
It is estimated that about 760 million people (more than half of the population) are subject to some form of restrictions on outdoor activities. After the epidemic entered the global stage in March, the Chinese authorities took strict measures to prevent the virus from "importing" from other countries and regions.
For example, Beijing imposes a 14-day mandatory quartet for entry into Beijing. In the five days prior to March 23, there was only one local case in mainland China, which was transmitted by a traveler returning to Guangzhou from Istanbul.
On March 24, 2020, Chinese Premier Li Keqiang reported that the spread of local cases has been basically curbed, and the epidemic in China has been controlled.
On the same day, travel restrictions were liberalized after a full two-month lockdown in Hubei regions other than Wuhan. The Chinese Foreign Ministry announced on March 26, 2020 that it had suspended the entry of foreigners with valid Chinese visas and residence permits since March 28, and did not disclose the relevant details about when the policy would end.
People who want to enter China need to apply for a visa at the Chinese embassy or consulate.
The Chinese government encouraged enterprises and factories to resume work on March 30 and provided a monetary stimulus package for businesses. The State Council declared April 4 as a day of national mourning, and a three-minute national mourning campaign was launched at 10:00 on that day. The day coincided with the Qingming Day, in which the central government advocated for families to jointly pay tribute to deceased loved ones through the Internet in order to maintain physical distance and avoid the second COVID-19 outbreak.
On January 20, 2020, there were confirmed cases of COVID-19 in South Korea.
South Korea’s health agency reported a significant increase in confirmed cases on Feb. 20, mainly due to a new religious movement rally in Daegu, South Korea, the Church of Jesus in the new world.
Xintiandi believers who traveled from Wuhan to Daegu are suspected of being the source of the outbreak.
As of Feb. 22, 1,261, or about 13%, out of 9,336 churchgoers, had reported symptoms. South Korea was declared at the highest alert level on February 23, 2020.
On February 28, South Korea reported more than 2,000 confirmed cases, rising to 3,150 on February 29.
Thirty-three soldiers tested positive for the virus, and all South Korean military bases have been quarantined since then.
The flight schedule has also been adjusted due to the impact of the epidemic. South Korea has launched what is considered to be the world's largest and best-organized virus prevention and control plan, that is, virus screening of a large number of people, isolating all infected people, and tracking and estuaries of close contacts of infected people.
Screening methods include manditiating new arrivals from abroad to self-report symptoms through a mobile app, getting off-of-virus testing and getting results the next day, as well as improving detection capabilities and accommodating up to 20,000 people per day.
Although the entire city was not quarantined, South Korea's plan was seen as a sluggish measure. South Korean society's initial response to President Moon's crisis was polarized.
A number of South Koreans have signed the petition, with some calling for impeachment, accusing the government of mishandling the outbreak, while others praising his response strategy.
On March 23, it was reported that South Korea had the lowest single-day total number of new cases in the past four weeks.
On March 29, it was reported that from April 1, all newly arrived overseas personnel will be quarantined for two weeks.
According to media reports on April 1, South Korea has received requests for virus testing assistance from 121 different countries and regions.
On February 19, Iran announced the first confirmed cases of SARS-CoV-2 in Qom, and according to the Iranian Ministry of Health, two patients had died on the same day.
Early measures announced by the government include the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events, sporting events and Friday prayers, and the complete suspension of universities, higher education institutions and primary and secondary schools.
Iran allocates five trillion riyals to the fight against the virus.
On February 26, 2020, Iranian President Hassan Rouhani said that there are no plans to block the areas affected by the epidemic, but will only isolate infected individuals.
In March, Iran announced plans aimed at limiting intercity travel, but inter-city congestion continued into the Persian New Year.
Shiite shrines in the Qom region remained open to pilgrims until March 16, 2020. In February, Iran became the epicentre of the spread of the virus after China.
Many people believe that the extent of the outbreak in Iran has been covered up, including that as of February 28, the retrospective cases of more than a dozen countries pointed to Iran, thus indicating that the actual epidemic in Iran is more serious than the 388 cases reported by the government as of that date.
Iran’s parliament was shut down, and 23 of its 290 MPs reportedly tested positive for the virus on March 3.
On March 12, Human Rights Watch urged Iranian prisons to unconditionally release human rights defenders detained for peacefully expressing their objections, as well as the temporary release of all eligible prisoners.
The group noted that the spread of the virus in closed institutions such as detention centers is at greater risk and that these institutions lack adequate health care measures.
On March 15, Iran’s government reported 100 deaths in a single day, the country’s highest single-day death toll since the outbreak began.
As of March 17, at least a dozen current or former Iranian politicians and government officials had died of the disease.
As of March 23, there have been 50 new confirmed cases per hour in Iran, with one new death due to coronavirus every 10 minutes.
According to World Health Organization officials, the actual number of cases in Iran is likely to be five times higher.
It is also believed that the U.S. sanctions on Iran may affect the country's financial ability to deal with the epidemic.
The United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights has called for the lifting of economic sanctions on the countries most affected by the pandemic, including Iran.
On January 31, two Chinese tourists tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 in Rome, and the outbreak was confirmed to spread to Italy.
Subsequently, the number of cases in the country has risen sharply, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights between China and declare a state of emergency.
Beginning with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on February 21, an unlinked batch of COVID-19 cases was later discovered. On February 22, a cabinet meeting announced a new decree to curb the spread of the epidemic, including the isolation of more than 50,000 people in 11 different cities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte announced: "It is strictly forbidden to enter and leave the affected areas."
The affected areas have been ordered to shut down work and suspend sporting events.” On March 4, the Italian government ordered schools at all levels across the country to be completely closed due to 100 deaths in the country.
All major sporting events, including the Italian Primera División, will be held without spectators until April, but on March 9, all sporting events have been completely suspended for at least a month.
On 11 March, Prime Minister Conte decreed the cessation of almost all commercial activities except supermarkets and pharmacies. On 6 March, the Italian Society of Anesthetic analgesics (SIAARTI) published medical ethical recommendations on possible triage programmes.
On March 19, Italy reported 3,405 deaths from the pandemic, surpassing China to become the country with the highest number of new coronavirus deaths in the world.
On March 22, it was reported that Russia had dispatched nine military aircraft carrying medical equipment to Italy.
As of April 5, there have been 128,948 confirmed cases, 15,887 deaths and 21,815 curative cases in Italy, most of which have occurred in Lombardy.
CNN has reported that Italy's huge elderly population and so far unable to detect all people infected with the virus may be part of the cause of the high death rate.
The UK's initial response to the virus is believed to be one of the laxest in affected countries. It wasn't until March 18, 2020, that the U.K. government introduced various forms of social distancing or large-scale quarantine for its citizens.
On 16 March, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson issued a statement recommending the elimination of any non-necessary travel and social contact, advising people to work from home as much as possible and avoiding places such as bars, restaurants and theatres.
On March 20, the government announced that all leisure places, including bars and gymnasiums, should be closed as soon as possible, with a maximum wage of up to 80% of workers' wages and a monthly ceiling of 2,500 pounds, to prevent job losses caused by the crisis. On March 23, the British prime minister announced more stringent social distancing measures to prohibit gatherings of more than two people, and no travel and outdoor activities unless absolutely necessary.
Different from the previous measures, the announced restrictions are enforced by the police through fines and dissipation of assemblies.
In addition to supermarkets, pharmacies, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, gas stations and garages that have been identified as "essential", most of the other businesses have been suspended.
The first confirmed case of COVID-19 in the U.S. was discovered on Jan. 20 in the U.S. state of Washington, and the patient returned from Wuhan on Jan. 15.
On January 29, the White House established the Coronavirus Task Force.
On January 31, the Trump administration declared a public health emergency and imposed immigration restrictions on travelers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (the U.S. government's main public health agency) announced that it has developed its own test kits.
Despite this, the U.S. started slowly in terms of testing, masking the actual situation of the epidemic at that time.
There was a defect in the federal government's test kits produced in February until the end of February, when the federal government approved the use of non-governmental kits (from academia, companies, and hospitals), and it was not until the beginning of March to determine the test eligibility restriction standard (after which medical advice is required), all of which led to the obstruction of pre-tests in the United States.
On Feb. 27, the Washington Post reported that fewer than 4,000 U.S. tests had been carried out.
On March 13, The Atlantic reported that the total number of tests performed in the United States was less than 14,000.
On March 22, the Associated Press reported that "a large number of patients with symptoms and medical orders waited for hours or days to get tested." On February 29, Washington State reported its first U.S. death, Governor Jay Yingsley declared a state of emergency, and other states soon followed.
On March 3, Seattle-area schools were closed. In the middle of March, schools at all levels across the country were fully closed. On March 6, 2020, a number of epidemiologists at Imperial College in London made predictions about the impact of the new coronavirus in the United States.
On the same day, President Donald Trump signed the Coronavirus Prevention and Response Complementary Appropriations Act, which provides $8.3 billion in emergency funding for federal agencies to respond to the outbreak.
Enterprises impose travel restrictions on employees, cancel meetings, and encourage employees to work from home.
On March 11, Trump announced that he would impose a 30-day travel ban on most European countries, excluding the United Kingdom, from March 13.
The next day, Trump expanded the restrictions to include the United Kingdom and Ireland.
On March 13, Trump declared a national emergency and began to use federal funds to deal with the epidemic crisis.
Since March 15, many companies across the U.S. have shut down or shortened their business hours in an effort to ease the spread of the virus.
As of March 17, confirmed cases have been reported in all 50 U.S. states and the U.S. District of Columbia. On March 23, it was reported that the number of confirmed cases of the novel coronavirus in New York City reached 10,700, surpassing the total number of cases in South Korea.
On March 25, New York’s governor said social dissociation appears to have begun to play a role, extending the case-by-scaling interval estimate from 2.0 days to 4.7 days.
As of March 28, there have been 32,308 confirmed cases in New York City and 672 deaths due to the virus. On March 26, it was reported that the number of confirmed novel coronavirus infections in the United States exceeded that of any other country, including China and Italy. As of April 8, the cumulative number of confirmed cases in the United States reached 400,335 and 12,841 deaths.
According to media reports on March 30th, U.S. President Trump decided to extend the implementation of social distancing guidelines until April 30.
On the same day, about the 1,000-bed Consolation medical ship was anchored in New York.
On April 3, the U.S. added 884 new coronavirus deaths within 24 hours, the highest on record.
On April 3, the total number of cases in New York State had exceeded 100,000. The White House directed health officials and scientists to coordinate virus-related public statements and publications with Vice President Mike Pence’s office as a way to downplay the threat and control the transmission of information.
The general public's recognition of Trump's crisis prevention and control measures has been polarized by partisan differences.
Some U.S. officials and commentators have criticized U.S. dependence on imports of critical supplies such as basic medical supplies from China.
Air travel pattern analysis was used to map and predict communication patterns, and was published in the Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
According to information released by the International Air Transport Association in 2018, departures from Wuhan to Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Taipei have the highest volume of passengers.
It is also reported that Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne are also popular destinations for travelers departing from Wuhan.
According to reports, among the 20 most popular destination cities, Bali has the worst state of preparation and the best preparation status of cities in Australia. Australia has released a COVID-19 contingency plan for the new coronavirus (COVID-19) on February 7.
Knowledge of COVID-19 has not gone far enough and Australia will focus on border control and communication when it comes to dealing with the epidemic.
On March 21, Australia declared a “human biosecurity emergency.”
Due to the effective isolation of public transportation in Wuhan and other parts of Hubei, some countries and regions have planned to withdraw their citizens and diplomatic personnel living in these areas, mainly through national chartered flights and permission from the relevant Chinese authorities.
Among them, the first countries to vaccinate are Canada, the United States, Japan, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany and Thailand.
Pakistan has said it will not vaccinate from China.
On February 7, Brazil withdrew 34 Brazilians or family members, as well as four Poles, a Chinese and an Indian.
Before flying to Brazil, the Brazilian plane made a short stop in Poland, where citizens of Poland, China and India boarded the plane.
Brazilian citizens who had traveled to Wuhan were sent to a military base near Brasilia for quarantine.
On the same day, 215 Canadians (the first aircraft was withdrawn to 176 people and the second aircraft chartered by the U.S. government was withdrawn to 39 people) were withdrawn from Wuhan and sent to the Trenton Canadian military base for a two-week period of isolation.
On February 11, another plane carrying 185 Canadians took off from Wuhan and landed at the Trenton military base.
Australian authorities withdrew 277 citizens on 3 and 4 February and sent them to the Christmas Island detention center, which has been converted into a quarantine site, for a period of 14 days of isolation.
A New Zealand evacuee plane landed in Auckland on Feb. 5 and the passengers (including some from Australia and the Pacific) were sent to a naval base north of Auckland to be quarantined.
On February 15, the U.S. announced the withdrawal of the Americans aboard the Diamond Princess.
On Feb. 21, a plane carrying 129 Canadian passengers landed in Trenton, Ontario, among the passengers that had been withdrawn from the Diamond Princess cruise ship.
At the beginning of March, the Indian government began evacuating people from Iran. On March 14, the South African government chartered a South African Airways aircraft to evacuate 112 South African citizens.
A medical examination of the passengers was carried out prior to the plane's take-off, and four South Africans who showed signs of the new coronavirus were left behind to reduce the risk of transmission.
Only South Africans with a negative test were withdrawn.
All South Africans, including aircrews, pilots, hotel staff, police officers and soldiers involved in the humanitarian mission, tested negative, and as a precaution, they were sent to The Ranch Resort for 14 days of observation and isolation.
On March 20, affected by the epidemic, the United States began to withdraw some of its troops from Iraq.
On February 5, China's Foreign Ministry said 21 countries, including Belarus, Pakistan, Trinidad and Tobago, Egypt and Iran, had provided assistance to China.
According to reports, a joint team in the Greater Chicago area delivered 50,000 N95 masks to a number of hospitals in Hubei Province on January 30. The humanitarian aid organization Direct Relief, in coordination with FedEx, delivered 200,000 masks and other personal protective equipment, including gloves and protective clothing, to the Wuhan Confederate Hospital by January 30.
On February 5, the Gateses announced a $100 million donation to the World Health Organization to fund vaccine development and treatment efforts, as well as to protect “high-risk populations in Africa and South Asia.”
According to Interaksyon, after Philippine Senator Richard Gordon shipped 3.16 million masks to Wuhan, the Chinese government donated another 200,000 masks to the Philippines on February 6.
On February 19, the Singapore Red Cross announced that it would provide US$2.26 million worth of aid to China.
Japan donated 1 million masks to Wuhan, Turkey distributed medical equipment to Wuhan, Russia shipped more than 13 tons of medical supplies to Wuhan, Malaysia announced the donation of 18 million pairs of medical gloves to China, Germany sent 10,000 sets of protective clothing and other various medical supplies to China, the United States donated 17.8 tons of medical supplies to China, and promised an additional US$100 million in financial support to the affected countries and regions.
In March, China, Cuba and Russia sent experts and medical supplies to Italy to help it cope with the new coronavirus outbreak.
Entrepreneur Jack Ma sent 1.1 million kits, 6 million masks and 60,000 sets of protective clothing to Ethiopia’s capital Addis Ababa, which was distributed by the African Union.
Ma later shipped 5,000 detection tools, 100,000 masks and five ventilators to Panama.
In addition, Ma donated medical supplies to Canada. The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia and the Czech Republic have expressed concern about Chinese-made masks and kits.
For example, Spain withdrew 58,000 Chinese-made coronavirus test kits with an accuracy rate of only 30%, and the Netherlands recalled 600,000 defective Chinese masks.
Belgium has recalled 100,000 unusable masks believed to come from China but actually from Colombia.
China's aid, on the other hand, is popular in Latin America and parts of Africa. On April 2, the World Bank launched an emergency support operation for developing countries.
The World Health Organization (WHO) praised the efforts of the Chinese authorities in the prevention and control of the epidemic.
The World Health Organization (WHO) noted the contrast between the SARS epidemic in 2002-2004 and the current crisis. During the SARS epidemic, the Chinese authorities were accused of concealing the facts and hindering the prevention and containment efforts, during which the central government had begun to regularly update the situation before the Lunar New Year holiday in order to avoid panic.
On January 23, in response to the central government's decision to implement the closure measures in Wuhan, the World Health Organization's representative said that although this was "definitely not a recommendation made by the World Health Organization", it also "fully reflects the determination to curb the spread of the epidemic from the areas where the epidemic is most concentrated", calling the move "unprecedented in the history of public health". On January 30, the human-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-to-human-to-to-to-human phenomenon was confirmed outside of China and an increase in the number of cases in other countries.
World Health Organization Director-General Tedros noted that public health emergencies of international concern are determined on the basis of “the risk of global transmission, especially to low- and middle-income countries with weak health systems”.
In response to the imposition of travel restrictions, Tedros said that “there is no reason to take unnecessary measures to interfere with international travel and trade” and that “the World Trade Organization does not recommend restrictions on trade and flows.”
The World Health Organization (WHO) called on the international community to donate US$675 million to fund strategic preparedness in low-income countries, saying there is an urgent need to support countries that “do not have a supporting system to detect people infected with the virus.”
Tedros further issued a further statement, declaring that "the weakest link determines how strong we are" and urged the international community to "invest immediately, otherwise it will be more costly in the future." On February 11, the World Health Organization decided at a press conference to name the disease as COVID-19.
On the same day, Tedros said that United Nations Secretary-General António Guterres had agreed that "the United Nations will make every effort to deal with the epidemic."
To this end, the United Nations has launched a United Nations crisis management team to coordinate the United Nations-wide response. The World Health Organization (WHO) said it could "focus on the health response, while other agencies can also use their expertise to address the broader social, economic, and developmental impact of the outbreak."
On February 14, a joint mission in China, led by the World Health Organization (WHO), was launched to send international and World Health Organization (WHO) experts to China on the ground to assist in responding to local epidemics and assessing “the severity and contagiousness of the disease” by holding seminars and meetings with key state-of-the-art bodies, and to assess “the impact of provincial and county-level response activities, including different situations in urban and rural areas.”
In response to Iran's escalating outbreak, the World Health Organization sent a joint mission to the country to assess the situation. On February 28, World Health Organization (WHO) officials said that they have raised the global risk level of the coronavirus epidemic from "high" to "very high", which is also the highest warning and risk level of the assessment method.
Mike Ryan, the World Health Organization’s executive director of the Health Emergencies Program, said in a statement: “It’s a realistic examination of governments around the world: wake up.
If the virus may be approaching, please be prepared" and urge governments to take the right measures to avoid the "worst-case scenario" around the world.
Ryan went on to point out that the current numbers aren’t large enough for public health officials to declare into a global pandemic that would mean that “we basically agree that everyone on the planet may be exposed to the virus.”
On March 11, the World Health Organization announced that the new coronavirus outbreak constitutes a global pandemic.
The director-general of the World Health Organization said he was "concerned deeply about the reality of thought-provoking proliferation and severity and inaction". The World Health Organization (WHO) has been grossly criticized for its perceived mishandling of the global pandemic, including delays in declaring a public health emergency and classifying the virus as a global pandemic.
Among the more strong objections included a petition asking the World Health Organization Director-General Tedros Adhanom to submit his resignation, which was signed by 733,000 people as of April 6.
On March 26, 2020, Dozens of United Nations human rights experts stressed the need to respect the rights of everyone during the COVID-19 pandemic.
The Expert Group noted that everyone was entitled to life-saving interventions and that the Government should shoulder that responsibility.
The Expert Group emphasized that a lack of resources or health insurance should in no way justify discrimination against specific groups.
The experts stressed that everyone had the right to health, including persons with disabilities, minors, older persons, internally displaced persons, the homeless, groups living in extremely poor conditions, detainees and refugees in need of government support and other unspecified groups.
International governmental organizations are actively responding to the economic and social impact of the COVID-19 crisis.
The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) has launched a platform designed to provide timely and comprehensive information and advice on policy responses from countries around the world.
From policies that strengthen health systems and the world’s economy to deal with the impact of lockdowns and travel restrictions, a national policy-tracking system is set up to help countries learn from each other and contribute to a concerted global effort to address the challenges of the new coronavirus.
Due to the initial outbreak of the Pandemic in China's Hubei Province, the U.S., British Cabinet Office Secretary Michael Gove, and Eduardo Bolsonaro, son of Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro, criticized the Chinese government's handling.
Several CCP (CPC) provincial-level administrative officials were removed from office for mishandling anti-epidemic efforts in central China, reflecting the central government's dissatisfaction with local political institutions.
Some commentators believe that the move is intended to avoid the public's displeasure with General Secretary of the Communist Party of the Communist Party of China (CCP Central Committee) Xi Jinping
Some Chinese officials (such as Zhao Lijian) have denied that the previously admitted COVID-19 outbreak originated in Wuhan and supported conspiracy theories that the new coronavirus pneumonia originated in the United States or Italy.
U.S. President Donald Trump once referred to the new coronavirus as a “Chinese virus” or “Wuhan virus,” saying that China’s “censorship system has exacerbated the spread of the virus, making it now a global pandemic,” which in turn has been described by some critics as a form of racial discrimination and has “rebudised attention from its government’s failure to contain the virus.”
The Daily Beast obtained a U.S. government telegram outlining a communications strategy apparently issued by the National Security Council that said “none of this is at odds with China.”
We were told in any way, including press conferences and television appearances, to spread the message as much as possible.” Media outlets such as Politico, Foreign Policy and Bloomberg claimed that China’s assistance to the affected countries was intended to promote their global influence.
Josep Borrell, the EU’s head of foreign policy, warned that there was a “geopolitic element that competed for influence” through brokering and “generous politics”.
Borrel also said that “China is making great efforts to publicize that, unlike the United States, it is a responsible and reliable partner.”
China has also called on the U.S. to lift sanctions on Syria, Venezuela and Iran, and is said to have provided assistance to the latter two countries.
On April 3, Ma Yun donated 100,000 masks to Cuba were blocked by U.S. sanctions.
U.S. authorities are also accused of transferring aid to other countries.
According to reports, there is also a mask dispute between Germany, Austria and Switzerland, as well as between the Czech Republic and Italy.
In addition, Turkey intercepted hundreds of ventilators destined for Spain.
At the beginning of March, the Italian government criticized the European Union's insufficient support for Italy affected by the new coronavirus.
Maurizio Massari, Italy's ambassador to the EU, said: "Only China responded bilaterally."
Of course, this is not a good sign of European solidarity.”
On March 22, Russian President Vladimir Putin, after a phone call with Italian Prime Minister Conte, arranged for the Russian army to send military doctors, special disinfection vehicles and other medical equipment to Italy.
An anonymous “top political official” was quoted as saying that 80 percent of Russia’s aid was “virtually useless to Italy.”
The source accused Russia of “geopolitics and diplomacy.”
Attilio Fontana and Italy’s Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio rejected media reports and expressed their gratitude.
Russia has also sent the U.S. a cargo plane carrying medical aid.
Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said: “[Putin] in providing assistance to his U.S. colleagues, believes that once U.S. medical device and material manufacturers resume production capacity, they will pay back if necessary.”
The NATO “Defender 2020” military exercises planned in Germany, Poland and the Baltic Sea are the largest NATO military exercises since the end of the Cold War, and the scale of the exercises will be reduced.
Kate Hudson, secretary-general of the nuclear disarmament movement, criticized the "Defender 2020" exercise: "At a time when the world is facing a public health crisis, the exercise not only endangers the lives of U.S. and many participating European militaries, as well as the residents of the countries where the exercise is located." The Iranian government has been severely affected by the epidemic, with about 24 parliamentarians and 15 other current or former politicians infected.
On March 14, 2020, Iranian President Hassan Rouhani wrote an open letter to world leaders asking for assistance. The letter pointed out that U.S. sanctions on Iran have made it impossible for Iran to access the international market, making it difficult to fight the epidemic. The outbreak has prompted calls on the United States to adopt social policies that are common to other rich countries, including universal health care, universal child health care, paid parental leave, and improved public health funding.
Political analysts expect that the outbreak could have a negative impact on Donald Trump's re-election in the 2020 presidential election. Diplomatic relations between Japan and South Korea have also deteriorated due to the global pandemic.
Japan announced that everyone entering from South Korea was subject to a two-week quarantine at a government-designated location, and South Korea later criticized Japan's "violation measures are vague and passive."
South Korean society initially showed a polarized approach to President Moon’s crisis response.
A number of South Koreans have signed the petition, some calling for the impeachment of Moon, accusing the government of mishandling the outbreak, while others praising his response. The pandemic has prompted many countries to pass emergency legislation in response.
Some commentators are concerned that this could strengthen the government’s control over power.
In Hungary, the parliament voted in favour of Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to govern indefinitely by decree suspending parliament and elections, which can also be punished if they are found to have spread false information about the virus and the government’s crisis management.
The multi-faceted supply shortage can be attributed to the new coronavirus outbreak, which stems from the increased use of equipment around the world to combat the outbreak, panic shopping, and the disruption of factory and logistics operations.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has issued warnings about shortages of drugs and medical equipment due to increased consumer demand and supply disruptions.
In many places, there have been panic shopping phenomena, resulting in the emptiness of necessities such as food, toilet paper and bottled water on the shelves, resulting in a shortage of supply.
In particular, the technology industry has been warning of delays in the delivery of electronic products.
The Director-General of the World Health Organization said that the public's demand for personal protective equipment has increased by 100 times.
This demand has caused the price of such products to rise 20 times higher than usual, and the supply of medical supplies has been delayed by four to six months.
It has also led to a worldwide shortage of personal protective equipment. The World Health Organization (WHO) has warned that it poses a threat to healthcare workers.
In Australia, the Pandemic has provided new opportunities for purchasers to sell Australian products to China.
This activity led to a shortage of infant formula in some supermarkets, which was banned by the Australian government. Although COVID-19 in northern Italy and Wuhan were very serious, there were no serious food shortages in the two places.
Measures taken by China and Italy in response to the hoarding and illegal trade of key products have been very successful, avoiding severe food shortages, which are expected in Europe and North America.
Agricultural production in northern Italy has not fallen significantly, but industry representatives believe prices are likely to rise.
The emptiness of food shelves is only a temporary occurrence, even in Wuhan, where Chinese government officials also released pork reserves to ensure adequate nutrition for the residents.
There is a similar law in Italy that requires food manufacturers to reserve supplies in response to such emergencies.
The damage to the global economy has had an impact on China: a media report on March 16 said that in the first two months of 2020, the Chinese economy suffered a huge impact, and retail sales plummeted 20.5 percent in the first two months of 2020.
Since mainland China is a major global economic and manufacturing hub, the virus outbreak is seen as a major threat to global economic stability.
Agathe Demarais of The Economist Intelligence Unit predicts that the market will remain unstable until there is a clearer picture of the possible consequences.
In January 2020, a number of analysts estimated that the negative impact of the outbreak on global economic growth may exceed the SARS outbreak in 2002-2004.
An expert at the University of Washington in St. Louis estimated that the impact of the outbreak on the global supply chain would amount to more than $300 billion, possibly for as long as two years.
According to reports, due to the decline in Chinese demand, the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) is in a "difficult situation" after a sharp drop in oil prices.
On February 24, there was a significant increase in COVID-19 cases outside mainland China, leading to a decline in global stock markets.
On February 27, due to rising public concerns about the new coronavirus epidemic, various U.S. stock indexes, including the Nasdaq 100 index, the S&P 500 index, and the Dow Jones Industrial Average, all showed the largest decline since 2008, and the Dow fell 1,191 points, the largest single-day decline since the 2007-08 financial crisis.
Each of the three major indexes fell by more than 10 percent during the week.
Scope Ratings GmbH confirmed China’s sovereign credit rating, but maintained a negative outlook.
People's concerns about the new coronavirus caused the stock market to plummet again, and the biggest decline occurred on March 16.
Many people believe that the recession is a high-probability event.
Economist Mohamed El-Erian lauded central banks and states for taking timely and urgent measures.
Compared with the 2008 financial crisis, the central bank responded even faster.
Affected by the travel ban, the closure of public places such as tourist attractions, as well as government recommendations to avoid travel, the tourism industry has become one of the hardest-hit industries.
Affected by this, many airlines cancelled flights due to the decline in demand, including British Airways, China Eastern Airlines and Qantas, while the British regional airline Flybe unfortunately collapsed.
The impact on the cruise industry is even more unprecedented.
A number of railway stations and ferry ports have also been forced to close.
The epidemic coincides with the Spring Festival, due to the Chinese Spring Festival holiday, which was originally an important tourist season.
The national and local governments canceled a series of events involving crowd gatherings, including the annual Spring Festival, and private companies closed their own shops and tourist attractions, such as Hong Kong Disneyland and Shanghai Disneyland.
A number of Lunar New Year activities and tourist attractions, including the Forbidden City and traditional temple fairs in Beijing, have been cancelled and closed to prevent large-scale crowd gatherings.
Twenty-four of the country’s 31 provinces, municipalities and autonomous regions have extended their New Year’s holidays to Feb. 10, requiring most companies not to return to work by that date.
The gross domestic product (GDP) in these regions accounts for 80% of the country's gross domestic product (GDP) and 90 per cent of exports
In Hong Kong, Hong Kong has raised its infectious disease response to the highest level, declared a state of emergency, closed schools until March, and New Year's celebrations were canceled. The retail industry has been affected globally, and store hours have been shortened or temporarily closed.
Retail store visits in Europe and Latin America fell by 40 per cent.
The number of retail stores in North America and the Middle East decreased by 50-60%.
In addition, the epidemic also led to a 33-43% drop in traffic in shopping malls in March compared to February.
Shopping mall operators around the world have taken additional measures, such as increasing sanitation facilities, installing thermal scanners to measure shoppers' body temperature, and cancelling some activities. According to estimates by the United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America, pandemic-fueled recessions may cause Latin America's extremely poor to have between 14 million and 22 million more people than in the absence of a pandemic.
In January and February 2020, at the peak of the epidemic in Wuhan, about 5 million people in China lost their jobs.
There are nearly 300 million migrant workers in China, many of whom are stranded in the inland provinces or Hubei provinces. In March 2020, more than 10 million Americans lost their jobs and applied for government assistance.
According to estimates from the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, the new coronavirus outbreak could result in 47 million U.S. job losses and an unemployment rate of 32%. India's lockdown has resulted in tens of millions (daily-salaried) Indian migrant workers losing their jobs. The Angus Reed Institute survey found that 44% of Canadian households have experienced some form of unemployment.
In the second half of March, 4 million French workers applied for temporary unemployment benefits, and 1 million British workers applied for general benefits. Nearly half a million companies in Germany adopted a government-subsidized, government-subsidized time-lapse working-time.
France and the United Kingdom have also adopted Germany's time-shrinking work compensation system.
The performing arts and cultural industries have also been decimated by the epidemic, and organizations around the world, as well as employed employees and individuals, have not been spared.
The arts and cultural industry organization strives to stick to its mission (usually funded by the government), to showcase cultural heritage to the community, to maintain the safety of its employees and the general public, and to support the artists as much as possible.
By March 2020, museums, libraries, performing arts venues and other cultural institutions around the world have been closed indefinitely to varying degrees, and scheduled exhibitions, events and performances have been forced to be cancelled or postponed.
In order to cope with this situation, the parties began to vigorously provide alternative services through digital platforms. The epidemic has recently led to many religious services, major sports events and other social activities (such as music festivals, concerts, technical seminars, and fashion performances) being cancelled, and this negative impact will rapidly increase.
The film industry has also taken a hit. The Vatican announced that it was canceling Holy Week, which will be commemorated in Rome during the last week of Christian Lent.
Many dioceses advise older Christians to stay at home and do not have to attend Mass on Sundays; some churches hold liturgies by radio, online or on television, while others allow in-car worship.
Christian pilgrims were not seen in St. Peter's Square due to the closure of Roman Catholic boroughs, and other religious groups cancelled or limited public gatherings in churches, mosques, synagogues, shrines, shrines, and shrines.
Iran’s Ministry of Health announced the cancellation of Friday’s prayers in areas affected by the outbreak, which was later forced to close, while Saudi Arabia barred foreign pilgrims and their residents from the holy sites of Mecca and Medina.
The pandemic wreaked havoc on the world’s sports agenda since World War II.
The majority of major sporting events have been canceled or postponed, including the 2019-20 UEFA Champions League, the 2019-20 Premier League, the 2020 Champions League, the 2019-20 NBA season, and the 2019-20 National Hockey Federation season.
The outburst disrupted plans for the 2020 Summer Olympics, originally scheduled to be held at the end of July; the International Olympic Committee announced on March 24 that the event would be “rearranged beyond 2020, but no later than the summer of 2021.” Casinos and other gaming venues around the world have been closed, and live poker tournaments have been postponed or cancelled.
As a result, many gamblers switched to online Gambling, and a number of online gambling websites reported a significant increase in new registration rates. The entertainment industry was also affected, with various music groups suspending or canceling tours.
A number of large theaters, such as Broadway theaters, also suspended all performances.
Some entertainers have also managed to continue to create and share their work over the Internet as an alternative to traditional live performances, such as live-streamed concerts, or the creation of an online “Festival” for artists to perform, distribute, and promote their work.
On the Internet, a large number of online memes with the theme of the new coronavirus have spread, and at a time when uncertainty is spreading, many people have begun to respond with a sense of humor and diversion.
Since COVID-19 broke out, there has been an increase in prejudice, xenophobia, and racial discrimination against Chinese, East Asians, and people from hot spots in Europe, the United States and other countries.
Many countries and regions, especially in Europe, East Asia, North America and the Asia-Pacific region, have experienced incidents of fear, suspicion and hatred.
Many reports in February of this year (when most of the cases were still limited to China) documented the racist sentiment shown by different groups around the world against the Chinese, believing that the Chinese should be infected with the virus or accept so-called legitimate retaliation.
Some African countries have also experienced an increase in anti-Chinese sentiment.
According to reports, many residents of Wuhan and Hubei have been subjected to geographical discrimination.
Whether offline or offline, there are voices supporting the Chinese people and the residents of the severely affected areas.
Italy became the first country in Europe with a severe outbreak of COVID-19 as the epidemic spreads to other hotspots, and people from the country may also be affected by suspicion and xenophobia. Some citizens in Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore, South Korea and other countries initially signed a petition to ban Chinese people from entering the country in an attempt to stop the spread of the disease.
In Japan, there is a hashtag #ChineseDontComeToJapan launched on Twitter to spread widely.
The Chinese, as well as other Asians in the United Kingdom and the United States, have reported increasing levels of racial abuse and attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump has been criticized for referring to the new coronavirus as a “China virus,” with critics dismissing his rhetoric as racially discriminatory and anti-China.
Protesters in Ukraine attacked a bus from Wuhan to New Sanzari, carrying Ukrainian and foreign evacuees.
According to reports, students from northeastern India (bordering China) and studying in India’s major cities have also reportedly been molested due to the novel coronavirus outbreak.
Dilip Ghosh, chairman of the BJP’s West Bengal state, said that the Chinese had destroyed nature, “so God will retaliate against them.”
These remarks were later condemned as "wrong" by the Chinese consulate in Calcutta. In China, xenophobia and racial discrimination against non-Chinese residents have also been exacerbated by the pandemic, with foreigners described as "foreign garbage" and should be the target of "garbage disposal".
A number of newspapers with paywalls have lifted restrictions on paywalls for some or all of their coverage of the new coronavirus.
Many scientific publishers have publicly published scientific papers related to the epidemic.
Other scientists chose to quickly share their findings on preprint servers such as bioRxiv.
Emerging Infectious Diseases - Infectious diseases caused by emerging pathogens, which usually have a different range of outbreaks or spread than in the past.
