Respiratory coronavirus disease (COVID-19) and related SARS-CoV-2 virus laboratory testing methods include detecting the presence of the virus and whether antibodies are produced after infection.
RT-PCR can detect the RNA of the coronavirus and thus determine the presence of the virus in the sample.
This test is a SARS-CoV-2 viral RNA test.
It can be used to determine whether you have recently been infected or if there is an active infection.
Antibody testing (serotesting) can be used for diagnostic and group monitoring.
Antibody tests showed how many people had been infected with the virus, including those with mild and unreported or asymptomatic infections.
The results of this test can determine the accurate fatality rate of the disease, as well as herd immunity levels in the population.
Due to a limited number of tests, as of March 2020, no country has been able to provide reliable data on the prevalence of the virus in its population.
As of March 23, the number of detections in no one country exceeds 3% of its population, and there are huge differences in the number of tests completed in each country/territory.
Such a discrepancy could also seriously affect the reported case fatality rate, which in some countries could be grossly overestimated.
Real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) tests using throat swabs can only achieve reliable results if they are completed within the first week after infection with the virus.
In general, the results can be obtained within a few hours to 2 days.
An RT-PCR test with a throat swab can only yield reliable results if it is completed within the first week after infection with the virus.
After that, the virus disappears in the throat, but continues to multiply in the lungs.
For infected people who are tested in the second week after infection, the sample can be substituted for a deep respiratory extract obtained through the sputum-absorbing tube, or a collapsory (spice fluid) can be used.
In January 2020, rRT-PCR was released at the Charité hospital in Berlin, one of the early PCR tests, and served as the basis for 250,000 test kits distributed by the World Health Organization (WHO).
On January 23, 2020, the United Kingdom also developed a detection method. On January 28, 2020, the South Korean company Kogenebiotech developed a clinical-grade, PCR-based SARS-CoV-2 test kit (PowerChek Coronavirus).
It looks for "E" genes common to all beta-coronaviruses, as well as SARS-CoV-2-specific RdRp genes. In China, the BGI Group was one of the first companies to be approved by the State Drug Administration of China for the production of PCR-based SARS-CoV-2 test kits. In the United States, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) distributed the 2019 Novel Coronavirus (2019-).
An early version of the test kit uses three genetic tests, one of which cannot determine the results due to reagent defects, at the same time, there are detection bottlenecks; this led to an average of less than 100 successfully processed samples per day throughout February 2020.
The two-part test method was not considered reliable until February 28, 2020, at which time the state and local laboratories were only allowed to start testing.
This test was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration under an emergency use authorization. U.S. commercial laboratories began testing in early March 2020.
LabCorp announced that RT-PCR-based COVID-19 testing will be available nationwide from March 5, 2020.
Quest Diagnostics has also announced nationwide COVID-19 testing as of March 9, 2020.
Quantitative limits have not been announced; sample collection and processing must be performed as required by the CDC.
COVID-19 testing is being developed and produced by the National Research Center for Virology and Biotechnology (VECTOR).
On February 11, 2020, it was reported that the Mayo Clinic had developed a kit to detect COVID-19 infection. On March 13, 2020, Roche Diagnostics was approved by the FDA to complete a large number of tests within 3.5 hours, allowing a single device to perform approximately 4,128 tests within 24 hours.
On March 19, 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization (EUA) to Abbott’s m2000 system for testing at Abbott; the FDA had previously issued a similar license to Hologic, LabCorp, and Thermo Fisher Scientific.
On March 21, 2020, Cepheid’s approximately 45-minute-a-minute-detection method was also authorized by the FDA.
The FDA has approved an assay that replaces PCR with a nucleic acid amplification technique.
Since this method does not require a series of warming cycles, a positive result can be obtained in only 5 minutes, and a negative result can be determined within 13 minutes.
The U.S. currently has about 18,000 such devices, and Abbott is expected to increase production in order to provide 50,000 tests per day. Taiwan is developing a test that uses monoclonal antibodies, specifically to bind to the novel coronavirus's nuclear cappella protein (N protein), in the hope that it will provide test results within 15 to 20 minutes, just like a rapid influenza test.
A March 2020 bibliographic review came to the conclusion that, in the early stages of the virus, the diagnosing value of the thoracic tablets is small, and the performance of CT [computed tomography] may be obvious, even before the onset of symptoms.”
Typical features of CT include double-pulmonary multi-leaf abrasive glass shadows with peripheral asymmetry and rear partial cloth.
With the progress of the disease, it will develop pleural subglamorous, gravel signs, and pulmonary changes.
A study comparing PCR and CT in Wuhan (the site of the initial outbreak of the current pandemic) showed that the sensitivity of CT is much higher than that of PCR, but the accuracy of CT is lower, and many of its imaging characteristics overlap with other pneumonia and diseases.
As of March 2020, the American Radiological Society recommended that "CTs should not be used to screen for COVID-19 or as a first-line testing tool for diagnosing COVID-19." Starting in March 2020, the CDC recommends the use of PCR for initial screening.
A partial immune response to an infection is the production of antibodies, including IgM and IgG.
These antibodies can be used to detect the infection of an individual about 7 days after the onset of symptoms to determine the immunity of the monitored population. The assays can be performed either in a central laboratory (CLT) or through an instant test (PoCT).
High-throughput automatic systems in many clinical laboratories can do this, but their availability depends on the productivity of the individual systems.
In the case of CLT, although the immune response can be tracked through a sequenced sample, it is usually measured using an peripheral blood sample.
In the case of POCT, a skin puncture is generally used to obtain a blood sample.
On March 26, 2020, the FDA approved the test-declared test by 29 entities that are now able to distribute their antibody test kits.
As of April 7, 2020, the FDA has approved only one test based on an emergency use authorization. In late March 2020, Oumont Medical Laboratory Diagnostics AG and Epitope Diagnostic's test kits have been approved in Europe to detect IgG and IgA antibodies against viruses in blood samples.
The detection capacity can detect hundreds of samples in a matter of hours, so it is much faster than traditional PCR assays for viral RNA.
In general, antibodies can be detected 14 days after the start of the infection. In early April, the United Kingdom found that none of the antibody test kits it purchased was good enough.
Hong Kong has set up a mechanism in which suspected patients can stay at home, "emergency rooms to provide patients with sample test tubes", patients spit saliva into the test tube, and then send the test tube back and receive the results at a later date. The British NHS has announced that it is piloting a solution to detect suspected cases at home, which eliminates the risk of patients infecting other people when they go to the hospital, and can also eliminate the risk that patients must be sterilized by an ambulance.
In Germany, the National Association of Statutory Health Insurance Physicians said on March 2 that non-hospital institutions have the capacity to perform about 12,000 tests per day, up from 10,700 in the previous week.
In the case of testing at the doctor's request, the cost will be covered by health insurance.
The director of Germany’s Robert Koch Institute said Germany’s total testing capacity is 160,000 units per week.
As of March 19, car-free testing has been provided in several major cities.
As of March 26, 2020, the total number of tests carried out in Germany is not known, because only the number of positive test results has been reported.
According to an early laboratory survey, as of the 12th week of 2020, a total of 483,295 samples (including the 12th week of 2020) had been tested, and 33,491 samples (6.9%) SARS-CoV-2 were positive. In Israel, researchers at Technion and Rambam hospitals had developed and tested a method for the simultaneous detection of 64 patient samples at the same time, that is, a pooled sample, and further tests could only be carried out if the combined samples were found to be positive.
The laboratory was built for 5 days under the supervision of BGI founder Wang Jian, and the modeling shows that without achieving the above-mentioned detection capabilities, the number of cases in Hubei will be 47% higher, and the corresponding treatment and isolation costs will be doubled.
After the Wuhan laboratory, Shenzhen, Tianjin, Beijing and Shanghai immediately set up fire-eyed laboratories, and the laboratory has been established in a total of 12 cities in China at present.
As of March 4, 2020, the daily flux of laboratory tests totaled 50,000 copies per day. Origami Assay's open-source, multi-circuit designs tested up to 1122 patient samples with only 93 measurements of COVID19. These balanced designs could operate in small laboratories without the need for automatic pipettes.
As of the end of March, a shortage of reagents and insufficient numbers has become an obstacle to large-scale testing in the European Union, the United Kingdom and the United States.
As a result, some initiators explored sample-preparation schemes that involve heating samples to 98 °C (208 °F) for 5 minutes to release the RNA genome for further testing. The United Arab Emirates announced on March 31 that a larger number of its population has now been tested for the new coronavirus in more people than in any other country, and is in the process of expanding detection levels to allow a large number of people to be tested.
The method to achieve this detection capability is to use drive-through detection on the one hand, and to build a high-throughput laboratory to deal with the needs of large-scale population testing at Group 42 and BGI, based on its “fire eye” emergency testing laboratory in China.
Built within 14 days and capable of conducting tens of thousands of RT-PCR tests every day, it is the world’s first laboratory of its size to operate outside China.
China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States have all developed different detection methods for various parts of the genetic map of the coronavirus.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has adopted Germany’s method of producing test kits for low-income countries that don’t have the resources to develop them.
Germany's method was announced on January 17, 2020, and the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention's program was available after January 28, limiting U.S. testing capabilities. Both China and the U.S. encountered problems with the reliability of test kits in the early stages of the outbreak, and these two countries and Australia were unable to provide enough test kits to meet demand and health experts.
On the contrary, experts believe that extensive testing in South Korea will be very helpful in reducing the spread of the new coronavirus.
Benefiting from the testing capacity built by the Korean government in the past few years, it is mainly concentrated in laboratories in the private sector.
On March 16, the World Health Organization (WHO) called for an increase in testing programs as the best way to slow the development of the COVID-19 epidemic. The widespread spread of the virus has led to a very large demand for testing, which has caused a backlog of hundreds of thousands of tests in private laboratories in the United States, and the supply of swabs and chemical reagents has also become strained.
In March 2020, China reported problems with the accuracy of its test kits.
In the U.S., CDC-developed test kits had "deficiencies"; subsequently, the government removed bureaucratic hurdles that prevented testing in private laboratories. Spain purchased the test kits from the Chinese company Shenzhen Yi Rui Biotechnology Co., Ltd. and found that the test results were inaccurate.
Inaccuracy in the test results may have been the result of improper collection of samples or incorrect use of test kits.
Spain's cabinet said it would recover the kit that returned the wrong results and replace it with another kit provided by Shenzhen Bioeasy. Eighty per cent of the test kits purchased by the Czech Republic from China had incorrect test results.
Prime Minister Matovič suggested that the test boxes should be thrown into the Danube. Ateş Kara of Turkey's Ministry of Health said there was a "very high error rate" that Turkey had purchased from China and did not "take them into use".
A positive effect can be achieved by testing and quaritting those who test positive and tracking the contact history of SARS-CoV-2-positive persons.
The researchers, who worked in the Italian city of Vò (the site of the first COVID-19 patient’s death in Italy), tested a total of about 3,400 people in two rounds, each about ten days apart.
About half of them tested positive and asymptomatic, and all of the detected cases have been quarantined.
By imposing travel restrictions on the community, new infections have been completely eradicated.
Through large-scale contact tracing, inbound travel restrictions, detection and quarantine, the 2020 coronavirus is transmitted much more slowly in Singapore than in other developed countries, and Singapore does not adopt extreme restrictive measures, such as forced closures of restaurants and retail venues.
A number of activities were cancelled in Singapore, and residents were advised to stay at home on March 28, but the school returned to class on March 23 after the holiday.
Several other countries, such as Ireland and South Korea, have also curbed the large-scale epidemic of disease through large-scale tracing of contacts, restrictions on inbound travel, testing and quarantine, and less aggressive lockdown measures.
According to a statistical study, countries with a higher number of detections relative to the number of deaths have lower case fatality, which may be because these countries are better able to detect people with only mild or asymptomatic symptoms.
The WHO recommends that countries that do not have testing capacity and have limited national laboratory experience with COVID-19, send their first five positive and top ten negative COVID-19 samples to one of the 16 WHO reference laboratories for confirmation testing.
Of the 16 reference laboratories, seven are in Asia, five are in Europe, two are in Africa, one is in North America and one is in Australia.
In the chart below, the “Princiquit-Positive %” column is influenced by the testing policy of the country in which it is located.
In other cases of the same conditions, the positive detection rate was higher in countries that tested only for hospitalized persons compared to countries testing all citizens, whether or not they showed signs of symptoms.
Hand washing, also known as hand cleaning, is the act of cleaning the hands to remove dust, grease, microorganisms, or other useless substances from the hands.
Insisting on hand-washing with soap at certain “critical moments” every day can prevent the spread of many diseases, such as diarrhoea and cholera, which can all be transmitted through fecal-oral routes.
If people don’t wash their hands before touching their eyes, nose, or mouth (i.e. mucous membranes), they may also become infected with respiratory diseases, such as the flu or the common cold.
There are five key moments to wash your hands with soap throughout the day: before and after defecation, after cleaning a child’s buttox or diapers, before feeding, before and after, before and after handling food or raw, fish, or poultry.
If there is no soap and water, you can clean your hands with ash. The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends washing your hands under the following conditions:
before, during, and after the preparation of the food.
before taking care of the patient.
After replacing a diaper or cleaning a child’s buttoice.
After rubbing the nose, coughing or sneezing.
After touching animals, animal food or animal droppings.
Hand hygiene refers to health habits associated with medical procedures.
Washing your hands before drug treatment or medical care can prevent or minimize the spread of the disease.
In the medical field, the main purpose of hand washing is to remove pathogens (bacteria, viruses, or other microorganisms that can cause disease) and chemicals on the hands that may harm the human body or cause the disease.
Handwashing is especially important for people who are engaged in food processing or working in the medical field, but it is also very important practice for the public.
There are a number of health benefits associated with hand washing, including minimizing the spread of influenza, coronavirus and other communicable diseases; preventing infectious diarrhoea; and reducing respiratory infections.
And to reduce the infant mortality rate at home.
A 2013 study showed that improving hand-washing habits had a slight effect on the increase in height in children under the age of five.
In developing countries, child mortality linked to respiratory and diarrhoeal diseases can be reduced by advocating for simple behavioural change (e.g., handwashing with soap).
This simple behavior can reduce the fatality rate of these diseases by nearly 50%.
Advocating for hand-washing as an intervention reduces diarrhoea events by about a third, with the same effect as providing clean water in low-income areas.
Handwashing with soap reduces diarrhoeal incidents by 48%. As a spontaneous act in homes, schools, and communities around the world, handwashing with soap is the most effective and inexpensive way to prevent diarrhoea and Acute Respiratory Infectations (ARIs).
Pneumonia, as a major acute respiratory infection, is the leading cause of death among children under the age of five, which kills about 1.8 million children every year.
Diarrhea and pneumonia kill nearly 3.5 million children every year.
More lives could save more lives than any single vaccine or medical intervention, bringing the habit of handwashing with soap before meals and toilets by a quarter compared to any single vaccine or medical intervention.
As part of the Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) program, other hygiene interventions are often accompanied by other hygiene interventions while promoting handwashing.
Hand-washing can also prevent the spread of pus through direct physical contact.
Handwashing can have a slight adverse effect, i.e. frequent hand washing can lead to dry skin, which can lead to skin damage.
A 2012 Danish study found that excessive hand-washing can lead to itching of the skin, cleavage, called hand eczema or hand dermatitis, which is particularly common among healthcare workers.
Frequent hand washing is also seen as a symptom of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD).
In order to reduce the fecal-oral transmission of the disease, there are five key moments to wash your hands with soap throughout the day: after going to the toilet (urinating, defecating), after cleaning a child’s buttomy (changing diapers), before feeding the child, before and after meals, or after handling raw meat, fish, or raw poultry.
In order to prevent the spread of the disease, the correct way should also be used to wash your hands in the following situations: before and after handling incisions or wounds; after sneezing, coughing or wiping the snot; after touching animal manure or treating animals; and after touching garbage.
In many countries, the rate of hand washing with soap is very low.
A 2015 study of hand-washing conditions in 54 countries found that an average of 38.7 per cent of households had the habit of handwashing with soap. A 2014 study showed that Saudi Arabia had the highest share, at 97 per cent; the U.S. was close to an average of 77 per cent; and China had the lowest rate at 23 per cent.
The Basic Health Care Program promoted by the Philippine Department of Education is an example of a large-scale initiative to improve children’s health and education.
At the heart of this national plan is deworming twice a year, supplemented by daily handwashing with soap and brushing with fluorinated toothpaste.
In Indonesia, the project has also been successfully implemented.
The integration of soaps or detergents into the water can better remove microorganisms from the skin.
The main function of soaps and cleaners is to reduce dissolving obstacles and improve solubleness.
Using water alone does not effectively cleanse the skin, as fats and proteins (components of organic soils) are not easily dissolved in water.
However, washing your hands requires reasonable use of water.
Since solid soaps can be reused, they may retain the bacteria that were left over from their previous use.
A small number of studies have investigated how bacteria are transferred from contaminated solid soap and concluded that the bacteria cannot metastasize because the bacterium is washed away by the foam.
However, the CDC still claims that “it’s best to use non-contact liquid soap.”
Antimicrobial soap has been greatly promoted by the health-conscious public.
So far, there has been no evidence that antibiotic-resistant microbes in nature have been selected using recommended antimicrobials or disinfectants.
However, antimicrobial soaps contain common antibacterial agents such as triclosan, which has a large number of drug-resistant microbial strains.
Therefore, even if antimicrobial soaps are not selected for antibiotic-resistant strains, antibacterial soaps may not be as effective as advertised.
In addition to surfactants and skin care agents, complex formulations may also contain acids (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid), antimicrobial active benzoic acid, and many other skin conditioners (paragus, vitamins, menthol, plant extracts). A comprehensive analysis conducted by the School of Public Hygiene at the University of Oregon showed that ordinary soaps have the same effect as ciclosan for preventing disease and removing bacteria on the hands.
Hot water hand washing is comfortable, but it’s not hot enough to kill the bacterium.
Bacteria develop rapidly at body temperature (37 °C).
However, when it comes to removing natural oils that contain soil and bacteria, warm soapy water is more effective than cold soapy water.
Scientific research has confirmed that the use of warm water has no effect on the reduction of microorganisms in the hands, which is contrary to the popular view.
Water-free hand sanitizer or bactericidal hand sanitizer is a non-water-based hand cleaning agent.
In the late 1990s and the beginning of the 21st century, non-water-based alcoholic hand cleaners (also known as alcohol-tipping hand fluids, bactericidal-free hand sanitizers, or hand sanitizers) were very popular.
These hand sanitizer formulations mostly contain isopropanol or ethanol, followed by the addition of kabom (acrylate polymer) and other binders to form a gel, or with moisturizers such as glycerin to form a liquid, or foamy for ease of use, and to relieve the dryness of the skin caused by alcohol.
The addition of diluted hydrogen peroxide can further increase antimicrobial activity. Hand sanitizers containing at least 60% to 95 per cent alcohol can be effectively sterilized.
Alcoastal handcuffs can kill bacteria, multidrug-resistant bacteria (Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) and anti-vancomic enterocococcus (VRE), tuberculosis and a number of viruses (including HIV, herpes, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), rhinovirus, cowpox, influenza, and hepatitis), as well as fungi.
After 30 seconds of alcohol rubbing, a 70 per cent alcohol clout can kill 99.97% of the bacteria in the hand (a reduction of 3.5 orders of magnitude, similar to a reduction of 35 decibels), and a bacterium can be reduced by 99.999% to 99.999% (4 to 5 orders of magnitude) after rubbing of the hands.
Alcohol-to-the-hand sanitizer has almost no effect on viruses such as norovirus, also known as Norovirus (Norwalk) (the most common cause of infectious gastrointestinal tractitis). It is necessary to use adequate bactericidal hand sanitizer or alcohol to thoroughly moisten or cover your hands.
Rinse the center of the hand and the back of the hand as well as between the fingers and the ends of the fingers for about 30 seconds, until the liquid, foam, or gel dries.
The fingertips must also be fully cleaned and the fingertips on two palms. The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention advises that it's best to wash your hands, rather than using hand sanitizer, especially if the hands look dirty.
Due to the fact that this type of handwashing fluid is easy to use and can quickly deactivate microorganisms, it is increasingly used; however, they should not be a substitute for proper hand washing, unless there is no soap and water to use.
Frequent alcohol-free hand sanitizer can cause dry skin, unless emollients and/or moisturizers are added to the formula.
It is possible to add glycerine and/or other emollients to the formula to reduce or eliminate dry skin caused by alcohol.
In clinical trials, compared with soaps or antibacterial cleaners, alcoholic hand sanitizer containing emollients can significantly reduce skin irritation and the occurrence of dry skin conditions.
Allergic contact with dermatitis, contact urticaria syndrome, or allergies to additives in alcohol or alcohol-rubbed hand sanitizers are rare.
Compared to handwashing with soap and water, alcoholic hand sanitizer is less likely to induce irritant contact dermatitis, which is one of the reasons for its attractiveness.
Although the no-water cleaning agent is effective, it does not remove the organic matter on the hands, but only disinfects it.
As a result, hand sanitizer is not as effective in preventing the spread of many pathogens as soap and water, because these pathogens are still on hand.
The effectiveness of non-alcoholic hand sanitizer depends to a large extent on the composition and formula, and its effect has always been much worse than that of alcohol and alcohol hand-washing.
More recently, ammonium benzal chlorination has been shown to have continuous, cumulative antimicrobial activity after use, which is different from alcohol, which has been shown to have a reduced effect after repeated use, possibly due to progressive adverse skin reactions.
Many people in low-income neighborhoods can’t afford soap and can be replaced with ash or soil.
Grass ash or soil is more effective than handwashing with water alone, but not as good as soap.
One of the problems that exists is that if soil or ash is tainted by microorganisms, it is likely to accelerate the spread of the disease, rather than slowing it.
Just like with soap, the ash is also a disinfectant, because it will form an alkaline solution after encountering the water.
If soap is not available, it is recommended that soap be replaced with grass ash or sand.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends proper hand-washing methods to prevent the spread of the disease are as follows:
Wet your hands with flowing warm water or cold water.
It is recommended to use flowing water because the vertical basin may be contaminated, and the water temperature does not seem to have a big relationship.
Use a sufficient amount of soap to repeatedly wash to produce foam, while washing the back of the hand, between the fingers and the nail seams.
Soap removes bacteriological bacteria from the skin, and studies have shown that people tend to wash their hands more thoroughly when using soap instead of using water alone.
Wash it for at least 20 seconds.
Blowing can create friction, which helps to remove the bacteria on the skin, and the longer the washing time, the more bacteria are removed.
It is properly cleaned with running water.
Washing your hands can cause re-contamination.
Use a clean towel to dry or dry it naturally.
Moistness in the hands is more prone to recontamination. Thumbs, wrists, areas between fingers, and nail slits are the most frequently overlooked areas.
Artificial fingernails and cracked fingernails may hide microbes.
Moisturizing moisturizing emulsions are generally recommended to avoid dry hands; dry skin can cause damage to the skin, which increases the risk of transmission of the infection.
Where tap water and/or soap are not available in developing countries, a variety of low-cost measures can be provided to facilitate hand-washing, for example, through hanging buckets or porous gourd pouring, and/or using grass and ash to wash their hands (if necessary). Water-saving hand-washing schemes, such as simple faucets and other low-cost methods, in situations where there is a limited water supply (for example, in schools or rural areas of the developing world).
The simple faucet technology is very simple, hang a water tank with a rope, and then pour a small amount of water into the hand through a foot pedal, and then use soap.
Drying your hands effectively is an important step in the hand hygiene process, but there is some debate about the most effective ways to dry your hands in the bathroom.
A growing number of studies have shown that paper towels are more hygienic than electric dry phones provided in many bathrooms.
In 2008, the University of Westminster in London conducted a study sponsored by the European Tissue Symposium in the paper towel industry, which compared paper towels, warm-air dry mobile phones and more modern jet dry mobile phones in terms of hygiene.
After washing your hands and drying your hands with a warm-breed dry mobile phone, the total number of bacteria on the fingers increased by an average of 194%, and the total number of bacteria on the palm increased by an average of 254%.
The total number of bacteria on the fingers increased by an average of 42%, and the total amount of bacteria on the palm increased by an average of 15%.
The total amount of bacteriological bacteria on the fingers was reduced by an average of 76% after washing hands, and the total number of palm bacteria decreased by an average of 77%. The scientists also carried out various tests to determine whether various dry hand methods may cause cross-infection in other bathroom users and toilet facilities.
It is said that the jet-type dry mobile phone spewing the air at 180 meters per second (650 km/h; 400 miles per hour), which is able to blown microorganisms in the hands and fuselage, which may contaminate other bathroom users and toilet facilities within 2 meters.
The use of warm-breed dry mobile phones can transmit microorganisms to places 0.25 meters other than dry mobile phones.
The results of dry hands using paper towels showed that there was no serious microbial transmission. In 2005, TÜV Produkt und Umwelt conducted a study to evaluate different methods of dry hands.
The following changes in the number of bacteriological bacteria were observed:
There are many different dry mobile phone manufacturers involved in the study, and these dry mobile phones were compared with dry hands using paper towels.
During the trip, in the absence of soap and water, you can choose to wash your hands using hand sanitizer wipes.
The alcohol content should be at least 60 per cent.
Ignaz Semmelweis, a Hungarian doctor, discovered in 1846 that handwashing was effective in preventing the spread of disease in hospitals, but it took a long time before it was mandatory for medical staff to wash their hands.
Hospitals use electronic devices that can provide feedback to alert hospital staff when they forget to wash their hands.
One study found a reduction in the rate of infection following the use of these devices.
Medical personnel should wash their hands for at least 15 seconds, using sufficient amounts of soap and water or gel to create foam and wash the various parts of their hands.
The fingers of his hands clasped to each other.
If there is residue between the nail seams, it can be removed with a hard bristles.
Since there may still be bacteriological residual moisture in the hands, it must be fully cleaned and dried with a clean towel.
After drying, paper towels should be used to close the faucet (if necessary, use paper towels to open the exit door).
This prevents recontamination of the hand as a result of contact with these surfaces.
In health care settings, the purpose of hand washing is to eliminate pathogenic microorganisms (“bacteria”) and to avoid transmission.
According to a report in the New England Journal of Medicine, in most medical settings, there are still cases where hand washing does not reach an acceptable level, and a large number of health care workers often forget to wash their hands before they come into contact with their patients, resulting in the spread of micro-organisms.
One study showed that proper hand-washing, combined with other simple procedures, could reduce catheter-related bloodstream infection rates by 66%. The World Health Organization has published a worksheet demonstrating standard methods of hand washing and hand-washing in healthcare settings.
The organization also published a draft hand hygiene guide on its website for public comments.
Whitby et al. provided a related review.
If it is necessary to verify regulatory compliance, then commercial equipment can be metered and verified against hand hygiene.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends “Five Moments” to wash your hands:
After contact with blood/body fluids
Before the sterile operation and
Adding antimicrobial chemicals to the soap ("pharmaceutical soap" or "antimicrobial soap") enables hand sanitizer to have a bactericidal effect.
Such bactericidal operations may be required prior to surgery, or in an environment with a large number of antibiotic-resistant microorganisms. When "wiping" your hands for surgery, there must be a faucet that can be opened and closed without contact, sterile towel that is used to dry your hands after washing your hands, sterile sbrushes and other sterile tools used to clean the nail seams.
Every piece of jewelry must be removed.
This procedure requires washing hands and forearms up to the elbow, which usually takes 2–6 minutes.
It doesn’t have to be washed for a long period of time (10 minutes).
When rinsing, it is necessary to prevent the water on the forearm from flowing back to the hand.
After washing the hands, use a sterile cloth to dry your hands and put on a surgical suit.
In order to reduce the spread of bacteria, it is best to wash your hands or use bactericidal hand sanitizer before and after contact with the patient.
In order to control staph infections in hospitals, it has been found that the greatest benefit of cleaning the hands comes when the frequency of washing hands reaches 20%, and the further benefits are extremely limited when the frequency of cleaning hands is increased to more than 35%.
Compared with the use of antibacterial soap, the use of ordinary soap to hand-washing causes a more than three-fold increase in the proportion of bacterial infectious diseases that can spread to the food. After comparing the use of alcohol-washed hand-washing with antibacterial soap for 30 seconds, it was found that the proportion of bacterial infections using alcohol rubbing hand sanitizer was 26% lower than that of antimicrobial soap.
However, soap and water are more effective than alcohol-rubbed hand sanitizers for reducing the H1N1 (HI1N1 influenza A) virus and Clostridium difficile spores. Hand hygiene in healthcare facilities can be improved through interventions such as training employees on how to wash their hands, increasing the availability of alcohol handcuffs, and providing written and verbal reminders to employees.
Which interventions are most effective in a variety of medical settings require further study.
In developing countries, hand-washing with soap is considered to be a cost-effective tool to achieve good health and even good nutrition.
However, the lack of reliable water supply, soap or hand-washing facilities in people’s homes, schools and workplaces poses a challenge.
For example, in most rural areas of Africa, it is very rare to have faucets for hand-washing faucets near every private bathroom or in a public toilet, even if there is a cheap way to build a hand-washing station.
However, the low frequency of hand-washing can also be caused by deep-rooted habits, rather than due to a lack of soap or water.
Advocating and championing the use of soap for handwashing can influence policy development, raise awareness of the benefits of hand-washing, and lead to long-term behavioral changes for residents.
In order to be effective, it is necessary to carry out monitoring and evaluation.
A systematic review of 70 studies found that community-based approaches were effective at increasing the rate of hand-washing in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), while social marketing campaigns became less effective. An example that advocates of hand-washing in schools is UNICEF's "three-star approach", in which, among other hygiene requirements, schools are encouraged to take simple and inexpensive measures to ensure that students wash their hands with soap.
When the minimum standard is reached, the school can go up from one star to the highest of the three stars.
Hand-wielding campaigns can include the construction of hand-washing stations to reduce disease and child mortality.
Another example of hand-washing awareness-raising activities is World Handwashing Day, which aims to try to achieve behavioural change. Due to the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, UNICEF promoted the use of a hand-washing emoji.
Very few studies have examined the overall cost-effectiveness of hand-washing and disability-adjusted life years (DALYs) in developing countries.
However, there have been reports that advocating the cost-effectiveness of hand-washing with soap is even greater compared to other water and sanitation interventions.
The first to recognize the importance of hand-washing for human health—especially for those who were physically weak (e.g., mothers or wounded soldiers who had just given birth in a hospital) were two pioneers of hand hygiene in the mid-19th century: Ignaz Semmelweis, a Hungarian doctor working in Vienna, Austria, and Florence Nightingale, the UK’s “founder of modern nursing.”
At that time, most people still thought that the infection was caused by a foul odor known as morbidity.
Food-borne disease outbreaks in the 1980’s and physogenous infections prompted the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to more actively advocate hand hygiene, emphasizing hand hygiene as an important way to prevent the spread of infection.
The outbreak of swine flu in 2009 and the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 have made many countries more aware of the importance of handwashing with soap to protect people from such infectious diseases.
In Germany, for example, there are “proper hand-washing practices” posters in public health rooms, office buildings, and washbasins in airport toilets.
The idiom of “washing your hands doesn’t work” means that someone says they don’t want to take responsibility for something or participate in something.
It derives from the contents of the biblical Gospel of Matthew, where Pontius Pilate washed his hands after his decision to crucify Jesus and later evolved into a wide-ranging idiom in some English-spewing groups.
In Shakespeare’s Macbeth, Lady Macbeth begins compulsive-washing in an attempt to wash her imaginary blemish, showing that she feels guilty about the crimes she has committed and instiating her husband’s crimes.
Studies have shown that people who remember or imagine unethical behavior tend to wash their hands more frequently than others, and tend to value handwashing facilities.
In addition, those who could wash their hands after conceivating unethical behavior are less likely to engage in other “purifying” compensatory actions, such as volunteering.
It is part of a number of hygienic and symbolic hand-washing rituals for sanitary and symbolic purposes, including Bahá'í faith, Hinduism, the tevilah and netilat yadayim cleaners in the Bahá'í Faith, the rituals of Christian handwashing, and small purity in Islam. Religions also have hygienic hand-washing regulations, especially after certain acts.
Hinduism, Judaism, and Islam require hand-washing after going to the toilet.
Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Judaism, and Islam require hand-washing before and after a meal.
COVID-19 workplace prevention
COVID-19 workplace prevention and control refers to occupational safety and health measures to prevent the use of dangerous occupational safety and health measures to prevent and control coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).
The appropriateness of workplace risk prevention and control depends on the specific work site and tasks, and should be determined on the basis of the risk assessment of the source of exposure, the severity of the disease in the community, and the risk factors of vulnerable individuals who may be exposed to COVID-19.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), low-exposure-at-risk jobs have minimal occupational contact with members of the public and other co-workers, so it is recommended to take basic infection prevention measures, including frequent hand-washing, encouraging employees to be isolated at home after illness, observing respiratory etiquette, and daily cleaning and disinfection of the work environment.
Moderate-exposure-at-risk work includes work that requires frequent or close contact with unknown or suspected COVID-19 patients who can become infected as a result of continued community transmission or international travel.
This includes staff who are exposed to the general public, such as schools, high-density working environments, and some large-scale retail environments.
In addition to basic infection precaution measures, the group should also use high-efficiency air filters and sneeze protection devices for ventilation, personal protective equipment and other dangerous prevention and control measures in contact with COVID-19 patients.
OSHA believes that there is a high risk of exposure to medical staff and morbid workers who have contact with known or suspected COVID-19 patients, and the risk of exposure becomes extremely high if the staff performs aerosol-generating operations on known or suspected COVID-19 patients or collects or processes specimens from them.
The dangerous prevention and control measures suitable for such personnel include engineering controls, such as negative pressure ventilators and personal protective equipment suitable for their working tasks.
COVID-19 outbursts can have a serious impact within the workplace.
A staff member may be absent due to illness, the need to take care of others, or the fear of exposure.
There may also be changes in the business model, including a change in the demand for goods, or a change in access to them (such as shopping during off-peak hours, or using a drop-off delivery service).
Finally, the logistics of products from areas with severe COVID-19 outbreaks can be disrupted. Infectious disease prevention and response programs can be used to guide conservation actions.
These programs address the levels of risk associated with a variety of workplaces and work tasks, including sources of exposure, risk factors from family and community settings, and risk factors for individual workers, such as older adults or groups with chronic diseases.
These plans also outline the prevention and control measures needed to deal with these risks, as well as contingency plans for situations that may arise as a result of an outbreak.
Infectious disease prevention and response plans may need to comply with national or local recommendations.
Targets in response to the outbreak include reducing employee-to-employee transmission, protecting people at higher risk of adverse health complications, maintaining business operations, and minimizing adverse impacts on other entities in the supply chain.
Respondents can also be affected by the severity of the disease in the communities in which businesses operate.
The risk prevention and control level is a widely used prevention and control framework in occupational safety and health, which is used to group the risk prevention and control measures according to the effectiveness.
If COVID-19 hazards cannot be completely eliminated, then the most effective control measures are engineering control, followed by administrative control, and finally personal protective equipment.
Engineering control refers to isolating employees from work-related hazards, rather than relying on employee behavior to control, which is also the lowest-cost solution.
Administrative control refers to changing work policies or procedures requiring workers or employers to take their own actions.
Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) has a lower prevention and control effect than engineering and administrative control, but it can help prevent some exposures.
All types of PPE need to be selected according to the level of risk faced by the staff, equipped according to the applicable conditions (such as respirators), and must always be worn correctly, regularly inspected and maintained, replaced if necessary, and properly unloaded, cleaned, stored or disposed to avoid contamination.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), low-exposure-at-risk jobs have minimal occupational exposure to the public and other colleagues.
It is recommended that all workplaces should take basic infection prevention measures, such as frequent hand-washing, advising sick employees to isolate at home, observing breathing rituals when coughing or sneezing, providing paper towels and garbage cans, preparing for telecommuting if necessary, commuting at the peak if necessary, discouraging employees from using other people's tools and equipment, and keeping the workplace clean and sterilized.
The timely identification and isolation of potentially contagious individuals is key to protecting workers, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that employees who show acute respiratory symptoms should be quarantined at home for at least 24 hours, until it is determined that there is no febrile, febrile signs, or any other symptoms, the sick leave policy should be implemented flexibly, allowing employees to take care of sick family members at home, and employees should be clearly informed of the policy.
According to OSHA regulations, moderate exposure-at-risk work includes frequent or close contact with unknown or suspected COVID-19 patients within a 6-foot (1.8-meter) range, but may cause an individual to become infected with SARS-CoV-2 as a result of continued community transmission near the workplace or due to a possible recent COVID-19 outbreak.
Engineering control measures for this and high-risk groups include the installation of high-efficiency air filters, improved ventilation, transparent plastic sneeze shields and other physical barriers, and the installation of drop-off windows to provide customer service. Administrative controls for this group and high-risk groups include encouraging home isolation of sick and high-risk groups, including the use of face-to-face meetings with virtual communications instead of face-to-face meetings, the establishment of non-necessary travel to COVID-19 outbreak sites, the use of non-necessary travel.
This type of staff should preferably be equipped with respirators.
If someone becomes ill on the plane, appropriate precautions should be taken to protect the staff and other passengers, including placing the patient six feet away from the other passenger, specifying a crew member to serve the patient, providing the patient with a mask, or requiring the patient to cover the mouth and nose with a tissue while coughing or sneezing.
Crew members should wear disposable medical gloves while caring for a sick traveler, touching body fluids, or potentially contaminant surfaces. Crew members should also wear additional personal protective equipment if they experience symptoms such as febrile, persistent coughing, or difficulty breathing.
Gloves and other disposable items should be disposed of in a biohazard bag followed by cleaning and sterilizing the contaminated surfaces. For commercial transport, including cruise ships and other passenger ships, risk prevention and control measures also include postponing travel in the event of illness.
Ideally, medical follow-up should take place in an isolated cabin. For schools and child care facilities, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends that if an infected person has been present in school, the corresponding schools and child care facilities should be closed for thorough cleaning or sterilisation, regardless of the spread of the community.
Social distancing strategies can be implemented for moderate-to-low community transmission, such as the elimination of other large agglomerations such as field visits, gatherings and sports classes, choral classes, or cafeteria meals, increasing the distance between desks, peak arrivals and departures, restricting non-essential visitor visits, and arranging separate health offices for children with flu-like symptoms.
In the case of severe community transmission, in addition to implementing social distancing strategies, an extension may also be taken into account. For law enforcement officers who carry out their day-to-day routine activities, the CDC believes that the direct health risks they face is low.
It is recommended that law enforcement officers who must be in contact with COVID-19-confirmed patients or suspected individuals should act in accordance with the same level of protection, including appropriate personal protective equipment.
In the event of close contact in the course of the arrest, the staff member should first clean and sterilize their service belts and equipment before using household cleaning sprays or rags for cleaning, and should comply with standard personal protective equipment sealing and disposal procedures and clothing sealing and cleaning procedures.
OSHA believes that certain health care and coroner workers face a high or very high risk of exposure.
High-exponential workers include health care, support, laboratory, and medical transportation workers who actually come into contact with known or suspected COVID-19 infection.
The risk of exposure becomes extremely high if a staff member conducts droplet-generating procedures for known or suspected COVID-19 patients, or collects or disposes of specimens of an infected or suspected infected person.
Droplet generation procedures include intubation, cough-inducing procedures, bronchoscope examination, some dental procedures and examinations, or body cavity specimen collection.
High-exposure risk mortuary workers include those involved in collating the bodies of the deceased, who were diagnosed or suspected cases of COVID-19 during their lifetime, and the increased risk of exposure if an autopsy is performed. Additional engineering measures for this risk group also include isolation rooms prepared for COVID-19-confirmed or suspected cases, including when performing droplet-generating procedures.
Some healthcare facilities and morgues can also be equipped with specialized negative pressure ventilation systems.
Biosafety level 3 precaution measures should be taken when treating specimens.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends demarcating different waiting areas for patients based on whether they are suspected cases of COVID-19. In addition to personal protective equipment, the Occupational Safety and Health Administration also advises respirators to wear respirators for people who are known or suspected to have been infected with SARS-CoV-2 and those who carry out droplet-generating procedures.
The U.S. is required to use NIOSH-approved N95-filtered masks or better masks in fully documented respiratory protection program environments such as health testing, training, and medical examinations.
Other types of respirators can provide better protection and improve the comfort of the staff. WHO does not recommend the use of protective clothing, because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease and is not transmitted through body fluids.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends that screening personnel at the entry point only need to wear medical surgical masks.
For workers who need to collect respiratory specimens from COVID-19 patients, as well as to provide care or transfer services to patients without having to carry out droplet-generating procedures, WHO recommends the use of medical surgical masks, goggles or masks, robes, and gloves.
In order to carry out droplet generation procedures, medical surgical masks must be replaced with N95 or FFP2 masks.
In view of the insufficient global supply of personal protective equipment, the WHO recommends reducing the demand for personal protective equipment through physical barriers such as telemedicine, transparent windows and other physical barriers such as telemedicine, transparent windows, only allowing direct caregivers to enter the rooms of COVID-19 patients, using personal protective equipment only when it is really necessary for specific tasks, continuing to use the same respirator when caring for multiple patients with the same diagnosis, monitoring and coordinating the supply chain of personal protective equipment, and discouraging the use of masks for asymptomatic personnel.
Katherine Maher
Recipients: Wikimedia Foundation
Theme: [Covid-19] lightly loaded, ready for the future
Delivery date/time: March 14, 2020 00:24 (coordinated world time)
