Over the past month, we’ve had some difficult situations.
In the face of the COVID-19 epidemic, we have clearly felt the shared destiny of all mankind, while also recognizing our responsibilities towards each other.
“We face unprecedented challenges, but we understand that the best way to deal with them requires people around the world to empathize, work together, and drive communities, which are at the heart of Wikimedia.”
Communicating fraternity and care among all our co-workers via e-mails, phone calls and chats is a testament to the remarkable differences among our colleagues and how fortunate we are to work with them.
I am very grateful and proud to be my colleagues.
Appreciation was expressed for our work last week.
They made me realize how meaningful it is to be able to use Wikipedia around the world at this time, and they also made me aware of the symbolic power that this critical resource brings to people around the world.
Regardless of whether your work is responsible for website operation, payroll or community safety, the proper operation of Wikimedia is inseparable from your hard work.
More than ever, the world needs the information provided by Wikipedia.
At this stage, the content of our work and even the mode of work will have an important impact on the world.
In view of the importance of this mission and the heavy responsibilities of our colleagues, we will make a number of important changes to the way we work together starting next week.
Modalities and schedule adjustments
As Robyn had previously mentioned, the executive team met last night to discuss the company’s way of working and schedule over the next few days and months.
At the meeting, we took into account what we believe is an appropriate response to the company’s current challenges, as well as best practices to ensure that the company remains sustainable during the epidemic.
The senior management team unanimously agreed to reduce the pressure on the company's employees, so that they can support the company's mission for a long time.
We allow you to reduce the stress of your work appropriately.
For all employees, contractors and contractors:
Our day-to-day working hours will be adjusted to 4 hours a day (or 20 hours per week) and will be notified of subsequent changes.
This adjustment is not a holiday notice. If you are able to work longer each day, we will give you a task according to your working hours.
However, given the unpredictability of the current world situation, we will prioritise taking care of your personal needs, such as taking care of your family, going out shopping or seeing a doctor.
We don’t keep track of your working hours.
If you’re sick, put down your job.
It’s self-evident, but we’d like to remind you.
You don’t have to take sick or paid leave, but you need to inform the manager and help your team revise their work calendars and schedules to make sure key areas of work aren’t affected.
(If you’ve been diagnosed with COVID-19 positivity, let’s inform Bryan of the Talent and Cultural Operations Department, which will support you and ensure that your condition is properly addressed by management.)
For hourly employees, we will pay in full.
We’ve said and recommence our renewed commitment to our contractors and hourly colleagues.
Salaries that we pay to all people will continue to be calculated on the basis of the remuneration they receive to complete their normal normal working hours.
It’s even if you’re sick or unable to work.
If you are willing to work, we will support you.
Many people choose to communicate with the outside world through work and release their own pressure.
“Our work can be incredibly rewarding, especially at a time like this.
Taking care of yourself still comes first.
We ask you to maintain communication with the manager so that we can update you and make adjustments accordingly.
Prioritize important work.
We have to maintain the normal operation of some important work.
Website reliability engineering teams, human resource operations teams, trust and security teams and fund-raising teams (and other teams) are critical and may require additional support.
We will begin to work with all departments to assess current objectives and shift our focus to supporting a number of matters that are critical to our mission.
We all have a lot of work to do, but we all need to focus our efforts on the most important projects.
Temporarily slowing does not affect future developments.
We don't plan to "overwhelm" after the epidemic.
We won’t ask you to work overtime to catch up, because it seems impractical right now.
We accept that the current environment has changed and we will set new goals and timetables as appropriate.
What will happen if the app (Annual Plan) changes?
In order to adapt to the new realistic situation and daily working time requirements, we plan to adjust the delivery schedule for the 2020-2021 program.
“We hope to propose an extension of the 2019-2020 program in exchange for more time to develop a budget that will allow our employees to prioritize critical tasks and take care of themselves and their families, as well as adjust their work arrangements for employees who need or want to reduce their workload in the next few weeks.”
Extending the schedule can significantly reduce the current planned workload of the entire organization and reduce work pressure.
We will make the above proposal to the board next week. If the proposal is approved, we will update representatives and the teams as soon as possible with the follow-up schedule.
I would like to thank the App team for their leadership in this process.
Office condition, exposure and cleaning
Last week, we learned that a colleague in the San Francisco office may have been exposed to the COVID-19 virus.
However, out of prudence, we hired a number of antiviral cleaners to fully sterilize the San Francisco office.
They use hospital-level disinfection solutions to sterilize a variety of surfaces as well as halls and elevators leading to our floors.
The office building is fulfilling its duty-of-care agreement, using the corresponding products to ensure the safety of the tenants.
We are confident that when everyone decides to return to work, the office is fully prepared to welcome everyone.
Our Washington, D.C. office uses the WeWork office, which shares the COVID-19 agreement with us and with all of our D.C. workforce.
As of last last week, the Washington, D.C. office had switched to full-to-teleworking in accordance with the guidelines we shared with our San Francisco office.
At the same time, as some New York office colleagues already know, we have also been discussing issues related to the leasing of offices in Brooklyn.
These discussions will continue, but they may be postponed.
For the first time, some of my colleagues came into contact with telecommuting.
Other long-term telecommuting colleagues would like to provide them with some suggestions for adjusting their working methods:
The meeting time should be controlled within one to two hours.
For a longer period of time, consider decentralizing the content of the meeting to meetings on multiple dates.
Clearly define meeting objectives, set the agenda for the meeting, and send reading materials in advance.
Use video conferencing as the default mode of meeting, using tools such as Google Docs and Zoom for online collaborations and connections.
In each meeting, a moderator is arranged to host the meeting, one person to monitor the questions in the chat window and to track the changes in the speaker's list, and the other person to be responsible (or using a multi-person collaborative approach) to help make the minutes of the meeting.
If you need a comfortable headset, please contact the technical support department by e-mail.
Use your health benefits to buy snacks.
Join the #remoties channel on Slack to discuss distributed work with colleagues
The human resources operations team is researching ergonomic guidance in the form of webinars to help all Foundation colleagues become more productive during a distributed office.
“Last week, we asked all community recipients to cancel Wikimedia-financed public events, such as the editorial pine, prior to the WHO’s announcement of the end of the outbreak.
These requests and other restrictions related to the cancellation of activities may result in the inability of these community recipients to complete their scheduled donation activities, and we have informed them that we understand this, while also promising that none of them will be penalized for postponing or modifying these goals.
Over the coming week, we’ll continue to provide more guidance at Wikimania and other regional community meetings and themed community meetings.
The global community currently appears to be shrouded in the grief caused by the destruction of the epidemic, but it is also a source of relief for being able to maintain a clear focus on their respective communities, Wikimedia, and others.
In the future, the CRT team will set up a page on the Meta-Wiki to provide a space for the community to monitor the impact and track the community’s interactions with us.
Keep in touch and communicate COVID-19-related issues
We plan to hold a special staff meeting next Thursday at 14:00 (coordinated Universal Time) / 07:00 (Pacific time), and we will send relevant invitations to your calendar later.
At the meeting, we will share some updates, answer your questions, and set aside some time for everyone to exchange and discuss.
We are united in helping you here.
At the same time, you can learn more about the information mentioned in this e-mail and other important information related to COVID-19.
The CRT team keeps these pages up-to-date and centralizes all the information in one place.
“We are trying to maintain regular communication with our employees living in countries that are currently severely affected by the outbreak.
If you have any questions about travel, activities, major workflows, or insurance, or require additional assistance, please feel free to contact the CRT team.
We are happy to provide you with the necessary support and contacts.
If you’re having a confidant or sensitive issue, please email Bryan Judan, head of human resources operations worldwide.
Although we are faced with these changes, we cannot give up our jobs and obligations.
On the contrary, this epidemic has made us realize that we may now need to adjust our work and obligations in a way that has not existed in the past.
In our view, these measures are necessary to allow us to support each other to continue to do their jobs, to provide the necessary support for our actions, and to continue to bring to the world the services on which they depend.
Always plan without being afraid of the challenge.
Now, please support each other and work together to create space for important work in the coming weeks or even months.
We need everyone to contribute to achieving this goal, so please take care of yourself and your family in order to be at your best when needed.
Finally, please remember to wash your hands and do not touch your face.
Katherine, the CRT team (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doreen D, Gregory V, Jaime V, Joel L, Lynette L, Lynette L, Ryan M and Tony S) and the other members of the leadership team (Grant I, Heather W, Jaime V, Janeen U, Lisa S, Robyn A, Ryan M, and Toby N).
Angiotensin-converting Enzyme 2, ACE2 is an enzyme attached to the outer surface (cell membrane) of cells in the pulmonary, arterial, heart, kidney, and intestine.
ACE2 inhibits the activity of related angiotensin-converting Enzyme (ACE) by reducing angiotensin II content and increasing Ang (1-7) content, and is expected to be a drug target for the treatment of cardiovascular diseases. ACE2 is also an entry point for certain coronaviruses entering cells.
The human-derived version of this enzyme is commonly called hACE2.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is a zinc-containing metal enzyme located on the surface of endothelial cells and other cells.
The ACE2 protein consists of an N-terminal Peptide Enzyme M2 domain and a C-terminal Collectrin renal amino acid transport protein domain.
ACE2 is a uniplasmic type I membrane protein whose enzyme-active domains are exposed to the cell surfaces of the lungs and other tissues.
Another enzyme, known as sheddase, cleframes the extracellular domains of ACE2 from the transmembranian domains, releasing soluble proteins produced into the bloodstream and eventually excreted into the urine.
ACE2 is present in most organs: ACE2 is attached to the cell membrane, mainly type II alveolar cells of the lungs, intestinal epithelial cells in the small intestine, arterial and intravenous endothelial cells in most organs, and smooth muscle cells in the arteries.
ACE2 mRNA expression is also found in the cerebral cortex, striatum, hypothalamus, and cerebral stem.
The main function of ACE2 is to balance ACE.
The ACE cleavages of angiotensin I into angiotensin II with vasoconstrictive activity.
ACE2 then cleavedges angiotensin II (Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-Phe) of the cartilage terminal amino acid phenylalanine and hydrolyzes it into angiotensin (1-7) with vasodilative activity (H-Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-OH).
ACE2 can also cleavage a number of other peptides, including de-arginine 9 slow-bodied peptides (apelin), neurodepressin, strongphine A, and gastrophin.
ACE2 also modifies the membrane transport of the neutral amino acid transport protein SLC6A19 and is associated with Hartnup.
As a trans-membrane protein, ACE2 is the main entry point for certain coronaviruses entering cells, including HCoV-NL63; SARS-CoV (the virus that causes SARS); and SARS-CoV-2 (the virus that causes COVID-19).
More specifically, SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV2’s S1 spike proteins bind to the enzyme domains of ACE2 on the cell’s surface, leading to endocytosis, allowing the virus and the enzyme to be transferred together into the inner nuclei within the cell.
This entry process also requires host seronine protease TMPRSS2 to trigger the activation of the S-protein, which is currently being investigated as a potential treatment for inhibition of the process. Some have speculated that lowering ACE2 levels in cells may be helpful in fighting the infection.
However, a number of professional associations and regulatory agencies recommend the continued use of standard ACE inhibitors and ARB therapies.
A systematic review and meta-analysis, published on July 11, 2012, found that “the use of ACE inhibitors significantly reduced the risk of pneumonia by 34% compared to the control group.”
“For patients with a higher risk of pneumonia, especially those with stroke and heart failure, treatment with ACE inhibitors will also reduce the risk of pneumonia.”
The use of ACE inhibitors is also associated with a reduction in pneumonia-related mortality, but the results are not as reliable as reducing the overall risk of pneumonia.”
Recombinant human ACE2 (rhACE2) is believed to be a new approach to the treatment of acute pulmonary pulmonary pulmonary communicure and blood oxygen saturation in lipopolysaccharide-induced acute respiratory distress syndrome piglets.
rhACE2 has a half-life of about 10 hours in the human body, an effect time of 30 minutes, and the process of action (duration) is 24 hours.
A number of studies have shown that rhACE2 may be a promising drug for patients with classical renin-angiotensin system (RAS) inhibitor intolerances or diseases with elevated circulatory angiotensin II levels. The efficacy of rhACE2 infusion for acute respiratory distress syndrome has been evaluated in clinical trials.
The b’COVID-19 app is a mobile software designed to respond to the 2019-20 coronavirus outbreak, helping to track close contacts and identify individuals who may have come into contact with an infected person (“contacts”).
A large number of applications have been developed or proposed in a number of regions and jurisdictions with the support of official governments.
A number of frameworks have been established to build close contact tracing applications.
The issue of privacy has also attracted a lot of attention, especially because some systems need to track the geographical location of application users.
A less intrusive alternative includes using a Bluetooth signal to record a user’s distance from other phones.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced that they will integrate this Bluetooth-enabled application directly into the Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the government and Alipay have jointly developed an app that allows citizens to self-inflictot patients with COVID-19.
The program is currently used in more than 200 cities in China. Singapore has launched an app called TraceTogether.
The app was developed by the local IT community, was released as open-source software and will be handed over to the government. North Macedonia has launched a Bluetooth-based app, StopKorona!", which is used to track the exposure of potentially infected people and help healthcare organizations respond quickly.
The application was developed by the Ministry of Communications Technology of North Macedonia and the Ministry of Health.
As of April 14, 2020, the app is awaiting approval from the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store.
On April 12, the government announced that the close contact tracing app has entered the advanced stage of development and will be available in the coming weeks. Ireland and France also plan to launch a similar application ("StopCovid").
Both Australia and New Zealand are contemplating developing similar applications based on the TraceTogether app and the BlueTrace protocol. Russia plans to launch a geofencing app for patients with COVID-19 based in Moscow to make sure patients don't leave their homes.
Ross Anderson, a professor of security engineering at the University of Cambridge, cited a number of possible practical problems with the application’s systems, including false-positive false positives and a possible lack of effectiveness if only a small percentage of people use the app.
To stop the spread of misleading or harmful “coronavirus” apps, Apple has placed restrictions on the types of organizations that can release coronavirus-related apps in the Apple App Store, i.e. limited to “official” or other well-reputed organizations.
Similar restrictions have been imposed by Google and Amazon.
Privacy activists say they are concerned about mass surveillance using coronavirus applications, especially whether the surveillance infrastructure built in response to the coronavirus outbreak will be dismantled after the threat is eliminated.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organisations have called for restrictions on such surveillance.
These organizations have announced eight conditions to be met for government projects:
The surveillance must be “legitimate, necessary and proportionate.”
The monitoring and surveillance extension must have a “sunset” clause;
The collected data is used only for COVID-19 purposes;
Data security and anonymized information must be protected, and there is evidence that it is effectively protected.
Digital surveillance must avoid exacerbating discrimination and marginalization;
Any data-sharing with third parties shall be defined by law;
Safeguard measures must be provided to prevent data abuse and citizens must be given the right to deal with data abuse;
All "relevant stakeholders" are required to "practical participation", including public health experts and marginalized groups. The German Chaos Computer Club (CCC) and Reporter ohne Grenzen (RSF) have also issued verification checklists.
Google/Apple’s proposed plan is designed to address the issue of continuous monitoring and remove it from the device’s operating system as soon as tracking is no longer necessary.
In some countries, web-based instead of application location tracking, users do not need to download the application, but also avoid being tracked.
In Israel, web-based tracking has been approved.
Network solutions that have access to raw location data may have serious privacy concerns.
However, not all systems with a central server have access to personal location data; many privacy-preserving systems that only use central servers to communicate with each other have been created (see below for details below).
South Korea has launched a non-application-based system to perform contact tracing tasks.
Instead of using a dedicated app, the system chooses to collect tracking information from a variety of sources, including mobile device tracking data and credit card transaction data, and then combine that information to send notifications to potentially infected people via text messages.
In addition to using this information to alert potential close contacts, the government was also allowed to disclose the location information, as the country made significant changes to the information privacy law in the wake of the MERS outbreak.
This information can be obtained by the public through a wide range of applications and websites. Other countries (including Germany) are considering the use of both centralized systems and privacy protection systems.
As of April 6, 2020, specific details have not yet been released.
Close-to-net contact tracing for privacy protection is a well-established concept, and a large number of relevant research literature dates back to at least 2013. As of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen expert groups were working on privacy protection solutions, such as the use of Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) to record the distance between users and other mobile phones.
PEPP-PT is a harmonising scheme that includes a centralized and decentralized approach and is not a single protocol. Decentralized protocols include Decentralized Privacy Parity tracking (DP-PPT/DP-3T), temporary contact numbers (TCN, originally referred to as contact event number, CEN), privacy-sensitivity protocol, and mobile contact tracking mechanism (PACT).
According to these agreements, the personally identifiable personal data will never leave the device, and all matches will be made on the device.
The MIT Media Lab’s privacy team has been developing the SafePaths platform, which uses privacy-preserving technology when collecting and using location or path cross-data to track COVID-19.
In addition, Enigma MPC, a company originally founded and dedicated to the development of privacy at the MIT Media Lab, is developing a similar platform called SafeTrace.
SafeTrace leverages security hardware technology to enable users to share sensitive location and health data with other users and officials without compromising data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, a number of groups jointly established the Global TCN Alliance around basically the same approach and basically overlapping protocols, with the goal of reducing fragmented information and supporting global interoperability of tracking and alerting applications, which is a key to widespread adoption.
On April 9, 2020, the Singapore government announced that its official government application uses the open source BlueTrace protocol.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, which respectively control the Android and iOS mobile platforms, announced a close contact tracing program, claiming that the combination of Bluetooth low energy technology and privacy protection encryption technology can play a role in protecting privacy.
In addition, they published the core technical specifications used in the system.
According to Apple and Google, the system is planned to be rolled out in three phases:
A number of tools have been launched to support the government’s creation of an official coronavirus-tracking app that protects privacy.
To integrate this feature directly into the iOS and Android systems, Google and Apple plan to first launch the system through operating system updates, after the threat of the epidemic will remove the system in the same way, in order to solve the follow-up monitoring problem.
Drug repositioning (also known as drug reapproval, re-evaluation, redistribution, or therapeutic conversion) refers to the conversion of an approved drug for other uses to treat a disease or medical condition that is different from its initial development.
That’s the current line of research to develop safe and effective treatments for COVID-19.
Other research directions include the development of COVID-19 vaccines and recovery plasma blood transfusions. SARS-CoV-2 has about 66 pharmaceutical-ready proteins, each with multiple ligand binding sites.
Analyzing these binding sites provides a reasonable solution for the development of effective antivirals against the COVID-19 protein.
The most important SARS-CoV-2 target proteins include papaya-like proteases, RNA-dependent RNA polymerases, helicases, S proteins, and ADP ribose diphosphatase.
Several candidate compounds were studied by Hussein AA et al. and then optimized and analyzed the skeleton similarity of these candidate compounds against the most approved drugs in order to accelerate the development of effective anti-SARS-CoV-2 drugs in their pre-clinical trials and recommend them in clinical study design.
chloroquine is an anti-malarial drug that is also used to treat some autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that four drugs, including chloroquine and related hydroxychloroquine, will be studied as part of the solidarity clinical trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo has announced that tests of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine will begin on March 24. On March 28, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization (EUA), which allows the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate.
The treatment has not yet been approved by the FDA for clinical trial procedures and is only approved by the EUA as an experimental treatment for emergency use by patients who have been hospitalized but cannot be treated in clinical trials.
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) said that “the use, dose, or timing of hydroxychloroquine for the prevention or treatment of SARS-CoV-2 infection has not yet been determined.”
Doctors said they were using the drug when there was “no choice.”
A Turkish research group in Istanbul is conducting a small study of chloroquine in combination with zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
The University of Duc and the University of Oxford are conducting large-scale research.
New York University Langone School of Medicine is conducting an experiment on the safety and efficacy of prophylactic use of hydroxychloroquine.
Chinese clinical trials conducted in Wuhan and Shenzhen called fapiravir “significantly effective.”
Thirty-five patients tested negative within an average of four days, compared with 45 patients who did not receive the drug for 11 days.
In a study of 240 pneumonia patients conducted in Wuhan, half received fapilavir and the other half received Abidor.
The Italian Food and Drug Administration reminded the public that there is insufficient and preliminary evidence to support the drug.
On April 2, Germany announced that it would purchase the drug as a reserve from Japan and use the army to transport the drug to a university hospital to treat COVID-19 patients in the hospital.
According to the South China Morning Post, Shinzo Abe has proposed to the Trump administration to purchase the drug. The drug may be less effective in severely ill patients where the virus has bred.
It may not be safe for a pregnant or pregnant person to use the drug.
A study of lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra) (a combination of the antiviral drugs lopinavir and ritonavir) concluded that “no benefit has been observed”.
These drugs are designed to suppress the replication of the Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) by binding to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drug to find a compound that binds to the SARS-CoV-2 protease. The scientific community has been criticized for devoting resources to reusing drugs developed specifically for HIV/Acquired Immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS).
The World Health Organization included lopinavir/ritonavir in the international solidarity experiment.
Ridsivir is a drug created and developed by Gilead Sciences for the treatment of Ebola and Marburg virus infections. Gilead Sciences subsequently discovered that redcivir has antiviral activity against a wide range of filamentous viruses, pneumococcal viruses, and coronaviruses in vitro.
One problem with antiviral therapy is that resistance produced through mutations can lead to more severe disease and spread.
A number of early pre-trial studies have suggested that redesivir may have a higher drug-resistant genetic barrier. A number of clinical trials are currently underway, two of which are conducted by Cleveland University Hospital; one for patients with moderate symptoms, and the other for patients with more severe symptoms.
There are currently three intravenous clinical trials of vitamin C in critically hospitalized patients with COVID-19; two placebo-controlled trials (China, Canada) and one non-controlled trial (Italy).
New York State began testing the antibiotic azithromycin on March 24, 2020.
Japan’s National Center for Global Health and Medicine (NCGM) is planning a clinical trial of Teijin’s Alvesco, an inhaled corticosteroid used to treat pre-corrupted patients infected with the new coronavirus.
A Phase II trial of angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is underway, which will recruit 200 patients from severe hospitalized cases in Denmark, Germany and Austria to determine the effectiveness of the therapy.
Researchers at the Montreal Cardiology Research Institute in Canada are currently studying the role of colchicine in alleviating inflammatory and lung communicative complication in patients with COVID-19 mild disease.
The study, called COLCORONA, is enrolling 6,000 adults aged 40 and older who have been diagnosed with COVID-19 with mild symptoms that do not require hospitalization.
Maternity, lactation or women who do not use effective contraception are ineligible.
Several anti-coagulants are being tested in Italy.
Low molecular weight heparin is widely used for the treatment of patients, which prompted the Italian Food and Drug Administration to publish guidelines for its use.
On April 14, Italy announced a multi-centre study of 300 patients to study the use of therapeutic and therapeutic doses of intraheparin sodium.
Because SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, repurposing approved antiviral drugs is of considerable scientific concern, having been developed for diseases such as Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS), Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and West Nile virus.
According to the 7th edition of Chinese guidelines, ribavirin is recommended for COVID-19
Abidol hydrochloric acid: according to the 7th edition of Chinese guidelines, it is recommended to treat COVID-19 with Abitur hydrochloric acid
There are a number of potentially reusable antibiotics that have been identified for COVID-19 treatment:
Tocilizumab (Anti-IL-6 Receptor): approved in China.
Experiments have also been conducted in Italy and China. See Tocilizumab#COVID-19.
The b'A COVID-19 vaccine refers to a hypothetical vaccine against the 2019 coronavirus disease (COVID-19).
Although no clinical trials have yet been completed, a number of attempts to develop the vaccine are still underway.
In late February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) stated that it does not expect a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for this disease-causing virus within 18 months.
In April, there were five vaccine vaccine candidates entering the Phase I safety study.
COVID-19 was discovered in December 2019.
In 2020, a large-scale epidemic spread around the world, sparking a lot of investment and research in the development of vaccines.
Many organizations are developing a possible SARS-CoV-2 vaccine using published viral genomes.
CEPI proposed a vaccine development plan in April and stated that the top priorities are speed, production capacity, large-scale deployment, and global accessibility.
In April, CEPI scientists reported that in early 2020, 10 different technology platforms were working on an effective COVID-19 vaccine.
The main platform targets for Phase I security research include:
Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) (Phase I developers and vaccine candidates: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral vectors (Phase I developers and vaccine candidates: CanSino Biologics, type 5 adenovirus vector)
CEPI scientists reported in April that a total of 115 vaccine candidates are in the early stages of development, 78 of which have been identified as ongoing projects (79, according to the Milken Institute), and another 37 have been announced, with little public information (presumably planned or designed).
The Phase I-II trial conducts initial safety and immunogenicity tests, typically randomized, placebo-controlled multi-point trials, while determining more precise and effective doses.
Phase III trials typically involve a larger number of participants, including a control group, and test the effectiveness of the vaccine to prevent the disease, while monitoring adverse reactions at optimal doses.
Of the 79 actively developed vaccine candidates (recognised as of the beginning of April 2020), 74 had not yet been evaluated in humans (still in “pre-clinical” studies).
Around January 24, 2020, the University of Queensland in Australia announced that it was studying the potential of a molecular pliers vaccine that genetically modifies viral proteins to stimulate the immune response.
Around January 24, 2020, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) of the University of Saskatchewan in Canada announced that it will begin developing a vaccine with the goal of starting human trials in 2021.
The Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention and the University of Hong Kong announced vaccine development projects on January 26, and January 28, 2020, respectively.
Around January 29, 2020, Janssen pharmaceutical company led by Hanneke Schuitemaker announced that it had started developing the vaccine.
Janssen is working with its biotechnology partner Vaxart to develop an oral vaccine.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a production partnership with Vaxart to jointly develop the vaccine.
On February 8, 2020, the OncoGen research laboratory in Romania published a paper on vaccine design, using a technology similar to that used in immunotherapies for cancer neo-antigens.
On March 25, the director of the institute announced that they have completed the synthesis of the vaccine and began vaccine testing.
On February 27, 2020, NuGenerex Immuno-Oncology, a subsidiary of Generex, announced that it is embarking on a vaccine project to develop an Ii-Key peptide vaccine for COVID-19.
They hope to develop a vaccine candidate and conduct human trials “within 90 days.”
On March 5, 2020, the University of Washington in St. Louis announced its vaccine R&D program.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Army’s Medical Research and Materials Command at Fort Detrick and the Walter Reed Army Research Institute in Silver Spring (both located in western Maryland) announced that a vaccine is being developed.
Around March 10, 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced a partnership with Novavax Inc.
The development and production of vaccines.
The two organizations further announced plans to conduct preclinical trials and Phase I clinical trials by July 2020.
On March 12, 2020, India's Ministry of Health announced that it is working on 11 isolates that will take at least a year and a half to two years to develop a vaccine even if it accelerates progress.
On March 12, 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company based in Québe, Quebec, reported the development of coronavirus virus-like particles with partial funding from the Canadian Institutes of Health.
The vaccine candidate is currently under laboratory research and is scheduled to enter human trials in July or August 2020.
Earlier this week, the Guardian reported that U.S. President Donald U.S. President Donald Trump had offered CureVac a “big amount of money” to get exclusive access to the COVID-19 vaccine.
On March 17, 2020, U.S.-pharmaceutical Pfizer announced a partnership with the German company BioNTech to jointly develop a mRNA-based vaccine.
The mRNA-based vaccine candidate, BNT162, is currently in preclinical testing and is expected to begin clinical trials in April 2020.
On March 17, 2020, Takis Biotech announced that it will receive preclinical test results in April 2020, and its final vaccine candidate may begin human trials in the fall.
On March 19, 2020, in France, Epidemic Preparedness Innovation Consortium (CEPI) announced a $4.9 million investment in the establishment of a COVID-19 vaccine research consortium consisting of the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience, and the University of Pittsburgh, which brings CEPI’s total investment in COVID-19 vaccine research and development to $29 million.
CEPI’s other investment partners in COVID-19 vaccine R&D include Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, the University of Hong Kong, the University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On March 20, 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had begun animal trials of six different vaccine candidates.
On March 20, 2020, researchers at Imperial College London announced that they were working on a self-amplified RNA vaccine for COVID-19.
The vaccine candidate was developed within 14 days of receiving a virus sequence released in China.
In late March, the Canadian government announced $275 million to fund 96 medical response research projects for COVID-19, including numerous vaccine candidates from Canadian companies and universities, such as Medicago and the University of Saskatchewan program.
At about the same time, the Canadian government announced a $192 million effort to develop the COVID-19 vaccine and plans to establish a national “vaccine bank” that includes several new vaccines so that they can be put into use in the event of a renewed coronavirus outbreak.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the Faculty of Medicine at the University of Pittsburgh reported a test of PittCoVacc, a COVID-19 vaccine that could be used in mice, saying that “the SARS-CoV-2 S1 subunit vaccine provided by MNA allows [the mice] to produce a powerful antigen-specific antibody response that begins two weeks after immunization.”
On April 16, 2020, the School of Pharmacy at the University of Waterloo in Canada announced the design of a DNA-based vaccine candidate as a possible nasal spray.
Using bacteriophages, the researchers designed DNA to replicate inside human bacteria to produce harmless virus-like particles, which may stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies against the SARS-CoV-2 virus.
In March 2020, the U.S. government, industry and the three universities pooled their resources, combined with cloud computing resources from HP, Amazon, Microsoft, and Google, to access IBM’s supercomputers.
Some vaccines have heterogeneous effects, also known as non-specific effects.
This means that in addition to preventing the disease, they also bring other benefits.
A further randomized trial conducted in Australia is recruiting 4,170 health care workers.
Vaccines under development may be unsafe or ineffective.
Using COVID-19-specific animal models, such as ACE2 transgenic mice, other experimental animals, and non-human primates to assess vaccine efficacy, earlier studies to assess the efficacy of the vaccine showed the need for biosafety level 3 containment measures for the treatment of live viruses, as well as international coordination to ensure compliance with standardized safety procedures.
Vaccines against SARS and MERS have already been tested in non-human animal models.
As of 2020, there are no safe and effective SARS treatments or prophylactic vaccines for humans.
The identification and development of new vaccines and drugs for the treatment of SARS for the treatment of SARS, according to research papers published in 2005 and 2006, is a top priority for governments and public health institutions around the world. Moreover, there are currently no effective vaccines against MERS.
At the time of the MERS epidemic, it was thought that existing SARS research could provide a useful template for the development of vaccines and therapies for MERS-CoV infection.
As of March 2020, there has been a (DNA-based) MERS vaccine that has completed Phase I human clinical trials, and three other vaccines are under development, all of which are viral vector vaccines, two of which are adenoviral vector vaccines (ChADOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one is MVA vector vaccine (MVA-MERS-S).
A variety of social media articles have fuelled conspiracy theories claiming that the virus behind COVID-19 has been found and that a vaccine is already available.
The patents cited in a variety of social media articles refer to existing patents on the genetic sequences and vaccines of other existing strains of coronaviruses, such as SARS coronaviruses.
COVID-19 is an infectious disease caused by Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus type 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include febrile fever, cough and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms may include weakness, muscle soreness, diarrhoea, sore throat, loss of smell, and abdominal pain.
The incubation period ranging from exposure to the onset of the disease ranges from 2 to 14 days and is usually about 5 days.
Although the symptoms are mild in most cases, in some cases develop viral pneumonia and multi-organ failure.
As of April 17, 2020, more than 2.24 million cases had been reported in 210 countries and territories around the world, resulting in more than 153,000 deaths.
More than 568,000 people have been rehabilitated. The virus is transmitted from person to person primarily through close contact, often by coughing, sneezing, or using droplets that occur during coughing, sneezing, or speaking.
Although exhalation will produce droplets, droplets usually fall on the ground or the surface of various objects and do not cause long-distance infection.
In addition, if someone touches the virus-contaminated surface, and then touches the mouth of their eyes and nose, they may also be infected with the virus.
Viruses can live up to 72 hours on an object’s surface.
Although it is also possible to spread in the early and later stages of the disease, it is most contagious for the first three days prior to the onset. The standard diagnostic method is to perform real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) detection of nasopharyngeal swabs.
It is recommended that people with suspected viral infections and their caregivers wear masks.
Suggestions for the wearing of masks by the public vary from place to place, with some authorities objecting to the wearing of masks, while others require them.
There are currently no COVID-19 vaccines or specific antiviral treatments.
The vast majority of countries in all six WHO regions have reported local transmission of the disease.
People infected with the virus can be asymptomatic, and there may also be suspected flu symptoms, such as fever, coughing, weakness and shortness.
Urgent symptoms include breathing difficulties, persistent chest pain or chest tightness, blurred consciousness, difficulty waking, and turbulence in the face or lips; if these symptoms occur, immediate medical attention is recommended.
A small number of cases may be associated with upper respiratory tract symptoms, such as sneezing, runny nose, or sore throat.
Different proportions of gastrointestinal symptoms, such as nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea, were also monitored.
In some cases in China, only chest tightness and palpitations occurred at an early stage.
In some cases, it can lead to viral pneumonia, multi-organ failure, and even lead to death.
We call it the incubation period.
COVID-19 typically has an incubation period of 5-6 days, but it may extend to between 2 and 14 days.
97.5% of infected people will experience symptoms within 11.5 days of the infection.The report indicates that not all infected people will develop symptoms.
The role played by asymptomatic infected people in the spread of the epidemic is not yet entirely clear; however, preliminary evidence suggests that asymptomatic infected persons may contribute to the spread of the disease.
The proportion of asymptomatic infected people has not yet been determined, and research is still under way. The Korean Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (KCDC) reports that 20% of the confirmed cases do not show any symptoms during their hospitalization.
Since April 1, the National Health and Health Commission of China has included asymptomatic cases in its daily notification; of the 166 infected people on that day, 130 (78%) were asymptomatic at the time of testing.
Both sputum and saliva can carry a large number of viruses.
Talking out loud sprinkles more droplets than a normal speech.
A study in Singapore found that an unobstructed cough can cause droplets to travel up to 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although such viruses generally do not spread through the air, the National Academy of Sciences determined that it may be transmitted through bio-aerosols, and the air collector in the hallway outside the ward detected positive samples of viral RNA.
Medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) can negate respiratory secretions, resulting in airborne transmission.
There are also concerns that the virus may spread through feces, but this risk is thought to be low. The virus is most contagious when a patient develops symptoms; although it may spread before symptoms appear, the risk is lower.
According to the European Center for Disease Control and Prevention (ECDC), although it is not entirely clear about the spread of the new coronavirus, 2-3 people are usually infected by one person. The survival time of the virus on the surface of the object ranges from a few hours to a few days.
Specifically, it is known that the new crown virus can survive one day on cardboard, up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304), and for up to four hours on 99%-pure copper products.
However, its survival time is also affected by humidity and temperature.
If used correctly, soaps and cleaners can also kill viruses. Soap products can degrade the aliphatic protective layer of the virus, making it inactive, while also removing the virus from the skin and other surfaces.
Other solutions, such as ammonium benzar chloride and glucosamine (a surgical disinfectant), were less effective. A study in Hong Kong, China, showed that saliva samples were collected on average two days after being admitted to the hospital.
5/6 of the patients had the highest viral load in the first sample, and 1 in 6 patients detected the highest viral load the next day.
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a new type of Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus, which was first isolated from three pneumonia patients in a group of acute respiratory disease cases in Wuhan.
All the characteristics of this new SARS-CoV-2 virus are present in relevant coronaviruses in nature.
Before the virus enters the human body, it can be slaughtered by household soap, which destroys the virus's protective bladder. SARS-CoV-2 is closely related to the original SARS-CoV.
The lungs are the organ most affected by COVID-19, as the virus enters the host cell via angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), which has the highest levels in type II alveolar cells of the lungs.
The virus binds to ACE2 through a special surface glycoprotein called a "snaps" (capsule particles) and enters the host cell.
Among the patients admitted to the hospital in Wuhan, China, 12% of infected people were found to have acute cardiomyocardial damage, and the proportion of seriously ill patients was relatively high.
Cardiovascular inflammatory reactions and immune system disorders due to the deterioration of the disease, but acute cardiomyocardial damage may also be associated with the heart's ACE2 receptor.
The ACE2 receptor is highly expressed in the heart and is involved in regulating heart function.
It is reported that ICU-supervised COVID-19 patients have a higher incidence of thrombosis (31%) and intravenous thromboembolism (25%), which may be associated with poor prognosis. An autopsy of COVID-19 deceased patients has found diffuse alveolar injury (DAD) and lymphocytic inflammatory immersion in the lungs.
Although SARS-COV-2 is addicted to respiratory epithelial cells expressing ACE2, patients with severe COVID-19 experience a systemic inflammatory response.
In particular, pathogenic GM-CSF secreted T cells in COVID-19 patients were associated with the recruitment of inflammatory IL-6 secretory monocytes with severe pulmonary pathology.
The autopsy report also saw lymphocyte infiltration.
The World Health Organization (WHO) has published several disease detection programs.
The standard detection method uses real-time reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR).
This test usually detects respiratory samples obtained through nasopharyngeal swabs; however, nasal swab or sputum samples can also be used.
In general, the results can be obtained within a few hours to two days.
Blood tests can also be carried out, but a two-week interval is required to take two blood samples, and the results have almost no immediate value.
Chinese scientists have been able to isolate coronavirus strains and publish genetic sequences so that laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing reagents to see if they are infected with the virus.
As of April 4, 2020, antibody testing (not only detecting active infections, but also detecting whether an individual has been infected with the virus in the past) is still under development, but it has not yet been popularized.
According to China's experience, the detection accuracy is only 60% to 70%.
On March 21, 2020, the U.S. FDA approved the first real-time diagnosing reagent, which was put into use at the end of that month. Diagnostic guidelines issued by Zhongnan Hospital of Wuhan University proposed a method for detecting infections based on clinical characteristics and epidemiological risks.
The early infection is more common in double-pulmonary multi-leaf-grinding glass shadows, with peripheral asymmetry and rear part cloth.
With the development of the virus, there may be pleural subarural, gravel road signs (leaf spacing with varying degrees of alveolar filling) and pulmonary changes.
There is very little information on COVID-19 micropathological damage and pathophysiology.
The main pathological findings of the autopsy are as follows:
Examination with the naked eye: pleurisy, pericarditis, pulmonary densification and pulmonary edema
Four severe levels of viral pneumonia were observed:
Mild Pneumonia: pulmonary edema, pulmonary cell hyperplification, a large number of atypical pulmonary pulmonary cells, interstitial inflammatory accompaniment lymphocyte infiltration and the formation of multi-nucleomy cells
Severe Pneumonia: Diffuse alveolar injury (DAD) accompanied by diffuse alveolar exudation.
DAD is the cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and severe hypoxemia.
Pneumonia in the recovery period: repulmonary exudation and pulmonary interstitial fibrosis
Blood: Dispersive intravascular coagulation (DIC); young white-young erythrocyte response
Precautions that can be taken in order to reduce the risk of infection include: staying at home; avoiding entering densely populated areas; washing your hands frequently with soap and water for at least 20 seconds; maintaining good respiratory hygiene; and avoiding touching the eyes, nose, or mouth without washing your hands.
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends covering the mouth and nose with a tissue when coughing or sneezing; if there are no paper towels, it is recommended to use the inside of the elbow.
It is recommended to properly clean hand hygiene after coughing or sneezing.
At the heart of maintaining a social distancing strategy focuses on the closure of schools and workplaces, restricting travel, and canceling large public gatherings, thereby reducing the chances of infected people coming into contact with large-scale groups.
In addition, social distancing guidelines require maintaining a distance of at least 6 feet (1.8 meters) in interpersonal communication.
There are no effective drugs to prevent COVID-19. Given that a vaccine is not expected to be developed until at least 2021, efforts to reduce the peak of outbreaks are the key to controlling COVID-19’s spread, which is what we are known as “leveling the epidemiologic curve.”
At the same time, the CDC recommends that people regularly wash their hands with soap and tap water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or with obvious dirt on their hands, before meals, and after ejaculating, coughing, or sneezing.
In addition, it is also recommended to use alcohol-containing hand sanitizers with an alcohol content of at least 60%, but only when there is no soap and running water around them. For areas where water-free hand sanitizer cannot be purchased, the WHO provides two formulations for local production.
These formulas excite antimicrobial activity through ethanol or isopropanol.
The use of hydrogen peroxide to help eliminate bacterial spores in alcohol; hydrogen peroxide is not an “active hand disinfection substance.”
Add glycerin as a moisturizer.
Provides people with supportive care, which may include infusion therapy, oxygen support, and support for other affected vital organs.
The CDC advises suspected carriers of the virus to wear simple masks.
In vitro membranous oxygenation (ECMO) has been used to alleviate the symptoms of respiratory failure, but its benefits have yet to be investigated.
It is recommended to maintain personal hygiene and promote healthy lifestyles and eating habits to improve immunity.
Supportive therapy may be effective for patients with milder conditions in the early stages of infection. The WHO and China's National Health Commission have issued an initiative calling for the proper treatment of COVID-19 patients who have been admitted to the hospital.
U.S. critically ill experts and pulmonary experts have compiled the treatment recommendations made by various institutions and provided them free of charge through IBCC.
As of April 2020, COVID-19 therapy has not yet been developed.
In response to certain symptoms, some medical experts recommend the use of paracetamol (acetamine) instead of ibuprofen for first-line treatment.
Precautions must be taken to minimize the risk of transmission of the virus, in the medical environment to complete the operation that may produce aerosols, such as intubation or ventilation to pay special attention to protection.
For healthcare professionals caring for patients with COVID-19, the CDC advises that in addition to standard precautions, contact prevention measures, and air-property-destimation preventive measures, patients should be transferred to air-transmitted infectious disease isolation rooms (AIIRs). The CDC outlines guidelines for the use of personal protective equipment (PPEs) during an outbreak.
PPE protective clothing, respirators or masks, eye protection, and medical gloves. If possible, it is best to use a respirator (rather than a mask).
N95 masks are approved for use in industrial applications, but the FDA has authorized the use of such masks in accordance with an emergency use authorization (EUA).
It is mainly used to prevent inhalation of suspended particles (such as dust), but it can not be used for other purposes to effectively isolate specific biological agents.
If you don’t have a mask, the CDC recommends using a mask, and you can also wear a homemade mask if necessary.
Most COVID-19 cases have mild symptoms and do not require the use of mechanical ventilation or other alternatives, but a certain proportion of cases require such measures.
Healthcare professionals are actively conducting research to provide respiratory support to COVID-19 patients with symptoms of respiratory failure. There is evidence that intubation can be avoided by nasal high-traffic oxygen therapy or bi-phase airway.
It’s unclear whether the above two treatment options can have the same efficacy in severely ill patients.
If conditions permit, some doctors tend to use invasive-mechanical ventilation, because this technology can also limit the spread of aerosol particles compared to high-traffic oxygen therapy through the nose. Severe cases are mainly in the elderly (over 60 years old, especially those over 80 years old).
In many developed countries, the number of beds per capita is insufficient, and in view of limited medical conditions, the health care system is unable to cope with the severe situation that requires hospitalization due to the surge in COVID-19 cases.
A Chinese study found that 5% of patients need to be sent to the intensive care unit, 2.3% require mechanical ventilation, and 1.4% will die as a result.
About 30 percent of COVID-19 patients in China end up in the ICU.
With the development of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) caused by COVID-19, the mechanical ventilation complexity is further increased, and oxygenation is becoming more and more difficult.
Respirators supporting pressure control mode and high PEEP need to be used to maximize the delivery of oxygen, while minimizing the risk of lung damage and pneumothorax caused by ventilators.
Older ventilators may not achieve high PEEP.
Prospective therapies have been studied since January 2020, and a number of antiviral drugs are currently in clinical trials.
Ridsivir appears to be one of the most promising drugs so far.
Although the new drug may not be developed until 2021, several of the drugs tested have either been approved for other uses or have entered the advanced testing phase.
Severely ill patients can try antiviral drugs.
The WHO calls on a wide range of volunteers to be involved in trials for the effectiveness and safety of potential therapies. The FDA has provisionally authorised recovery plasma transfusions as an experimental treatment for critically ill patients with their lives.
There have been no clinical studies necessary to prove whether the therapy can safely and effectively treat the disease.
In February 2020, China released a mobile app to contain the outbreak.
The user must enter the name and identity card number upon request.
The app is able to determine the potential risk of infection by detecting “close contact” by monitoring data.
Each user can also check the status status of the other three users.
Once a potential risk is detected, the app not only advises self-isolation, but also alerts local health officials. Synergistic mobile data analytics, facial recognition technology, cell phone tracking, and artificial intelligence are used to track infected people and those they come into contact with in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government authorized security agencies to track mobile phone data from suspected coronavirus carriers.
The purpose of this measure is to impose mandatory isolation and to protect groups that may be in contact with infected citizens.
Also in March 2020, Deutsche Telekom shared aggregation of mobile phone location data with Germany’s German federal government, the Robert Koch Institute, to study and prevent the spread of the virus.
Russia deploys facial-recognition technology to detect people who violate quarantine regulations.
Giulio Gallera, Italy’s regional health commissioner, pointed out that mobile phone operator data shows that “40% of citizens are still moving around.”
The German government held a 48-hour weekend hackathon involving more than 42,000 participants.
Estonian President Kersti Kaljulaid has called for innovative solutions on a global scale to prevent the spread of the coronavirus.
Segregation, travel restrictions, treating side effects, or worrying about self-inflicted infections can all be a nuisance.
According to Rory O’Connor, “Social isolation, loneliness, health anxiety, stress and economic distress are the perfect storms that hurt people’s mental health and well-being.”
The disease is milder, with very few or no symptoms, similar to other common upper respiratory tract diseases, such as the common cold.
Minor cases usually recover within two weeks, while severe or critical cases may take three to six weeks to recover.
According to data from other similar viruses, such as SARS and MERS, pregnant women are at a higher risk of developing severe COVID-19, but are not supported by COVID-19 data. COVID-19 may have an impact on the lungs of some people, which in turn can cause pneumonia.
COVID-19 may rapidly develop into Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS), leading to respiratory failure, septic shock, or multi-organ failure.
Combinations related to COVID-19 include sepsis, coagulation abnormalities, and heart, kidney and liver damage.
Six percent of COVID-19 enrollees had coagrine abnormalities, especially prolonged prothrombomerism, and four percent of patients in this group had kidney abnormalities.
About 20-30% of COVID-19 patients have elevated liver enzymes (transferases).
According to the same report, the average time from onset to death is ten days, of which the average length of hospital stay is five days.
Patients transferred to the ICU took an average of seven days from admission to death.
Early case studies found an average of 14 days from the onset of early symptoms to death, with an overall time range of 6 to 41 days.
A study by China’s National Health Commission (NHC) found a mortality rate of 2.8 percent for men and 1.7 percent for women.
Histopathological examination of the autopsy lung samples showed diffuse alveolar damage with cell mucomocus secretions in both lungs.
Pulmonary cells observed viral cell lesions.
Pulmonary images are similar to Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS).
11.8 percent of the deaths reported by China’s National Health Commission were due to heart damage caused by elevated troponin levels or sudden cardiac arrest.
According to data released in the U.S. in March, 89% of hospitalized patients have a previous medical history. The availability of medical resources and regional socio-economic conditions may also affect mortality.
In view of regional and methodological difficulties, disease mortality estimates differ.
Underestimating mild cases can lead to overestimation of mortality.
However, if the case death is due to a previous infection, it may mean that the current death rate is underestimated.
Compared to non-smokers, smokers are 1.4 times more likely to develop into severe COVID-19 patients, requiring intensive care or the risk of death is about 2.4 times higher.
The Hospital Authority of Hong Kong, China, found that the pulmonary capacity of some survivors decreased by 20% to 30%, and lung scans showed organ damage.
It is also possible to face post-primary and post-primity syndrome after recovery.
As of March 2020, it’s not certain that previous infections will enable survivors to achieve effective and long-lasting immunity.
Immunity may be possible based on the behavioral characteristics of other coronaviruses, but there have been reports that some COVID-19 cases tested positive for coronavirus shortly after recovery.
It is believed that these cases continue to worsen the infection rather than re-infection.
The virus is believed to be a natural virus that originates in animals and is infected by spillover.
The actual origin has not yet been determined, but as of December 2019, the scope of infection was almost entirely human-to-human transmission.
The first 41 confirmed COVID-19 case studies, published in The Lancet in January 2020, showed that the earliest onset date was December 1, 2019.
The earliest outbreak date reported in the WHO’s official publication was December 8, 2019.
Mortality is usually quantified in several ways.
These figures vary from region to region and change over time and are influenced by population characteristics such as detection volume, quality of the health system, treatment options, the timing of the first outbreak, as well as demographic characteristics such as age, gender, and general state of health.
At the end of 2019, the WHO designated the ICD-10 emergency disease code U07.1 for laboratory-confirmed deaths from SARS-CoV-2 infection, assigning U07.2 to clinical or epidemiologic diagnosis of COVID-19 but un laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 deaths. The case fatality rate equals the total number of deaths within a given time frame divided by the total number of confirmed cases.
As of April 17, 2020, the global case fatality rate was 6.9 percent (153,822/2,240,191).
Mortality rates also vary from region to region. Some other measures include case fatality (CFR) and infection-mortality (IFR), which reflects disease deaths as a percentage of the number of confirmed cases, while the latter reflects the percentage of disease-to-kill deaths (both confirmed and undiagnosed).
There is no time limit to these statistics, and it tracks the entire process from infection to case resolution in a specific population.
Although not all infected people will produce antibodies, the presence of antibodies can provide information on the number of infections.
Castiglione d’Adda, the epicentre of the outbreak in Italy, a town of only 4,600 people, has so far killed 80 (1.7 per cent).
In Gangelt, the epidemic has spread through various carnival festivities, even affecting young people, but the mortality rate is relatively low, and not all COVID-19 deaths are officially classified.
Germany’s health care system is overstretched.
According to blood donors, about 3% of Dutch citizens may have antibodies.
So far, it has been confirmed that 69 people (0.004% of the total population) have died of COVID-19.
Epidemic impact and mortality rates vary by gender.
Studies in China and Italy have shown a higher mortality rate for men than for women.
Men between the ages of 50 and 60 had the highest risk of death, with a similar gender gap in the 90-year-old age bracket.
The male mortality rate in China is 2.8% and the mortality rate for women is 1.7%.
The exact cause of this sex difference is unclear, but it can be caused by genetic and behavioral factors.
Based on gender differences in immunology, smoking rates in women are lower, and men with comorbidities (such as high blood pressure) are younger than women, resulting in a higher mortality rate among men than among women.
In Europe, men account for 57% of infected people, accounting for 72% of the total deaths due to COVID-19.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government has not tracked gender-related data for COVID-19 infection.
Studies have shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza, and SARS affect both men and women differently.
The proportion of female health workers (especially nurses) is higher, and the risk of exposure to the virus is also higher.
On February 11, 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) announced that the disease was officially named "COVID-19".
Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, the head of the WHO, explained that CO stands for “coronary”, VI stands for “virus”, D stands for “disease”, and 19 stands for the first outbreak (December 31, 2019).
The name was chosen in order to avoid quoting specific geographic locations (e.g. China), animal species, or populations to comply with international naming recommendations to prevent stigmatization. The virus that causes COVID-19 is known as Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus type 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
In addition, the WHO also uses "COVID-19 virus" and "the virus that causes COVID-19 in mass transmission".
Diseases and viruses are commonly referred to as “coronaviruses”.
During the initial outbreak in Wuhan, China, people generally referred to the virus and the disease as the "coronavirus" and "Wuhan coronavirus".
In January 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO), based on its 2015 guidelines on the location of use in disease and virus names, recommended 2019-nCov and 2019-nCoV Acute Respiratory Illness as temporary virus and disease names.
The official names COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were released on February 11, 2020.
In view of capacity constraints in the standard supply chain, some digital manufacturers are starting to print health-care materials, such as nasal swabs and respirator components.
For example, when an Italian hospital was in desperate need of a ventilator, the supplier was unable to deliver the parts within the required amount of time, and a local start-up was reverse-engineered to print the required 100 ventilation valves overnight.
After the initial COVID-19 outbreak, conspiracies, misinformation and disinformation about the origin, scale, prevention, treatment and other aspects of the disease spread rapidly through the Internet.
Humans seem to be able to spread the virus to other animals.
The study found no evidence of viral replication in pigs, ducks and chickens.
There are currently no drugs or vaccines approved to treat the disease.
Government agencies, academic groups, and industry researchers are actively engaged in international research to develop vaccines and drugs to treat COVID-19.
In March, the World Health Organization (WHO) launched a "solidarity trial" to evaluate the therapeutic effects of the four existing antiviral compounds that are currently most likely to produce efficacy.
At present, there is no vaccine, but institutions at all levels are actively developing vaccine candidates.
Since both SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 enter human cells through the ACE2 receptor, previous SARS-CoV efforts need to be leveraged.
There are three vaccine strategies being studied.
First of all, the researchers plan to develop an all-viral vaccine.
The use of such viruses, whether inactive or dead, is designed to provoke a rapid immune response to COVID-19.
The second strategy is to develop subunit vaccines, aimed at developing a vaccine that energizes the immune system’s sensitive response to certain subunits of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, such studies focused on the S-synthetic protein that aids viruses invading ACE2 enzyme receptors.
The third strategy is to develop a nucleic acid vaccine (DNA vaccine or RNA vaccine, a new vaccine development technique).
It is necessary to test the safety and efficacy of trial vaccines developed in each of these strategies. On March 16, 2020, four Seattle volunteers launched the first clinical trial of the vaccine.
Vaccinations contain harmless genetic sequences copied from disease-causing viruses. It has long been assumed that antibody-dependent enhancement may be a major challenge in the development of the SARS-COV-2 vaccine.
As of April 2020, there were more than 300 clinical trials.
Seven trials were evaluated and approved for the treatment of malaria, including four studies on hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Switched antiviral drug uses account for the vast majority of Chinese studies, with nine phase III clinical trials on radivir to be conducted in multiple countries by the end of April.
As of April 2020, a dynamic review of the clinical development of COVID-19 vaccines and drug candidates has begun. Several existing antiviral drugs to treat COVID-19 are currently being evaluated, such as redisivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/ritonavir, and lopinavir/ritonavir combination interferon beta.
As of March 2020, the efficacy has been preliminarily confirmed.
It has been observed that the clinical symptoms of patients treated with the compassionate drug redicivir have been alleviated.
Phase III clinical trials are currently underway in the U.S., China, and Italy. In February 2020, chloroquine (used in the past to treat malaria) was launched in China with preliminary results.
However, there have been calls for a peer review of the study.
chloroquine is recommended by South Korean and Chinese health authorities.
However, the Wuhan Institute of Virology recommends taking 1 gram a day, while cautioning that twice the dose is very dangerous and potentially fatal.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization for chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, but doctors have the right to decide whether to treat COVID-19 patients with these two drugs. China's 7th edition of the guideline also proposes to treat COVID-19 with interferons, ribavirin, or abidol hydrochloric acid for COVID-19.
Preliminary data suggest that high doses of ribavirin have an inhibitory effect against SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
Further in vi vivo studies have been conducted using nitrozolinite after the low-concentration inhibition of SARS-CoV-2 has been confirmed. Studies have shown that the primordial spike protein induced by transmembrane protease Serine 2 (TMPRSS2) is necessary for SARS-CoV-2 to interact with the ACE2 receptor into the human body.
Whether combined with azithromycin and hydroxychloroquine studies have serious limitations and cannot be accepted by the medical community after further study. oseltamivir has no effect against SARS-CoV-2 in vitro and has not yet been found to have a therapeutic effect on COVID-19.
Cytokine storms can become a complication of late-stage treatment in critically ill patients with COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxychloroquine may be resistant to cytokine storms. Following the completion of a small study, China's National Board of Health has included tocilizumab in treatment guidelines.
Following the positive test results for critically ill patients, Italy is conducting a state-level phase 2 non-randomized trial.
Combine serum ferritin blood tests to identify cytokine storms aimed at curbing the development of the condition, which is thought to lead to the death of some infected people.
In 2017, supported by retrospective case studies, the FDA approved the use of interleukin 6 receptor antagonists for steroid refractic cytokine-releasing syndrome caused by other causes, known as CAR T cell therapy.
So far, there has been no randomized controlled evidence that tocilizumab can effectively treat CRS.
purified concentrated antibodies produced by COVID-19 rehabilitative immune systems are currently being studied, injected into people who need antibodies, as passive immunotherapies prior to the successful development of the vaccine.
This strategy has been tried during the SARS epidemic, but the efficacy is inconclusive.
Virus neutralization is an expected mechanism of action of passive antibody therapy that mediates SARS-CoV-2 immune defense.
However, other mechanisms such as antibody-dependent cytotoxicity and/or phagocytics can achieve the same effect.
Other forms of passive antibody therapies are currently being developed, such as the use of artificial monoclonal antibodies.
Serum production during rehabilitation may increase for rapid deployment. Serum is made up of a liquid part of a recovered patient's blood that contains specific antibodies against the virus.
